WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:05.167
All right, thanks to everyone for

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:06.620
coming today.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:07.944
Once again, conditions are

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.990
such that we are forced to hold our

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.768
annual research symposium online

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:14.630
for a second year.

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:17.769
Sorry about that, but I'm glad that all

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:19.359
of you have joined us today.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:21.856
Before we begin, I think

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:24.219
we'd like to note that

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.789
while we are meeting today, there is

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:29.829
an active conflict underway

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:31.899
where innocent lives are being lost in

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:33.240
Israel and Palestine.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:35.739
Many of us and many of you have

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:37.689
relatives and families impacted by this

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:38.690
conflict.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:40.780
The staff of the archive hopes that

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:43.659
the violence will soon cease and we wish

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.179
peace for both Palestinians and

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:46.340
Israelis.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.546
For those of you who I haven't met

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:50.582
before, my name is Stephen Naron and I

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:51.706
am the director of the Fortunoff Video

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.079
Archive for Holocaust Testimonies.

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.672
I'm grateful to all of you for Zooming

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:58.589
in from other parts of the country in

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.720
the world after a long year of

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.430
Zoom-based events.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.219
This is the Archive's fourth research

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:06.239
symposium.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.249
Our fellowship program is still very

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:09.929
young, but I hope that you will agree,

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.129
after seeing the results of today's

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:12.747
symposium, that it aims to make a

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.489
significant contribution to

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:16.379
international scholarship about

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.128
and based upon video testimonies

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.219
of Holocaust survivors.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.739
We established the Fortunoff Archives

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:23.969
Fellowship program with the launch of

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.391
the Hartman Fellowship in 2018,

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.829
where it was conceived as a fitting

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.059
way to honor one of the archive's

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.069
founders, its faculty advisor for

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:33.735
decades, Professor Geoffrey Hartman,

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:36.126
who passed away on March 14, 2016.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.089
An eminent professor of comparative

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.799
literature at Yale, Geoffrey's vision

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.179
and leadership helped build the

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.709
Fortunoff Archive into the institution

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:44.710
that it is today.

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:47.085
Our fellowship program has grown to

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:49.097
include other fellowships, all named for

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:50.980
the four founders of the archive,

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:52.587
Dori Laub, Geoffrey Hartman, Laurel

00:01:52.588 --> 00:01:54.560
Vlock, and Willie Rosenberg,

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:57.129
four recurring fellowships with

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:58.569
different areas of focus for four

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:00.609
individuals with whom we would

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:01.610
not be here today.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:04.569
Beyond our founders, there are many

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.279
other individuals and organizations to

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.659
whom I would like to offer a few words

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.459
of thanks. This fellowship program would

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:10.839
not be possible without the support of

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.541
our faculty advisor, Timothy Snyder,

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:14.780
Professor Doug Rogers, who's head of

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.156
Russian, East European and Eurasian

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.118
Studies at Yale, and Jennie Shamasna

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.573
at the MacMillan Center, which is the

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:23.543
official institutional home of all of

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.000
our fellows at Yale.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.251
The archive is grateful for all of your

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:29.849
assistance, recruiting, and shepherding

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:31.199
our international fellows through the

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.000
process of becoming part of the

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.120
community.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.329
I would also like to thank our partners

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.189
at the Vienna Wiesenthal Institute, in

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:40.032
particular Eva Kovacs, for

00:02:40.506 --> 00:02:41.759
providing the framework, funding and

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.475
international support for our joint

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:44.590
fellows in Vienna.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.795
Finally, I would like to express my

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.710
gratitude to the Fortunoff, Blaustein,

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.379
Kohl, Hornos, Libby, and Ross families,

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.193
and dozens of other donors, large and

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:55.985
small, that helped make the archive

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:57.550
possible.

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:00.789
The primary goal of the fellowship

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:02.652
program is to encourage scholarship in

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:04.659
the collection and to unlock testimonies

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.490
and make them more accessible and

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.560
as intellectually as well as

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:11.250
accessible to users by,

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.149
by charging our fellows with the task of

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:15.309
producing an annotated critical edition

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:16.779
of a single testimony from the

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:17.780
collection.

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:20.332
I'd like to invite our Hartman Fellow,

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.639
Gil Rubin, to tell us more about the

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:24.590
critical edition series, as well

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:26.799
as well as introduce our speakers today.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:28.170
Gil?

00:03:28.719 --> 00:03:29.913
Thank you, Stephen, so I'm just going to

00:03:29.914 --> 00:03:31.580
speak, just for a few minutes, I'm

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.439
going to introduce our Critical Editions

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:35.848
series that was launched earlier

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:38.419
this year in February.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:39.469
And I'm actually going to share my

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:41.234
screen now and tell you a little bit

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:42.529
about what it is.

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:45.770
So every year,

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:48.259
our postdoctoral fellows,

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:50.479
our visiting scholars, and various

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.669
other people associated with the archive

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:55.009
produce a critical edition.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:58.220
And what the critical edition is-- and

00:03:58.400 --> 00:03:59.869
does everybody see the page currently

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:00.870
or? Yeah, okay.

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:02.689
What a critical decision is, is

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:04.789
basically every visiting scholar

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:06.470
selects one testimony from our

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:08.809
collection and then they produce

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:10.380
two things from it.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:12.800
They produce an introduction, which is

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:14.839
a text that is designed for the public,

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:17.059
for students, or other researchers that

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:18.971
places that testimony in its historical

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:20.989
context and situates within the

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:23.209
contemporary historical scholarship and

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:24.640
literature that is relevant for it.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.199
So we had, we could have about, right

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.999
now we have eight different testimonies

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.070
here, eight different critical editions.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:33.491
And the second thing that it does is it

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.049
actually, the researcher

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:38.279
and scholar, the look at the, view

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.180
the testimony, and they present and

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.850
they produce an annotated transcript.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:45.340
That is once you view the transcript

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:47.239
online, I'm going to show it in a second,

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:49.549
can actually see explanations of various

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:51.720
words, places and so forth, that help

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:53.390
you kind of understand that testimony

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:55.339
while situated in its time and place.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:57.229
So I'm going to look now at just one

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.111
testimony and one of

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:00.739
our critical editions and kind of guide

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:01.740
you through it.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:03.799
So this is, this was produced by Sarah

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:05.029
Garibova, who was

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:07.910
one of the Hartman fellows here.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:09.919
And what you can see here through this

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.439
online viewer is that we have an online

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:15.139
display of her introduction

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:17.029
to the testimony that presents kind of

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:18.799
the general historical background, but

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.319
also situates with the relevant

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:21.510
literature.

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:23.419
You can click on the various footnotes

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:25.160
here and get

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:28.669
either more information or other sources

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:29.670
that are relevant.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.509
And this is how it's been viewed online,

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:34.339
from the landing page to viewing each

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:35.340
and every testimony.

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:37.939
And then, if you have if you want, you

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:39.436
can actually hear their songs in the

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:40.470
testimony.

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:42.298
So they will open up and you can

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:44.340
actually listen to this,

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:46.300
in this case, the edition

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:48.559
she worked on includes a song that was

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:50.360
later reproduced also

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:52.699
in our Songs From Testimonies project.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.039
And additionally, beyond this, you can

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:56.559
actually go online and view the

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:58.092
testimony that is open, open

00:05:58.760 --> 00:05:59.760
to everyone to view.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:01.736
I'll just open here, and

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:03.836
view it together with an

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:05.912
annotated transcript. [CLIP OF TESTIMONY
IN RUSSIAN]

00:06:05.913 --> 00:06:08.180
So I'll just show how it works.

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:12.289
So you can see over here, once there's a

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:14.389
word that

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:16.279
requires any type of explanation.

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:17.910
So you can see here

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:20.586
Dr. Garibova's explanation on this

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:22.579
specific word, on this specific concept

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:24.949
and this specific idea that is presented

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:26.449
in the annotated transcript.

00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:29.029
So the idea here is actually to take our

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:31.730
testimonies that are often accessible,

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:33.538
either in specific language to a

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:35.449
group of researchers or people speak the

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:38.150
language and bring them to researchers

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:40.399
might not work on this specific subset

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.109
of testimonies and languages, bring them

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:43.339
to students, and bring them to the

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:44.340
public.

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:45.790
And so far, within the Critical Edition

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:48.049
series, we have eight different

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:50.149
critical editions and we hope to add

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:51.259
more throughout the year.

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.779
And actually three of the talks

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:55.459
that we are going to hear

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.339
today are, are actually

00:06:58.340 --> 00:06:59.840
going to turn into critical editions.

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:02.149
And I think the speakers will present

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:03.390
more about that.

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:05.829
I will, I will add now to the chat

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:08.149
a link to our critical edition series.

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:10.609
And I'm happy to answer any

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:12.589
happy to answer any more questions about

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:14.120
this. But I just wanted to kind of

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:15.949
present here both so we can celebrate

00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:17.665
and announce the publication of the

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:19.639
series, which was

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:22.219
the result of a lot of effort of work by

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:24.230
our scholars and other people

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:26.839
at the archive, and also

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:28.189
kind of give context into some of the

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:29.869
talks that we'll hear today and see

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:31.639
actually how these critical editions are

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:33.800
created and the type of questions

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:35.509
and issues that the scholars are

00:07:35.510 --> 00:07:37.209
grappling with as they produce it.

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:40.069
So I'm going to put it in the chat

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:41.430
in a minute.

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:43.440
But before we do that, I'm just going to

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:45.069
present our first speaker for today.

