and let you present.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:50.260
And.

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:53.300
Stop sharing.

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:56.089
Have you? Oh, there it goes.

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:07.850
All right, thank you for that

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:10.080
introduction. We're seeing my screen OK?

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:13.364
All right. On the occasion of Samuel

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:16.370
Willenberg's passing in 2016,

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:18.280
media outlets around the world mourned

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:20.629
him as the last Treblinka death

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:21.630
camp survivor.

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:24.270
Many outlets ran stories and obituaries,

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:26.539
prominently featuring that word last.

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:28.669
Two days later, though, the Times of

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:30.379
Israel corrected itself by running

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:32.539
another story entitled, quote, "There

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:34.629
Are Still Treblinka Survivors Among Us.

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:37.190
One Tells His Tale," unquote.

00:09:38.030 --> 00:09:39.598
Having seen earlier reports, the

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:41.384
daughter of Leon Rytz reached out to

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:43.249
the press to tell of her father's

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:44.250
experiences.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:46.490
Rytz still lives in Sweden, where he

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:48.280
settled not long after World War II.

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:51.434
He's well known there, but his story

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:52.759
hadn't come to the attention of the

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:53.929
press outside of Sweden.

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:57.529
After the Times and other outlets wrote

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.119
about Rytz though, a new framing of

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:00.889
survival at Treblinka began to take

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:01.890
hold.

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:03.739
The Times subsequently relabeled

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:05.504
Willenberg the final survivor of the

00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:07.649
August 1943 Treblinka

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:09.529
prisoners revolt, with italic

00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:10.950
emphasis.

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:13.669
Leon Rytz, as she explained to Amanda

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:15.979
Borschel-Dan of the Times, escaped

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:17.842
Treblinka before the now famous August

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:19.970
2, 1943, revolt.

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:22.078
Rytz also survived several other

00:10:22.730 --> 00:10:23.730
camps.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:25.060
These facts led Borschel-Dan

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:27.933
questioning, left her questioning how

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:30.889
we should label his experiences.

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:33.679
She wondered whether it

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:35.680
was factually correct to call Rytz

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:38.149
a Treblinka survivor because he was also

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:39.985
imprisoned at Majdanek, Buchenwald,

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:41.850
and Bergen-Belsen.

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:45.229
Borschel-Dan took her question to Yad

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:47.539
Vashem's Ehud Amir, who

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:49.304
assured her that there's, quote, "no

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:52.009
binding definition of how a Holocaust

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:53.600
survivor is labeled.

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:55.811
A person who is in several camps can be

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:57.919
considered a survivor from each camp in

00:10:57.920 --> 00:10:59.509
which they were a prisoner," unquote.

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:02.059
The passing of Samuel Willenberg and the

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:03.824
late recognition of Leon Rytz opened

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:05.517
new conversations in the media.

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:07.819
Noting as they did the different kinds

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:09.853
of survival stories told by Willenberg

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:12.049
and Rytz, media outlets began

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:13.789
to qualify different versions of

00:11:13.790 --> 00:11:15.649
survival at Treblinka.

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:17.714
Willenberg became the last Treblinka

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:20.149
revolt survivor, while Rytz holds

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:22.609
center stage as the last living survivor

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:24.814
and a pre-revolt

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:26.370
escapee.

00:11:27.050 --> 00:11:28.471
Amir's explanation highlights

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:30.559
the fact that there's no commonly

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:32.929
understood way to label Holocaust

00:11:32.930 --> 00:11:34.460
survivor experiences.

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:36.439
And in addition to this, at present,

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:38.253
there's no historiography of the term

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:39.656
"survivor" as it relates

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:42.859
to a specific place like a camp,

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:44.129
ghetto, or shooting site.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:46.817
Many historians and organizations

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:48.800
offer capacious and respectful

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:51.049
definitions of the wider term "Holocaust

00:11:51.050 --> 00:11:52.814
survivor." But I don't think we have

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:54.799
looked at how the term "survivor" can be

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:56.899
employed locally and what that might do

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:57.900
for research.

00:11:58.490 --> 00:12:00.559
I think that lack of place, specific

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:01.981
understandings of survival is

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.440
particularly noteworthy

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:06.620
with the vibrant expansion of spatial

00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:07.779
Holocaust research.

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:10.323
But as they reacted to the passing of

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:12.319
Willenberg and the late emergence of

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:13.496
Rytz, reporters began an

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:16.519
exploration of these questions and what

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:18.049
their answers might mean for

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:19.640
understandings of the Holocaust.

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:22.700
A revisitation of survival at Treblinka

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:25.159
offers opportunities to analyze

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:27.049
what it means to be a survivor a certain

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:29.239
place during the Holocaust, as

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:31.200
well as a chance to assess how many Jews

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:32.987
made it out of this extermination

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:34.940
camp, and how they did so.

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:37.754
Defining survivorship for individual

00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:40.162
Holocaust locations like the thousands

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:42.169
of camps and ghettos of the Nazi system

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:44.869
is an essential step for historians

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:47.179
seeking to clarify the roles, uses

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:48.950
and evolutions of those sites.

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:51.379
At Treblinka, the varied means of

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:53.095
survival represented by people like

00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:54.947
Willenberg and Rytz also point to

00:12:55.460 --> 00:12:57.109
a need for like, some,

00:12:57.650 --> 00:13:00.229
basic categories of survivor experience.

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:02.597
Research that defined survival of

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:04.609
certain places can, for instance, help

00:13:04.610 --> 00:13:06.227
scholars understand the reach and

00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:09.109
influence of a site beyond its seemingly

00:13:09.110 --> 00:13:11.539
discrete fences, walls, or

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:13.479
borders like that.

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:17.149
My own interest in survival

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:19.219
at Treblinka and finding out how many

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:21.350
people survive this place comes from

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:24.349
my work's need to deal

00:13:24.350 --> 00:13:25.350
with a well accepted

00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:28.860
number of survivors at Treblinka.

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:32.089
Many in this audience and other

00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:33.658
panelists here might, might know

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:36.289
the number 68, that might jump out

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:37.809
at them as the

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:39.849
accepted number of survivors at

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:41.089
Treblinka.

00:13:41.090 --> 00:13:43.789
And that number comes from the 1979

00:13:43.790 --> 00:13:45.490
book The Death Camp Treblinka,

00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:48.409
an edited volume by a man

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:50.419
named Alexander Donat, who was himself

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:52.579
a Holocaust survivor

00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:54.019
and had been in the Warsaw ghetto.

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:56.984
He listed 68 people that he was able

00:13:57.320 --> 00:13:59.809
to find in the process of his research

00:13:59.810 --> 00:14:00.810
in the mid-70s.

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:03.169
And though he said at the time

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:06.019
that that list was anything

00:14:06.020 --> 00:14:08.029
but, I think his

00:14:08.030 --> 00:14:09.451
exact words were are anything

00:14:10.280 --> 00:14:12.130
but exhaustive, it's

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:13.899
pretty much gained a canonical

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:14.900
acceptance.

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:17.486
When I begin talking about my work

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:19.359
at a conference or something like that,

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:21.039
I usually get the question, how do you

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:22.040
do work on Treblinka?

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:23.799
There were only 68 survivors.

00:14:23.830 --> 00:14:25.389
It happens quite a bit.

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:26.979
It's a very well accepted number.

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:29.975
But I have looked at this

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:32.606
matter of how many people survived

00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:35.110
Treblinka and come now to,

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:36.990
after a lot of archival work throughout

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:38.619
my dissertation research,

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:41.109
come to 251

00:14:41.110 --> 00:14:43.599
named or identified survivors

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:44.600
of the camp and come

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:47.889
up with three main ways in which

00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:48.890
they survived Treblinka.

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:51.594
So of those 251

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:53.000
people, 71 are

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:55.883
revolt survivors like Isadore H.

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:57.525
from the Fortunoff Archive.

00:14:58.150 --> 00:14:59.919
And that category

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:02.499
kind of, it seems to jump out

00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:04.659
as the most easy to explain

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:06.460
category of survival experience at

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:07.359
Treblinka.

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:09.730
But it gets it gets somewhat murky

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:12.383
and it is instructive for finding the

00:15:12.460 --> 00:15:14.730
boundaries of the camp.

00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:17.079
So Isadore H.

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:18.850
escaped Treblinka on

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:20.509
August 2, 1943 during

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:22.945
that that prisoner revolt.

00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:25.959
He took part in the revolt, but if

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:27.999
I'm looking at whether or not to put a

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:29.829
person in that category, I don't require

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:31.630
that they took part or even knew that

00:15:32.020 --> 00:15:33.931
resistance was going to take place that

00:15:33.940 --> 00:15:36.039
day. For me, it's enough that

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:38.194
they saw the revolt taking place and

00:15:38.470 --> 00:15:40.381
wisely made for the fences, as everyone

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:41.480
else did.

00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:44.619
Where the category gets instructive

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:46.335
about the limits of Treblinka local

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:49.089
power, is in looking at

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:52.779
the, so about 300 people

00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:54.339
made it out during the revolt, but we've

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:56.320
only identified 71 of

00:15:56.350 --> 00:15:57.350
those individuals.

00:15:57.790 --> 00:15:59.919
Many lost their lives in, in

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:01.900
the chase away from Treblinka,

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:03.080
or were perhaps,

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:06.340
some were caught by locals

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:08.859
and brought back to the camp in

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:10.659
search of, in hope of a reward,

00:16:11.620 --> 00:16:12.639
so on and so forth.

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:13.640
So in looking at how

00:16:14.890 --> 00:16:16.599
far they got away from the camp

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:19.299
after how much time they were brought

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:21.260
back, I believe that in that we can kind

00:16:21.340 --> 00:16:22.900
of see the local power

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:25.239
of Treblinka and its, and its

00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:26.240
perpetrators.

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:28.989
The second group

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:30.880
of individuals that I look at,

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:32.982
a larger group that is that I look at,

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:34.450
is escapees.

00:16:35.650 --> 00:16:37.512
And to disambiguate that term, I mean,

00:16:37.540 --> 00:16:39.820
anyone who escaped Treblinka

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:41.274
on a day other than August

00:16:42.190 --> 00:16:43.190
2, 1943.

00:16:43.510 --> 00:16:45.669
It's, to my knowledge

00:16:45.670 --> 00:16:48.039
it is only these 74

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:49.599
people are the only individuals who

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:50.980
escaped before the revolt.

00:16:51.490 --> 00:16:53.223
So sometimes they escaped in...random

00:16:58.140 --> 00:17:00.299
acts, unplanned acts,

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:02.309
I should say, and sometimes they

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:04.739
escaped in kind of smaller

00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:07.319
scale, networked,

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:10.588
preplanned events where

00:17:10.829 --> 00:17:12.838
they, and I think the largest

00:17:12.839 --> 00:17:14.309
I've seen is about nine others

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:16.979
got out at the same time, by

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:18.779
coming up with and planning a means to

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:19.780
escape the camp.

00:17:20.790 --> 00:17:22.469
And what they do and what looking at

00:17:22.470 --> 00:17:24.179
this category and its,

00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:27.239
and its timeline, especially because

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:28.979
there's a certain date at which about

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:30.480
January of 1943

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:33.193
at which escape from Treblinka really

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:34.660
comes to an end.

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:36.649
This option of resistance,

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:38.249
this option of trying to survive the

00:17:38.250 --> 00:17:40.349
camp kind of stops being

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:41.350
viable.

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:43.936
And that is because there had been

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:46.049
so many escapes, you'll notice that

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:48.359
number is just, it's pretty large,

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:50.009
I believe, for a camp that only operated

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:51.900
for 11 months, and

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:53.916
larger than our previous

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:55.949
knowledge of how many people who

00:17:55.950 --> 00:17:56.950
survived at all.

00:17:57.330 --> 00:17:58.555
And in that, it instructs

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:01.920
as to how big of a problem

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:04.349
escape was for the Nazi

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:05.719
guards who ran Treblinka.

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:08.085
So much so that in January of '43,

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:10.259
they opened a new barracks building that

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:12.459
basically cut off this version of this,

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:13.480
of survival.

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:14.970
But at the same time,

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:17.369
that was something of a putting out a

00:18:17.370 --> 00:18:19.169
bonfire by starting a forest fire,

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:20.983
because once they moved all Treblinka

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:23.130
prisoners to that new barracks,

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:26.099
prisoners testimonies then start

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:27.930
to say, well, now we could plan

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:30.029
a revolt because we could all talk to

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:31.339
each other in the barracks at night.

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:33.690
So that was a pivotal turning point

00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:36.180
in Treblinka history brought about by

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:38.509
this form of resistance by escapes

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:39.700
prior to the revolt.

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:42.809
And finally, there's one last major

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:45.959
category that I want to cover.

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:48.269
Perhaps I'll have to leave some of it

00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:49.979
for Q&amp;A if we want to get there.

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:51.839
But I called this last group

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:53.653
through-transport survivors, and this

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:55.919
sort of changes our understanding

00:18:55.920 --> 00:18:57.809
of how the Nazis used Treblinka

00:18:57.930 --> 00:19:00.060
as a part of the wider Holocaust,

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:02.789
because the certain timing of

00:19:02.790 --> 00:19:04.380
people who were brought to Treblinka,

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:06.329
stepped off the train,

00:19:07.800 --> 00:19:09.417
and were then put back on a train

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:11.547
to somewhere else, tells us about

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:14.670
the camp's role in wider perpetration.

00:19:14.680 --> 00:19:15.680
So, Ben L.

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:18.239
here, who was

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:20.309
incredibly shocking for

00:19:20.310 --> 00:19:21.959
me to find because it turned out that

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:24.059
Ben L., his,

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:26.339
his family is actually third

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:28.079
generation Wisconsin Badgers.

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:29.759
So I ended up finding out that

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:32.309
a current undergrad at

00:19:32.310 --> 00:19:34.529
my own university is a descendant of,

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:36.100
of this man.

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:40.449
And these people came in about

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:42.279
two different ways that came as the

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:44.499
Warsaw Ghetto Uprising was taking place,

00:19:44.500 --> 00:19:46.539
and they also came as

00:19:46.540 --> 00:19:48.630
a resistance was going on in the

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:49.780
Bialystok ghetto.

00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:53.049
And in a longer explanation,

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:55.509
I could get into how that really informs

00:19:55.510 --> 00:19:57.225
us about Treblinka's, how Treblinka

00:19:57.730 --> 00:19:59.980
fit into these other events,

00:20:00.340 --> 00:20:02.319
and how those other events changed what

00:20:02.320 --> 00:20:03.849
went on at Treblinka.

00:20:05.200 --> 00:20:07.359
That being my last major case,

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:08.949
if you've been following along quickly

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:10.269
with your calculator or something,

00:20:10.270 --> 00:20:11.769
you'll notice that the numbers don't

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:13.509
entirely add up, and that's because

00:20:13.510 --> 00:20:15.420
there are some individuals

00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:17.049
their, their trip, their story doesn't

00:20:17.050 --> 00:20:19.329
exactly give us enough information to,

00:20:20.890 --> 00:20:23.079
to say for certain how they survived.

00:20:23.500 --> 00:20:26.079
And there are a few very unique

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:27.080
cases, but I

00:20:28.270 --> 00:20:29.859
couldn't get into those in the amount of

00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:30.860
time here.

00:20:31.060 --> 00:20:32.060
So just

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:34.269
to, to wrap up,

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:37.539
Leon Rytz, the media's, quote,

00:20:37.630 --> 00:20:39.609
"last survivor of Treblinka,"

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:41.764
may well be the last living voice of

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:43.145
survivor survival at this

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:46.059
extermination camp, though

00:20:46.060 --> 00:20:47.609
it's impossible to know for certain.

00:20:47.620 --> 00:20:49.389
I think that's the most important thing

00:20:49.390 --> 00:20:51.790
to point out about any making of a list.

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:53.799
The fact that a Treblinka survivor would

00:20:53.800 --> 00:20:56.049
now be nearing 100 years old

00:20:56.350 --> 00:20:57.720
makes it likely that Mr. Rytz

00:20:58.420 --> 00:21:00.259
is alone in his experiences.

00:21:00.610 --> 00:21:02.374
Similarly, the increasing age of all

00:21:02.650 --> 00:21:04.599
but the youngest child survivors of the

00:21:04.600 --> 00:21:06.119
Holocaust means that these last

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:08.105
experience and last voice

00:21:09.040 --> 00:21:10.608
news stories, I think will sadly

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:13.089
only increase in the coming years.

00:21:13.570 --> 00:21:14.942
As they do, journalists like

00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:17.660
the Times' Borschel-Dan will struggle to

00:21:17.680 --> 00:21:19.959
find prudent and respectful ways

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:22.420
to describe the lives and paths of

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:23.450
the people they cover.

00:21:24.100 --> 00:21:26.109
Historians, I think, would be would do

00:21:26.110 --> 00:21:28.089
well to get ahead of this coming wave of

00:21:28.090 --> 00:21:29.854
media definitions by providing their

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:31.160
own.

00:21:31.180 --> 00:21:33.009
This, however, is not the only reason

00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:34.608
that scholars should address the

00:21:34.690 --> 00:21:37.179
definition of survivorship at the places

00:21:37.180 --> 00:21:38.180
they study.

00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:40.779
Research on Treblinka demonstrates

00:21:41.140 --> 00:21:43.119
that we should be prepared to find

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:44.541
another kind of last survivor

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:46.509
of the camp.

00:21:46.510 --> 00:21:47.510
Many individuals now

00:21:48.670 --> 00:21:50.881
passed away remain unacknowledged

00:21:50.980 --> 00:21:52.464
survivors of this place.

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:55.160
The permanently incomplete nature of any

00:21:55.330 --> 00:21:57.549
survivor list means that even after Mr

00:21:57.550 --> 00:21:59.619
Rytz leaves us, others who

00:21:59.620 --> 00:22:02.319
survived will still await recognition.

00:22:03.730 --> 00:22:04.850
Defining place-specific

00:22:05.890 --> 00:22:08.289
survival can advance our understandings

00:22:08.290 --> 00:22:09.760
of localized perpetration, the

00:22:10.480 --> 00:22:12.187
actions of victims and context,

00:22:12.550 --> 00:22:14.461
and a spatial reach of individual sites

00:22:14.710 --> 00:22:16.000
of Holocaust criminality.

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:18.582
Revolt survivors compel us to consider

00:22:18.670 --> 00:22:20.890
where the bounds of this camp stopped,

00:22:21.310 --> 00:22:23.025
complicating any simplistic view of

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:25.420
the fences as its

00:22:25.660 --> 00:22:27.339
bounds of perpetrator power.

00:22:27.790 --> 00:22:29.799
Escape survivors, meanwhile, provide an

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:31.299
important understanding of how Jewish

00:22:31.300 --> 00:22:33.579
resistance changed the very face

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:35.529
of Treblinka and helped create the

00:22:35.530 --> 00:22:37.930
conditions needed for revolt planning.

00:22:38.770 --> 00:22:40.689
Through-transport survivors take us,

00:22:41.110 --> 00:22:42.819
take our knowledge of resistance other

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:44.199
than revolt still further,

00:22:45.250 --> 00:22:47.499
while also allowing us to see how

00:22:47.500 --> 00:22:50.020
Treblinka fit into the wider Holocaust,

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:52.509
as well as how camp routines changed

00:22:52.510 --> 00:22:53.510
over time.

00:22:54.070 --> 00:22:56.140
I don't propose a single definition

00:22:56.260 --> 00:22:58.180
of place-based survivorship.

00:22:58.600 --> 00:23:00.453
I think that many of the over 44,000

00:23:00.880 --> 00:23:02.439
camps and ghettos of the Third Reich

00:23:02.440 --> 00:23:04.311
will require unique understandings.

00:23:04.870 --> 00:23:06.730
The thousands of mass shooting sites

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:08.933
may demand still more reckonings with

00:23:09.220 --> 00:23:11.020
the idea of survivorship.

00:23:12.100 --> 00:23:14.060
Localized definitions, though, will help

00:23:14.260 --> 00:23:16.239
scholars seeking to understand the

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:18.309
experiences, choices and paths

00:23:18.640 --> 00:23:20.355
of the individuals they studied who

00:23:20.740 --> 00:23:22.920
were forced to confront Nazi genocide.

00:23:23.900 --> 00:23:25.489
Spatial studies of the Holocaust will

00:23:25.490 --> 00:23:27.739
benefit still further from this means

00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:29.179
of determining the boundaries of their

00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:31.700
studies and how they fit into

00:23:31.850 --> 00:23:33.739
overall trends.

00:23:34.550 --> 00:23:36.729
Finally, the active idea

00:23:36.740 --> 00:23:38.455
of defining survival in a capacious

00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:40.699
and respectful manner makes it possible

00:23:40.700 --> 00:23:42.769
to honor the stories of the people

00:23:42.770 --> 00:23:43.770
that we study.

00:23:44.380 --> 00:23:46.129
Increasing the number of survivors of a

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:48.079
place like Treblinka that killed as many

00:23:48.080 --> 00:23:49.080
as 925,000

00:23:50.780 --> 00:23:51.890
from 68 to

00:23:52.790 --> 00:23:54.603
251 in no way alters its overwhelming

00:23:55.130 --> 00:23:56.130
lethality.

00:23:56.450 --> 00:23:58.067
The importance of my work for the

00:23:58.190 --> 00:23:59.569
history of Treblinka is instead the

00:23:59.570 --> 00:24:01.549
creation of an update to Donat's list

00:24:01.880 --> 00:24:03.799
that's more capable of foregrounding

00:24:04.580 --> 00:24:06.559
the differences and individual paths to

00:24:06.560 --> 00:24:08.659
survival and demonstrating to

00:24:08.660 --> 00:24:10.730
interested researchers that additional

00:24:11.030 --> 00:24:13.390
testimonial evidence awaits their use.

00:24:14.810 --> 00:24:16.259
The stories of those left off Donat's

00:24:16.610 --> 00:24:18.709
list deserve recognition and inclusion

00:24:18.710 --> 00:24:20.539
by future scholars, even while we

00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:22.304
continue to acknowledge that no list

00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:24.259
will ever be complete.

00:24:25.160 --> 00:24:26.160
Thank you.

00:24:28.660 --> 00:24:29.660
I'll stop sharing now.

00:24:31.890 --> 00:24:32.890
Thank you, Chad.

00:24:33.149 --> 00:24:34.900
Thank you so much for your talk.

00:24:35.480 --> 00:24:37.640
I'll just remind everyone that you can

00:24:37.760 --> 00:24:39.379
leave questions in the Q&amp;A and that will

00:24:39.380 --> 00:24:40.380
take them after Chad's talk.

00:24:40.940 --> 00:24:42.859
It was a wonderful talk and super

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:45.439
interesting. I think it's going to tie

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:46.429
to some of the other things we're going

00:24:46.430 --> 00:24:47.430
to hear today.

00:24:48.020 --> 00:24:49.619
Is somebody raise his hand?

00:24:50.510 --> 00:24:51.510
Jonathan? Sure.

00:24:52.340 --> 00:24:54.680
I did raise my hand, at least virtually.

00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:57.169
Thank you, Chad, for an excellent talk.

00:24:57.180 --> 00:24:58.180
Very interesting.

00:24:58.310 --> 00:25:00.350
I wanted to ask you about the

00:25:00.830 --> 00:25:02.960
through-transport survivors.

00:25:03.770 --> 00:25:05.659
I confess, I had not heard about people

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:07.571
arriving at Treblinka and then diverted

00:25:07.940 --> 00:25:09.380
and sent elsewhere.

00:25:09.920 --> 00:25:11.359
Could you elaborate and tell us a bit

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:13.369
more about what the purpose was

00:25:13.370 --> 00:25:14.659
and what the experience was like for

00:25:14.660 --> 00:25:16.349
those through-transport survivors?

00:25:16.650 --> 00:25:17.650
Thank you.

00:25:18.650 --> 00:25:20.390
I think, So there's two...

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:23.924
two more prevalent experiences among

00:25:23.990 --> 00:25:24.990
that group.

00:25:25.400 --> 00:25:26.870
Number one is that individuals

00:25:27.680 --> 00:25:29.591
would arrive at Treblinka sometimes and

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:31.569
the train would stop at Treblinka II,

00:25:32.360 --> 00:25:33.559
the extermination camp.

00:25:34.010 --> 00:25:35.382
And then at times, the labor

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:37.639
needs of Treblinka I,

00:25:38.270 --> 00:25:40.369
the nearby labor and

00:25:40.370 --> 00:25:41.780
penal camp, which

00:25:42.620 --> 00:25:44.433
is often said to have been for Polish

00:25:44.450 --> 00:25:45.979
prisoners, but it had a large Jewish

00:25:45.980 --> 00:25:46.980
population as well.

00:25:47.300 --> 00:25:48.829
Sometimes that camp's

00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:51.619
needs, if we want to put it that

00:25:51.620 --> 00:25:52.620
way, would be met

00:25:53.630 --> 00:25:55.394
by taking people off of the train at

00:25:55.730 --> 00:25:57.950
Treblinka II, and putting them back

00:25:57.980 --> 00:26:00.019
on, sorting

00:26:00.020 --> 00:26:01.489
them if you want, and putting them back

00:26:01.490 --> 00:26:02.833
on a train to Treblinka I.

00:26:03.800 --> 00:26:05.329
And we learn about resistance in this

00:26:05.330 --> 00:26:06.653
moment, even if it's a very

00:26:07.400 --> 00:26:09.229
short period of time, because a lot of

00:26:09.230 --> 00:26:11.329
stories are told about how, how people

00:26:11.330 --> 00:26:13.159
tried to look ready for work,

00:26:13.550 --> 00:26:15.619
how they tried to jump into a line

00:26:15.620 --> 00:26:17.384
if they saw that, you know, this was

00:26:17.630 --> 00:26:18.949
the right line to be in.

00:26:19.250 --> 00:26:20.869
So there's a lot of very quick thinking

00:26:20.870 --> 00:26:22.732
resistance that's like short episodes,

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:25.519
but very important and saves many

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:26.520
lives.

00:26:27.230 --> 00:26:29.779
That other group is

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:31.060
usually sent on to Majdanek.

00:26:31.890 --> 00:26:34.429
So, at the time of the Warsaw

00:26:34.430 --> 00:26:36.469
Ghetto Uprising particularly,

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:38.599
I'll just simplify things by staying

00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:40.970
on that one topic with this answer.

00:26:41.750 --> 00:26:43.069
At the time of the Warsaw Ghetto

00:26:43.070 --> 00:26:44.393
Uprising, it seems that the

00:26:45.170 --> 00:26:46.297
Nazis tried to push the

00:26:47.180 --> 00:26:49.009
population of the Warsaw Ghetto out on

00:26:49.010 --> 00:26:51.230
trains as fast as they possibly could,

00:26:51.560 --> 00:26:53.324
and that meant that they didn't sort

00:26:53.330 --> 00:26:54.949
probably in the way that they normally

00:26:54.950 --> 00:26:56.322
did, and people were instead

00:26:57.050 --> 00:26:58.430
sorted at Treblinka.

00:26:58.520 --> 00:27:00.469
And then those thought fit for labor

00:27:00.890 --> 00:27:02.989
were place back on the train and sent

00:27:03.140 --> 00:27:04.140
to Lublin-Majdanek.

00:27:06.420 --> 00:27:08.599
So that, that experience

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:09.979
happens at basically two different

00:27:09.980 --> 00:27:11.793
times. It's kind of the same episode,

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:14.329
the same reasoning going on, but

00:27:14.330 --> 00:27:15.799
it occurs at the time of the Warsaw

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:17.839
Ghetto Uprising and at the time of

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:19.283
resistance in the Bialystok ghettos as

00:27:19.580 --> 00:27:21.139
that is being closed.

00:27:23.840 --> 00:27:24.859
Excellent, excellent.

00:27:25.070 --> 00:27:26.299
Thank you, very, very helpful.

00:27:26.960 --> 00:27:29.449
Out of curiosity, what was the survival

00:27:29.450 --> 00:27:31.219
rate at Majdanek for the people who were

00:27:31.220 --> 00:27:32.220
sent on their,

00:27:33.320 --> 00:27:35.589
probably very, very low as well, I

00:27:35.600 --> 00:27:36.600
would presume.

00:27:37.220 --> 00:27:38.420
It's, yeah.

00:27:38.510 --> 00:27:40.429
So it's it's very, it's low, it's not

00:27:40.430 --> 00:27:42.319
good. However, it's,

00:27:43.490 --> 00:27:45.619
it's way better, you

00:27:45.620 --> 00:27:46.849
know, it's way better than if they had

00:27:46.850 --> 00:27:48.139
stayed at Treblinka where there's

00:27:48.140 --> 00:27:49.939
basically, other than the revolt,

00:27:51.290 --> 00:27:52.613
once you're in the camp, if

00:27:53.330 --> 00:27:54.799
you can't get out in the revolt or you

00:27:54.800 --> 00:27:56.959
can't escape on your own, it's almost

00:27:56.960 --> 00:27:57.960
certain death.

00:27:58.820 --> 00:28:00.190
Being sent on to Majdanek

00:28:01.400 --> 00:28:03.079
after you've already been chosen,

00:28:03.410 --> 00:28:04.864
quote unquote, "fit for work,"

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:08.510
the chances of survival are

00:28:08.990 --> 00:28:11.119
much, much better than had you not

00:28:11.120 --> 00:28:13.069
stayed or then had you stayed at

00:28:13.070 --> 00:28:14.070
Treblinka. So it's, um,

00:28:15.560 --> 00:28:17.809
a lot of those people do lose

00:28:17.810 --> 00:28:19.427
their lives later, either through

00:28:19.910 --> 00:28:20.910
being worked,

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:24.529
worked far too hard at wherever they

00:28:24.530 --> 00:28:26.500
were sent next, but,

00:28:26.630 --> 00:28:27.979
you know, I didn't actually say in the

00:28:27.980 --> 00:28:29.940
talk that I count someone as a Treblinka

00:28:30.170 --> 00:28:31.999
survivor even if they die later in the

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:33.559
Holocaust. And I know of many

00:28:34.010 --> 00:28:35.210
individuals who fit

00:28:36.650 --> 00:28:38.359
that explanation.

00:28:39.500 --> 00:28:41.029
But I think that we need to keep them

00:28:41.030 --> 00:28:42.439
there because, you know, that doesn't

00:28:42.440 --> 00:28:44.559
change the one event in their life to

00:28:44.560 --> 00:28:46.819
have to have perished

00:28:46.820 --> 00:28:47.820
in another incident.

00:28:49.040 --> 00:28:51.200
But, yeah, it's still bad,

00:28:51.470 --> 00:28:52.579
just to sum up. But

00:28:53.810 --> 00:28:55.427
so much better than, than staying

00:28:56.150 --> 00:28:57.150
at Treblinka.

00:29:00.140 --> 00:29:01.180
Thank you both.

00:29:01.300 --> 00:29:02.599
With the rest of the questions, it seems

00:29:02.600 --> 00:29:04.480
there is another question from Professor

00:29:04.760 --> 00:29:06.559
Avinoam Patt, but we'll take them, I

00:29:06.560 --> 00:29:08.422
think, after Julie's presentation just

00:29:08.630 --> 00:29:10.300
we kind of take the questions then.

00:29:10.340 --> 00:29:11.750
That's fine, I mean, I could have,

00:29:12.550 --> 00:29:14.360
but so I'll just

00:29:14.660 --> 00:29:16.069
present Julie briefly.

00:29:16.070 --> 00:29:17.980
And then after Julie's presentation,

00:29:19.190 --> 00:29:21.049
we're going to have a Q&amp;A session

00:29:21.350 --> 00:29:22.880
with both Julie and Chad.

00:29:23.630 --> 00:29:25.051
Julie Dawson is the Fortunoff

00:29:26.030 --> 00:29:27.310
fellow at the Vienna Wiesenthal

00:29:27.770 --> 00:29:29.436
Institute and a doctoral candidate

00:29:30.170 --> 00:29:31.759
at the University of Vienna's Institute

00:29:31.760 --> 00:29:33.009
for Contemporary History.

00:29:33.410 --> 00:29:35.359
Her dissertation examines postwar Jewish

00:29:35.360 --> 00:29:37.009
life in Romania through the lens of

00:29:37.010 --> 00:29:38.990
recently found diaries of a

00:29:39.050 --> 00:29:40.400
Transnistrian survivors.

00:29:41.030 --> 00:29:42.739
She worked for the Leo Baeck Institute

00:29:42.740 --> 00:29:44.306
from 2010 to 2019

00:29:44.840 --> 00:29:46.489
and directed their archival survey of

00:29:46.490 --> 00:29:48.205
Transylvania and Bukovina from 2012

00:29:49.040 --> 00:29:50.209
to 2019.

00:29:50.900 --> 00:29:52.223
And she was a researcher in

00:29:53.270 --> 00:29:54.581
Medias, Romania for the

00:29:55.370 --> 00:29:57.439
EU Horizon 2020 project

00:29:57.590 --> 00:29:59.599
called TRACES: Transmitting Contentious

00:29:59.600 --> 00:30:01.670
Cultural Heritage with the Arts.

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:03.710
And she's published several articles

00:30:03.980 --> 00:30:05.562
which you can see on her

00:30:06.080 --> 00:30:08.029
website,  under her, on her bio on her

00:30:08.030 --> 00:30:09.769
website and her research more generally

00:30:09.770 --> 00:30:11.740
includes Jewish Bukovina, Communist

00:30:12.080 --> 00:30:14.209
Romania, women's history, trauma,

00:30:14.210 --> 00:30:15.349
and memory studies.

00:30:16.100 --> 00:30:18.200
And I'll let you start presenting Julie.

00:30:18.620 --> 00:30:20.119
And please write any other questions you

00:30:20.120 --> 00:30:21.499
have for either Julia and Chad in the

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:22.369
Q&amp;A session.

00:30:22.370 --> 00:30:24.140
And we will discuss them briefly.

00:30:26.520 --> 00:30:27.749
OK, thank you.

00:30:28.680 --> 00:30:30.119
I'm going to share my screen.

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:40.210
All right.

00:30:41.490 --> 00:30:43.230
All right, thank you very much,

00:30:43.510 --> 00:30:45.127
Gil and Stephen and the Fortunoff

00:30:45.180 --> 00:30:47.309
Archive for the invitation and

00:30:47.310 --> 00:30:48.310
support.

00:30:48.840 --> 00:30:50.400
My project is

00:30:50.910 --> 00:30:52.109
somewhat different from the other

00:30:52.110 --> 00:30:53.825
presentations as my focus is not on

00:30:54.270 --> 00:30:56.609
oral histories per se, but rather

00:30:56.610 --> 00:30:58.679
on this newly discovered written

00:30:58.680 --> 00:31:00.542
testimony that Gil mentioned, a set of

00:31:00.630 --> 00:31:02.609
diaries written in the immediate

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:04.530
postwar period in Romania.

00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:07.844
Due to the nature of the diaries,

00:31:07.980 --> 00:31:09.793
however, consulting oral histories is

00:31:09.990 --> 00:31:12.329
crucial for contextualizing my subject's

00:31:12.330 --> 00:31:13.590
Holocaust experience.

00:31:13.950 --> 00:31:15.749
And then I use oral histories to

00:31:15.750 --> 00:31:17.416
complement and expand my subject's

00:31:17.910 --> 00:31:18.910
writings.

00:31:19.020 --> 00:31:20.789
I'm also only a little halfway through

00:31:20.790 --> 00:31:21.669
my fellowship.

00:31:21.670 --> 00:31:23.789
I took maternity leave partway through,

00:31:24.150 --> 00:31:26.010
so my research is still ongoing,

00:31:26.570 --> 00:31:28.619
I view new material every

00:31:28.980 --> 00:31:31.409
week, every day and gain new insights

00:31:31.410 --> 00:31:32.410
all the time.

00:31:32.610 --> 00:31:33.959
In this short talk, I'm going to

00:31:33.960 --> 00:31:35.759
introduce my overall project with the

00:31:35.760 --> 00:31:37.890
diaries as the core analytic lens.

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:40.019
I'll give some biographical background

00:31:40.020 --> 00:31:41.882
on the author, of just a tiny bit into

00:31:42.210 --> 00:31:44.369
trauma theory, and discuss my

00:31:44.370 --> 00:31:46.829
continuing examination of the associated

00:31:46.830 --> 00:31:47.830
oral histories.

00:31:48.750 --> 00:31:50.584
So, the diary.

00:31:50.790 --> 00:31:52.859
One spring day in 2009, a

00:31:52.860 --> 00:31:54.809
peach colored notebook was discovered

00:31:55.200 --> 00:31:56.849
by volunteers cleaning up a

00:31:56.850 --> 00:31:58.467
document-littered woman's balcony

00:31:59.160 --> 00:32:00.929
of a shuttered synagogue from a small

00:32:00.930 --> 00:32:02.069
Transylvanian town.

00:32:03.040 --> 00:32:04.979
The notebook, it quickly became clear,

00:32:04.990 --> 00:32:07.259
was a diary, filled from cover to cover

00:32:07.260 --> 00:32:09.180
with carefully pencilled German, the

00:32:09.220 --> 00:32:11.170
words very clear to read

00:32:11.590 --> 00:32:12.700
and often distressed.

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:15.339
Eventually four notebooks and several

00:32:15.340 --> 00:32:16.380
related papers were found.

00:32:17.740 --> 00:32:19.602
Containing over 800 entries, the diary

00:32:20.050 --> 00:32:21.760
stretch from 1948 to

00:32:22.570 --> 00:32:24.579
1961 and record the postwar

00:32:24.580 --> 00:32:26.580
life of brief and limited triumph

00:32:26.590 --> 00:32:28.550
of a young survivor of the Transnistrian

00:32:28.660 --> 00:32:30.130
Holocaust, Blanka Lebzelter.

00:32:32.390 --> 00:32:34.359
These diaries are a tremendous tool for

00:32:34.360 --> 00:32:35.289
researchers.

00:32:35.290 --> 00:32:37.329
They provide multifaceted entry points

00:32:37.330 --> 00:32:39.450
for examining daily life of Jewish

00:32:39.460 --> 00:32:41.322
survivors in the initial decades after

00:32:41.440 --> 00:32:43.057
the war, as well as for analyzing

00:32:43.690 --> 00:32:45.552
the repercussions of violent trauma to

00:32:45.730 --> 00:32:46.730
everyday life.

00:32:47.290 --> 00:32:49.239
They indicate spaces in which

00:32:49.240 --> 00:32:51.369
individuals were able to perform acts of

00:32:51.370 --> 00:32:53.859
agency despite totalitarian strictures

00:32:53.860 --> 00:32:55.479
and also those places where the cold

00:32:55.480 --> 00:32:57.342
machinations of the totalitarian state

00:32:57.640 --> 00:32:59.799
devastated individual lives.

00:33:00.510 --> 00:33:02.372
They, moreover, shed light on cultural

00:33:02.440 --> 00:33:04.599
and social practices, and Jewish life

00:33:04.600 --> 00:33:06.429
at the time with especially fascinating

00:33:06.430 --> 00:33:07.949
insights into the operations of

00:33:08.440 --> 00:33:10.204
Romanian Zionist youth organizations

00:33:10.630 --> 00:33:12.100
in the late 1940s, about which

00:33:12.790 --> 00:33:14.265
little to nothing has been written to

00:33:14.453 --> 00:33:15.453
date.

00:33:16.130 --> 00:33:17.929
Furthermore as an inherently gendered

00:33:17.930 --> 00:33:20.029
source, the writings testify to the life

00:33:20.030 --> 00:33:21.500
of a young single woman raised

00:33:22.340 --> 00:33:23.929
at the interface of tradition and

00:33:23.930 --> 00:33:26.089
modernity, struggling for a voice

00:33:26.090 --> 00:33:28.070
in the profoundly patriarchal nation.

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:30.869
Finally, the author's narration of

00:33:30.870 --> 00:33:32.519
global events from the founding of the

00:33:32.520 --> 00:33:34.649
state of Israel to the 1956

00:33:34.650 --> 00:33:35.973
Hungarian uprising, as well

00:33:36.720 --> 00:33:38.582
as political and cultural responses to

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:41.279
the Holocaust, such as the establishment

00:33:41.280 --> 00:33:42.869
of German reparations and the

00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:44.939
publication of Anna Frank's diaries,

00:33:45.360 --> 00:33:46.977
indicate an interconnectedness to

00:33:47.430 --> 00:33:49.279
the world that belies and soon the

00:33:49.310 --> 00:33:51.420
impermeability of the Iron Curtain.

00:33:52.380 --> 00:33:54.140
So, who was Blanka Lebzelter?

00:33:55.110 --> 00:33:56.874
Blanka Lebzelter was born near or in

00:33:56.940 --> 00:33:58.655
Czernowitz in the early, mid, early

00:33:58.980 --> 00:34:00.240
to mid 1920s.

00:34:01.210 --> 00:34:03.489
Her father, Josef Lebzelter, was a civil

00:34:03.490 --> 00:34:04.573
servant during the Austro-Hungarian

00:34:05.290 --> 00:34:07.320
period, fill, filling

00:34:07.830 --> 00:34:10.039
official clerical duties in several

00:34:10.050 --> 00:34:11.949
Bukovinian villages and towns.

00:34:12.120 --> 00:34:14.010
Her mother, Anna, grew up in Czernowitz

00:34:14.400 --> 00:34:16.349
and her family overlapped with the

00:34:16.350 --> 00:34:18.448
social circles of Paul Anschel, later,

00:34:18.449 --> 00:34:19.449
the poet line Celan.

00:34:20.219 --> 00:34:21.787
Lebzelter's first cousin, Gustav

00:34:22.380 --> 00:34:24.689
was, Gustav Chomed, was close

00:34:24.690 --> 00:34:26.669
boyhood friends with Anschel and in

00:34:26.670 --> 00:34:28.619
postwar correspondence, the two wrote

00:34:28.620 --> 00:34:30.316
nostalgically of Gustav's

00:34:30.690 --> 00:34:32.999
home and the Topfergasse, this

00:34:33.090 --> 00:34:34.310
being also the home of Lebzelter's

00:34:34.889 --> 00:34:35.918
grandparents and aunt

00:34:36.960 --> 00:34:37.960
and uncle.

00:34:38.100 --> 00:34:39.899
The Lebzelter family were entrenched

00:34:39.900 --> 00:34:41.762
German speaking Central European Jews.

00:34:42.330 --> 00:34:44.290
Her older brother had recently graduated

00:34:44.460 --> 00:34:45.899
from the University of Prague in

00:34:45.960 --> 00:34:48.329
Architecture; her fiance had studied

00:34:48.330 --> 00:34:49.330
medicine there.

00:34:49.770 --> 00:34:51.719
Her father, most recently secretary of

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:53.968
the Waschkoutz town hall was in his 60s

00:34:53.969 --> 00:34:56.129
at the start of the war, living in quiet

00:34:56.130 --> 00:34:58.199
retirement, in a home with

00:34:58.200 --> 00:35:00.239
a garden full of fruit trees and a

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:02.580
small riverside town short train ride

00:35:02.910 --> 00:35:03.910
from Czernowitz.

00:35:04.620 --> 00:35:06.041
The Lebzelters were generally

00:35:06.210 --> 00:35:08.340
assimilated and marginally religious,

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:10.249
probably in the inferred manner

00:35:10.260 --> 00:35:11.940
explained in one testimony

00:35:12.330 --> 00:35:14.010
from Pearl Taub of Czernowitz,

00:35:15.060 --> 00:35:16.529
who, when asked about her family's

00:35:16.530 --> 00:35:18.360
religious observance and education

00:35:18.780 --> 00:35:20.544
demures, but when asked specifically

00:35:20.550 --> 00:35:22.510
whether her mother lit candles on Friday

00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:24.599
evenings, answered, "Oh, definitely,

00:35:24.740 --> 00:35:25.740
yes. Yes." When

00:35:26.880 --> 00:35:28.439
the war broke out, Lebzelter was

00:35:28.440 --> 00:35:29.790
probably 19 or 20.

00:35:29.970 --> 00:35:31.930
She was well educated, speaking, besides

00:35:32.100 --> 00:35:33.869
her German mother tongue, Romanian,

00:35:33.870 --> 00:35:35.909
Russian, English, most likely

00:35:35.920 --> 00:35:37.559
she understood Yiddish and Ukrainian.

00:35:39.370 --> 00:35:41.289
She had a deep appreciation for theater,

00:35:41.320 --> 00:35:43.209
literature, and the arts, distinct

00:35:43.210 --> 00:35:45.070
distaste for manual labor,

00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:47.520
and was deeply ashamed of depending

00:35:47.530 --> 00:35:48.530
on charity.

00:35:48.940 --> 00:35:50.588
In the summer of 1941, after the

00:35:50.780 --> 00:35:52.740
Barbarossa operation began, the Romanian

00:35:53.050 --> 00:35:54.652
army invaded northern Bukovina,

00:35:55.450 --> 00:35:57.129
which had been occupied by the Soviet

00:35:57.130 --> 00:35:58.379
Union the previous year.

00:35:59.450 --> 00:36:01.429
During the first few weeks of July,

00:36:02.060 --> 00:36:03.649
1941, pogroms were carried out in

00:36:03.650 --> 00:36:05.189
countless villages in this region.

00:36:05.710 --> 00:36:07.670
Perpetrators included Romanian soldiers,

00:36:08.270 --> 00:36:10.249
Ukrainian and Romanian peasants, as well

00:36:10.250 --> 00:36:11.383
as a German Einsatz troop.

00:36:12.230 --> 00:36:13.536
And in Lebzelter's home of Waschkoutz,

00:36:13.910 --> 00:36:15.469
the Jews pulled from their houses and

00:36:15.470 --> 00:36:16.879
gathered at the gendarmerie.

00:36:18.550 --> 00:36:20.349
A group of prominent men were selected

00:36:20.350 --> 00:36:22.260
for execution, marched to a lower

00:36:22.280 --> 00:36:23.603
hillside outside the town and shot.

00:36:24.430 --> 00:36:26.096
Among these Lebzelters, father and

00:36:26.320 --> 00:36:29.010
brother, Josef and Bruno, her fiancee,

00:36:29.050 --> 00:36:30.909
she learns, was similarly murdered in a

00:36:30.910 --> 00:36:31.910
different location.

00:36:32.560 --> 00:36:34.300
Lebzelter and her mother, Anna,

00:36:34.840 --> 00:36:36.699
were later deported to Transnistria,

00:36:37.060 --> 00:36:38.289
where they spent three years in the

00:36:38.290 --> 00:36:39.290
Moghilev ghetto.

00:36:40.730 --> 00:36:42.543
Upon their release in 1945, the women

00:36:42.920 --> 00:36:44.539
returned initially to Czernowitz.

00:36:44.540 --> 00:36:46.000
Regarding this homecoming, Lebzelter

00:36:46.250 --> 00:36:47.818
writes, quote, "The hour arrived

00:36:48.260 --> 00:36:49.849
where we saw appear in the distance the

00:36:49.850 --> 00:36:52.159
towers of the Czernowitz archbishop's

00:36:52.160 --> 00:36:53.149
residence.

00:36:53.150 --> 00:36:54.620
I gazed, spellbound upon these

00:36:55.160 --> 00:36:57.071
towers, and listened inside for an echo

00:36:57.320 --> 00:36:58.320
of joy.

00:36:58.430 --> 00:36:59.809
But there was nothing of the kind.

00:37:00.290 --> 00:37:02.389
I sensed only my raw, sore heart and

00:37:02.420 --> 00:37:03.541
worries about the future."

00:37:05.090 --> 00:37:06.739
She goes on to explain the motives for

00:37:06.740 --> 00:37:08.110
their subsequent departure.

00:37:08.210 --> 00:37:10.130
Quote, "we felt poorly in the old home

00:37:10.140 --> 00:37:12.139
upon the ruins of our lives, like day

00:37:12.140 --> 00:37:14.209
and night, by those hard memories

00:37:14.210 --> 00:37:16.121
and among those who are not innocent in

00:37:16.130 --> 00:37:17.130
our tragedy.".

00:37:19.100 --> 00:37:20.779
The two women left, making their way

00:37:20.780 --> 00:37:22.909
eventually to the Black Sea port town of

00:37:22.910 --> 00:37:23.910
Constanta.

00:37:24.500 --> 00:37:26.689
Here, the diaries begin in January

00:37:26.690 --> 00:37:28.069
of 1948 and until

00:37:28.880 --> 00:37:30.769
1961. When she finally received her

00:37:30.770 --> 00:37:32.410
longed for exit visa, Lebzelter

00:37:33.140 --> 00:37:35.060
kept a record of her daily life and

00:37:35.240 --> 00:37:36.919
struggle with loneliness, her mother's

00:37:36.920 --> 00:37:38.635
eventual death from tuberculosis in

00:37:38.810 --> 00:37:40.879
1952, and always

00:37:40.880 --> 00:37:41.880
of penetrating grief.

00:37:43.330 --> 00:37:45.429
When she left in 1961, she

00:37:45.430 --> 00:37:46.929
passed the diaries to her cousin,

00:37:46.930 --> 00:37:48.939
Babette Chomed, Gustav's sister,

00:37:48.940 --> 00:37:50.949
who had settled after the war in

00:37:50.950 --> 00:37:52.577
Medias, a town in

00:37:53.162 --> 00:37:54.162
Transylvania.

00:37:54.530 --> 00:37:56.709
Her last recorded words are, quote,

00:37:56.720 --> 00:37:57.889
"I'm very exhausted.

00:37:58.380 --> 00:38:00.169
The moment approaches to leave the old

00:38:00.170 --> 00:38:01.340
home and seek a new one.

00:38:01.550 --> 00:38:02.659
What will I find?

00:38:03.050 --> 00:38:04.548
One is not allowed to bring diaries.

00:38:04.670 --> 00:38:06.319
I could not bring myself to destroy

00:38:06.320 --> 00:38:08.599
them. I'm entrusting them to someone

00:38:08.600 --> 00:38:09.600
for safekeeping.

00:38:10.040 --> 00:38:11.749
Will they ever find their way back to

00:38:11.750 --> 00:38:12.750
me?" End quote.

00:38:13.700 --> 00:38:15.619
These diaries were found in 2009

00:38:15.920 --> 00:38:17.719
by volunteers cleaning up the woman's

00:38:17.720 --> 00:38:18.979
balcony of the synagogue in the

00:38:18.980 --> 00:38:20.696
Transylvanian town, where Lebzelter's

00:38:21.170 --> 00:38:23.239
cousin had remained almost until her

00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:24.860
death in the late 1980s.

00:38:27.040 --> 00:38:29.199
My project examines these diaries

00:38:29.200 --> 00:38:31.239
from multiple perspectives and singular

00:38:31.240 --> 00:38:33.039
testimony representing survivor

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:34.689
narratives that have been little probed

00:38:34.690 --> 00:38:36.639
and as a source allowing unexplored

00:38:36.640 --> 00:38:38.649
insights into social and

00:38:38.650 --> 00:38:40.599
cultural history considerations of

00:38:40.600 --> 00:38:41.699
postwar Romania.

00:38:42.190 --> 00:38:44.022
My overarching research question center

00:38:44.140 --> 00:38:46.021
around trauma, oppression, and agency.

00:38:46.450 --> 00:38:47.605
My current focus at the Vienna

00:38:47.606 --> 00:38:49.419
Wiesenthal Institute and working with

00:38:49.570 --> 00:38:51.481
the Fortunoff Archive is reconstructing

00:38:52.420 --> 00:38:53.890
Lebzelter's wartime experience

00:38:54.610 --> 00:38:56.679
in context while also

00:38:56.680 --> 00:38:58.297
deconstructing the manifestations

00:38:59.050 --> 00:39:00.760
of trauma and her postwar

00:39:01.060 --> 00:39:02.060
writings.

00:39:02.800 --> 00:39:04.539
In her seminal work on trauma and

00:39:04.540 --> 00:39:06.579
narrative, comparative literature

00:39:06.580 --> 00:39:08.050
scholar Cathy Caruth describes

00:39:08.620 --> 00:39:10.089
the double telling of the center of

00:39:10.090 --> 00:39:11.090
trauma narratives.

00:39:11.530 --> 00:39:13.392
She writes of the, quote, "oscillation

00:39:13.450 --> 00:39:15.159
between a crisis of death and the

00:39:15.160 --> 00:39:16.750
correlative crisis of life,

00:39:17.320 --> 00:39:19.035
between the story of the unbearable

00:39:19.090 --> 00:39:20.805
nature of an event and the story of

00:39:21.190 --> 00:39:23.260
the unbearable nature of its survival,"

00:39:23.860 --> 00:39:24.860
unquote.

00:39:25.390 --> 00:39:27.489
The diaries witnessed this crisis of

00:39:27.490 --> 00:39:28.830
life, but understanding Lebzelter's

00:39:29.440 --> 00:39:31.449
crisis of death is essential to

00:39:31.450 --> 00:39:32.690
presenting her story.

00:39:33.160 --> 00:39:34.749
My work with the oral histories of the

00:39:34.750 --> 00:39:36.220
Fortunoff Archive aims to fill

00:39:36.760 --> 00:39:38.800
this lacuna in Lebzelter's biography,

00:39:39.430 --> 00:39:41.829
even though such a contextualization

00:39:41.830 --> 00:39:44.199
will and must be, in its very nature,

00:39:44.200 --> 00:39:46.079
imperfect and incomplete.

00:39:48.420 --> 00:39:50.489
So how am I incorporating these

00:39:50.490 --> 00:39:52.519
oral histories into Lebzelter's story?

00:39:54.530 --> 00:39:56.672
She herself mentions Transnistria

00:39:56.780 --> 00:39:58.677
in the diaries only a handful of times,

00:39:59.060 --> 00:40:00.949
generally when some aspect of her life

00:40:00.950 --> 00:40:03.550
recalls to her mind, her experience,

00:40:03.560 --> 00:40:05.119
for example, when she and her mother are

00:40:05.120 --> 00:40:07.280
suffering from severe hunger or

00:40:07.310 --> 00:40:08.940
without heat, when her hachshara

00:40:09.430 --> 00:40:11.360
group is thrown out of its lodgings

00:40:11.510 --> 00:40:13.309
and made to stand outside in the snowy

00:40:13.310 --> 00:40:14.310
night, when state

00:40:15.380 --> 00:40:17.869
bureaucracy intrudes with irrational

00:40:17.870 --> 00:40:18.870
demands.

00:40:19.510 --> 00:40:20.880
Fine details about the pogrom

00:40:21.700 --> 00:40:23.049
during which her father and brother were

00:40:23.050 --> 00:40:25.420
executed are recalled in three letters

00:40:25.450 --> 00:40:26.519
she wrote to the dead.

00:40:27.220 --> 00:40:28.780
But regarding the years in the

00:40:29.240 --> 00:40:30.999
Moghilev ghetto, she writes only a

00:40:31.000 --> 00:40:33.329
handful of nondescript sentences.

00:40:34.180 --> 00:40:36.099
Their survival, she explains in

00:40:36.520 --> 00:40:38.770
disbelief, was, quote, "a miracle.

00:40:38.980 --> 00:40:41.269
I cannot explain it in any other way.".

00:40:43.900 --> 00:40:45.639
For my research, I'm looking at all the

00:40:45.640 --> 00:40:47.110
interviews of individuals with

00:40:47.320 --> 00:40:48.937
overlapping biographical details,

00:40:49.330 --> 00:40:50.920
especially those who spent time in

00:40:51.910 --> 00:40:53.289
Moghilev. Remarkably little has been

00:40:53.290 --> 00:40:54.369
written about the site.

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:56.139
And so I'm looking at anyone who was

00:40:56.140 --> 00:40:57.250
there or passed through.

00:40:57.790 --> 00:40:59.409
A number of individuals who actually

00:40:59.410 --> 00:41:01.050
remained in Moghilev is small.

00:41:01.060 --> 00:41:02.628
It was a transit point, and many

00:41:03.190 --> 00:41:05.199
arrived only to leave within a

00:41:05.200 --> 00:41:06.200
few days.

00:41:07.170 --> 00:41:09.130
Some who escaped from camps further east

00:41:09.270 --> 00:41:11.219
made their way back to Moghilev or

00:41:11.230 --> 00:41:13.019
generally tried to hide outside of the

00:41:13.020 --> 00:41:14.020
ghetto.

00:41:14.100 --> 00:41:15.689
The draw may have been due to the size

00:41:15.690 --> 00:41:17.849
of the town and the resulting anonymity

00:41:17.850 --> 00:41:18.719
possible.

00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:20.639
Food may have been easier to come by.

00:41:21.060 --> 00:41:23.219
Work, which resulted in food may

00:41:23.220 --> 00:41:24.929
also have been easier to obtain.

00:41:25.930 --> 00:41:27.699
Ideally, of course, I would find an oral

00:41:27.700 --> 00:41:29.589
history from a woman of similar age and

00:41:29.590 --> 00:41:31.749
background to Lebzelter who also spent

00:41:31.750 --> 00:41:33.012
years in the Moghilev ghetto.

00:41:33.460 --> 00:41:35.049
Thus far I have not found such an

00:41:35.050 --> 00:41:36.050
interview.

00:41:36.100 --> 00:41:37.570
The women, in fact, tend to be

00:41:37.930 --> 00:41:39.789
significantly younger than Blanka

00:41:39.940 --> 00:41:41.679
as they were children or adolescents

00:41:41.680 --> 00:41:42.680
during the war.

00:41:43.330 --> 00:41:44.506
These people experience,

00:41:45.340 --> 00:41:46.989
in addition to the specific acts of

00:41:46.990 --> 00:41:48.558
violence, the particular anguish

00:41:49.300 --> 00:41:51.039
of losing their innocence, learning that

00:41:51.040 --> 00:41:52.839
their parents could not protect them or

00:41:52.840 --> 00:41:53.840
their siblings.

00:41:54.580 --> 00:41:56.259
The other women tend to be older than

00:41:56.260 --> 00:41:58.239
Blanka and already with children of

00:41:58.240 --> 00:41:59.612
their own. Survival, or not,

00:42:00.700 --> 00:42:02.769
of their children generally forms the

00:42:02.770 --> 00:42:04.510
core of these women stories.

00:42:05.710 --> 00:42:07.180
The men who were in the ghetto

00:42:07.810 --> 00:42:08.810
speak of surviving with work.

00:42:09.820 --> 00:42:11.589
It is not clear if women were able to

00:42:11.590 --> 00:42:13.228
work in the same manner as the men.

00:42:13.750 --> 00:42:15.269
If not, than how their survival

00:42:16.090 --> 00:42:17.090
was achieved.

00:42:17.800 --> 00:42:19.569
As I mentioned, I'm only just halfway

00:42:19.570 --> 00:42:21.219
through my fellowship and I still have

00:42:21.220 --> 00:42:22.630
many interviews to listen to,

00:42:23.530 --> 00:42:25.110
I look forwatd to my ongoing work and

00:42:25.170 --> 00:42:26.999
especially to writing about my results.

00:42:28.530 --> 00:42:30.196
To conclude, I would like to quote

00:42:30.270 --> 00:42:31.949
scholars Marianne Hirsch and

00:42:32.490 --> 00:42:33.510
Leo Spitzer.

00:42:34.120 --> 00:42:36.219
In a reflection on creating channels

00:42:36.430 --> 00:42:38.199
silenced or neglected voices of the

00:42:38.200 --> 00:42:40.062
Transnistrian Holocaust they remind us

00:42:40.210 --> 00:42:41.925
that, quote, "Each individual story

00:42:42.670 --> 00:42:44.439
helps to shape a larger history,

00:42:44.800 --> 00:42:46.711
by providing it with detailed depth and

00:42:46.780 --> 00:42:49.059
nuance. And in turn, each story

00:42:49.060 --> 00:42:51.189
is enhanced and given broader meaning

00:42:51.190 --> 00:42:52.954
through its contextualization within

00:42:53.290 --> 00:42:54.879
a larger historical matrix.

00:42:55.570 --> 00:42:57.699
Post generations haunted by stories

00:42:57.700 --> 00:42:59.611
that have not been worked through still

00:42:59.860 --> 00:43:01.771
find that they owe the victims this act

00:43:01.780 --> 00:43:03.669
of attentive listening, as well as this

00:43:03.670 --> 00:43:06.099
act of historical work, of historical

00:43:06.100 --> 00:43:07.500
repair," unquote.

00:43:08.590 --> 00:43:10.160
So on that note, I want to thank the

00:43:10.170 --> 00:43:11.738
Fortunoff Archive and the Vienna

00:43:11.760 --> 00:43:13.622
Wiesenthal Institute for enabling this

00:43:13.650 --> 00:43:16.019
act of attentive listening and returning

00:43:16.020 --> 00:43:17.969
a voice to the subject was unable

00:43:18.480 --> 00:43:19.494
to publicly witness during her life.

00:43:19.495 --> 00:43:20.495
Thank you

00:43:24.080 --> 00:43:25.080
Thank you, Julie,

00:43:26.150 --> 00:43:27.380
for this wonderful talk.

00:43:27.410 --> 00:43:29.430
I think we'll open this up

00:43:29.450 --> 00:43:31.070
for questions.

00:43:31.340 --> 00:43:33.679
Feel free to write questions

00:43:34.700 --> 00:43:36.317
for Julie and Chad now in the Q&amp;A

00:43:37.070 --> 00:43:38.269
box and we'll read them out.

00:43:38.300 --> 00:43:40.339
I think we have a few questions for Chad

00:43:40.340 --> 00:43:42.287
that people wrote during your

00:43:42.536 --> 00:43:44.000
presentation. So I'm going to read them

00:43:44.330 --> 00:43:46.009
out and just read out, start with the

00:43:46.010 --> 00:43:47.823
first two questions, although I think

00:43:47.900 --> 00:43:49.039
the second and the third one

00:43:50.060 --> 00:43:51.060
tie together some, so I'm just going to

00:43:51.250 --> 00:43:52.700
give out the questions for Chad and give

00:43:52.760 --> 00:43:53.969
him the opportunity to answer them and

00:43:54.230 --> 00:43:55.099
Julie, if you have something to add to

00:43:55.100 --> 00:43:57.169
that, too, please feel free to

00:43:57.170 --> 00:43:58.170
jump in.

00:43:58.730 --> 00:44:00.769
So, Chad, if you also want to

00:44:01.070 --> 00:44:03.100
turn on your video and audio so

00:44:03.110 --> 00:44:04.909
you can add to that.

00:44:05.210 --> 00:44:07.219
So Avinoam Patt, Avinoam Patt asks,

00:44:07.220 --> 00:44:08.509
"what does your research suggests about

00:44:08.510 --> 00:44:10.249
knowledge of the final solution among

00:44:10.250 --> 00:44:12.019
Jews in Poland during the war for your

00:44:12.020 --> 00:44:13.456
subjects, particularly escapees?

00:44:14.090 --> 00:44:15.756
Escapees talk about encountering a

00:44:15.920 --> 00:44:17.709
refusal to believe the reports about

00:44:17.720 --> 00:44:18.720
Treblinka," and

00:44:19.760 --> 00:44:21.110
Gabriel Finder asks,

00:44:21.950 --> 00:44:23.749
"What would you consider Polish Jews who

00:44:23.750 --> 00:44:25.563
spent the war years and the Holocaust

00:44:25.880 --> 00:44:26.779
in Soviet Asia?

00:44:26.780 --> 00:44:28.299
Would you consider them also as

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:31.030
survivors, the, kind of,

00:44:31.280 --> 00:44:32.449
stayed within the interior of the Soviet

00:44:32.450 --> 00:44:34.820
Union?" And then we have another

00:44:35.030 --> 00:44:36.897
question from Oren Stier.

00:44:38.780 --> 00:44:40.969
"So you're, you're evolving a critical

00:44:40.970 --> 00:44:42.380
definition of survivorship.

00:44:43.340 --> 00:44:45.049
Your brief remarks at the end about

00:44:45.050 --> 00:44:46.422
category, about categorizing

00:44:46.880 --> 00:44:48.590
through-transport Treblinka survivors,

00:44:48.740 --> 00:44:50.659
even if they subsequently perished, were

00:44:50.660 --> 00:44:52.420
murdered in another camp, as Treblinka

00:44:52.490 --> 00:44:54.229
survivors, has a potential to shake up

00:44:54.230 --> 00:44:55.579
the very notion of survival.

00:44:56.210 --> 00:44:58.059
What, we do not typically classify

00:44:58.340 --> 00:45:00.230
as survivors, those individuals who

00:45:00.740 --> 00:45:02.509
made it through one or a series of camps

00:45:02.510 --> 00:45:04.099
only to be murdered at the last one.

00:45:04.340 --> 00:45:06.349
So what will this

00:45:06.350 --> 00:45:07.669
different perspective do to

00:45:07.670 --> 00:45:09.619
understanding of survivorship related to

00:45:09.620 --> 00:45:10.820
concepts like resistance?"

00:45:11.750 --> 00:45:13.465
I think all of these questions tied

00:45:13.760 --> 00:45:15.269
together and Chad,

00:45:16.020 --> 00:45:17.360
please feel free to answer.

00:45:17.990 --> 00:45:19.754
Yeah. Thanks for all these questions

00:45:20.090 --> 00:45:22.010
and thank Julie for her

00:45:22.370 --> 00:45:23.539
excellent talk as well.

00:45:25.840 --> 00:45:27.400
I think I'll take the first.

00:45:28.750 --> 00:45:30.159
I'm looking at the Q&amp;A here.

00:45:32.300 --> 00:45:33.829
So for escapees

00:45:34.430 --> 00:45:36.860
and what they encounter about

00:45:37.010 --> 00:45:38.774
refusal to believe the reports about

00:45:38.900 --> 00:45:40.510
Treblinka. Absolutely, there's, there's

00:45:40.520 --> 00:45:42.440
a great deal of that in

00:45:42.680 --> 00:45:44.570
oral histories and other testimonies.

00:45:46.490 --> 00:45:48.520
It took more than a few, the

00:45:48.530 --> 00:45:50.659
first you know, there's the first wave

00:45:50.660 --> 00:45:52.999
of escapees were not

00:45:53.450 --> 00:45:54.675
believed or were silenced

00:45:56.120 --> 00:45:58.399
because the, the reality

00:45:58.400 --> 00:46:00.060
of what they were saying was too hard

00:46:02.450 --> 00:46:03.539
for people to bear.

00:46:05.600 --> 00:46:06.600
But as as time goes

00:46:07.670 --> 00:46:09.829
on, the, partly

00:46:09.830 --> 00:46:11.599
through how many people escape the camp

00:46:11.600 --> 00:46:13.489
and how many voices there were saying

00:46:13.490 --> 00:46:14.666
what it really was, that

00:46:15.590 --> 00:46:17.501
information does get out and it becomes

00:46:17.780 --> 00:46:18.949
more and more accepted.

00:46:19.460 --> 00:46:21.559
And testimony

00:46:21.560 --> 00:46:23.929
from revolt survivors,

00:46:23.930 --> 00:46:26.239
particularly individuals who were there

00:46:26.240 --> 00:46:27.808
for more, a longer period in the

00:46:28.280 --> 00:46:29.603
camp. They talk about later

00:46:30.380 --> 00:46:32.389
transports from Poland, Polish

00:46:32.390 --> 00:46:34.340
Jews arriving at Treblinka,

00:46:36.170 --> 00:46:38.479
acting out in spontaneous

00:46:38.480 --> 00:46:40.219
acts of resistance as they get off the

00:46:40.220 --> 00:46:41.690
train because they know exactly what

00:46:41.730 --> 00:46:42.730
Treblinka is.

00:46:43.790 --> 00:46:45.739
There, there's the people have heard

00:46:45.740 --> 00:46:47.308
about how the, the arrival area,

00:46:47.840 --> 00:46:49.339
Treblinka, was painted to look like a

00:46:49.340 --> 00:46:50.340
train station.

00:46:50.480 --> 00:46:52.609
And that really, that ruse really

00:46:52.610 --> 00:46:53.884
only worked on individuals

00:46:54.950 --> 00:46:56.630
arriving from Western Europe.

00:46:57.860 --> 00:47:00.019
There were Jews sent to Treblinka

00:47:00.020 --> 00:47:01.790
from eleven different nations.

00:47:03.410 --> 00:47:05.719
Those from Poland tended to already

00:47:05.720 --> 00:47:06.589
know what it was.

00:47:06.590 --> 00:47:08.449
And there was a good deal of resistance

00:47:08.450 --> 00:47:10.039
in one form or another as they got off

00:47:10.040 --> 00:47:11.040
the train.

00:47:11.630 --> 00:47:13.247
Those from Western Europe or from

00:47:13.340 --> 00:47:15.349
further away tended not to

00:47:15.350 --> 00:47:16.526
know what Treblinka was.

00:47:17.030 --> 00:47:19.579
And perhaps that painting

00:47:19.580 --> 00:47:21.320
of the arrival area as,

00:47:23.540 --> 00:47:25.849
as a, as a train station actually worked

00:47:25.850 --> 00:47:26.850
in that way.

00:47:27.320 --> 00:47:29.390
To the other two questions, I do think,

00:47:29.870 --> 00:47:31.519
to Dr. Finder, I do think that

00:47:32.570 --> 00:47:34.879
Jews who lived out the the

00:47:34.880 --> 00:47:36.546
Holocaust years in Soviet Asia are

00:47:37.130 --> 00:47:38.502
survivors. I particularly, I

00:47:39.140 --> 00:47:40.610
personally like the very, very

00:47:41.270 --> 00:47:43.639
capacious definition

00:47:43.640 --> 00:47:45.349
of survival that's offered by the

00:47:45.650 --> 00:47:47.689
United States Holocaust Memorial Museum

00:47:48.710 --> 00:47:50.809
that says that anyone

00:47:50.810 --> 00:47:53.089
who fled persecution

00:47:53.090 --> 00:47:55.159
basically from the

00:47:55.160 --> 00:47:57.140
Nazi rise to power in 1933,

00:47:57.470 --> 00:47:59.381
however they get out, wherever they go,

00:47:59.450 --> 00:48:00.739
is a Holocaust survivor.

00:48:01.070 --> 00:48:03.108
And then to Dr. Stier's question that is

00:48:03.156 --> 00:48:04.610
sort of connected to that.

00:48:06.620 --> 00:48:07.747
I would say that, I see

00:48:08.690 --> 00:48:09.619
where you're asking that.

00:48:09.620 --> 00:48:11.389
And I think in the longer piece, I talk

00:48:11.390 --> 00:48:13.099
about how that, that individual is still

00:48:13.100 --> 00:48:14.779
not a survivor of the Holocaust, that

00:48:14.780 --> 00:48:16.544
person who dies after getting out of

00:48:16.550 --> 00:48:18.109
Treblinka, but they have survival

00:48:18.110 --> 00:48:20.449
experiences in their past.

00:48:22.460 --> 00:48:24.126
All of these people are victims of

00:48:24.530 --> 00:48:26.890
the Holocaust, to be sure.

00:48:27.440 --> 00:48:29.299
So that that terminology doesn't really

00:48:29.300 --> 00:48:31.579
help us either. But to say that someone

00:48:31.580 --> 00:48:33.148
who perishes at their last camp,

00:48:33.800 --> 00:48:34.999
I would still say they're not a

00:48:35.000 --> 00:48:36.409
Holocaust survivor because they

00:48:36.410 --> 00:48:37.410
perished.

00:48:38.540 --> 00:48:40.579
But they can be, they

00:48:40.580 --> 00:48:42.439
can have a past that is,

00:48:42.800 --> 00:48:44.564
as a survival, survivor of Treblinka

00:48:45.290 --> 00:48:46.613
or fill in other experience

00:48:47.780 --> 00:48:49.699
here. So I think that that just kind of,

00:48:50.730 --> 00:48:52.500
you know, maybe it adds a back story,

00:48:52.620 --> 00:48:54.629
looking at it in that localized context,

00:48:54.630 --> 00:48:56.249
as you bring up there, it adds a back

00:48:56.250 --> 00:48:58.289
story to individuals

00:48:58.290 --> 00:49:00.260
that we were only thinking of as as

00:49:00.270 --> 00:49:01.859
people who perished in the Holocaust,

00:49:01.860 --> 00:49:02.819
perhaps.

00:49:02.820 --> 00:49:03.820
But at some, at, you

00:49:04.890 --> 00:49:06.507
know, at another level, they have

00:49:06.960 --> 00:49:08.264
survived other things,

00:49:09.810 --> 00:49:10.980
whatever those might have been.

00:49:12.300 --> 00:49:13.590
And thanks for all those questions.

00:49:15.630 --> 00:49:17.309
Thank you, Chad. And I think Joanne

00:49:17.310 --> 00:49:19.250
here, Joanne Rudof writes that

00:49:19.320 --> 00:49:21.133
Fortunoff defines survivors as anyone

00:49:21.210 --> 00:49:22.949
who lived under Nazi occupation.

00:49:23.970 --> 00:49:25.181
That's another definition.

00:49:25.990 --> 00:49:28.050
I had a question for Julie also

00:49:29.340 --> 00:49:31.006
here. And my question was a little

00:49:31.440 --> 00:49:32.609
bit, if you could just expand a little

00:49:32.610 --> 00:49:33.610
bit about, right, because, you know the

00:49:33.990 --> 00:49:35.159
Fortunoff Archive, we have video

00:49:35.160 --> 00:49:36.689
testimonies and you're also kind of

00:49:36.690 --> 00:49:38.039
incorporating that with diaries.

00:49:38.530 --> 00:49:39.742
So maybe just also, you know, both

00:49:39.960 --> 00:49:41.249
specifically about your research, but

00:49:41.250 --> 00:49:42.989
also generally working with these two

00:49:42.990 --> 00:49:44.250
different forms of,

00:49:45.240 --> 00:49:47.070
of historical documentation.

00:49:47.460 --> 00:49:48.670
Right. And where do you see the
differences?

00:49:48.690 --> 00:49:50.159
What do you see and what your

00:49:50.160 --> 00:49:51.359
perspective working with both, with

00:49:51.360 --> 00:49:52.438
diaries and with video

00:49:53.460 --> 00:49:54.869
testimony. What is the difference and

00:49:54.870 --> 00:49:56.519
how differently do you think you

00:49:56.520 --> 00:49:57.899
approach and we should approach these

00:49:57.900 --> 00:49:59.519
types of testimony?

00:50:02.659 --> 00:50:03.659
Yeah, thank you.

00:50:04.760 --> 00:50:06.530
It's a question I think about a lot

00:50:07.760 --> 00:50:09.377
in particular with these diaries,

00:50:09.800 --> 00:50:11.869
because they were found diaries

00:50:11.870 --> 00:50:13.193
and there are other diaries

00:50:14.480 --> 00:50:16.010
and numerous memoirs

00:50:16.880 --> 00:50:18.769
about Transnistria and even

00:50:19.040 --> 00:50:21.079
that take place in Romania in some of

00:50:21.080 --> 00:50:22.080
the postwar time.

00:50:22.970 --> 00:50:25.039
But they've been curated and

00:50:25.040 --> 00:50:26.040
redacted.

00:50:26.420 --> 00:50:28.640
And what

00:50:29.240 --> 00:50:31.489
for me is particularly unique about

00:50:31.490 --> 00:50:33.760
the sources, it's

00:50:33.770 --> 00:50:34.709
lostness.

00:50:34.710 --> 00:50:35.780
I mean, the fact that

00:50:37.730 --> 00:50:38.779
actually we also don't know what

00:50:38.780 --> 00:50:39.679
happened to the woman.

00:50:39.680 --> 00:50:41.359
I haven't been able to find her yet as

00:50:41.360 --> 00:50:43.199
the project proceeds.

00:50:43.460 --> 00:50:44.570
I used to think that,

00:50:45.740 --> 00:50:47.329
well, I'm not going to focus on that

00:50:47.330 --> 00:50:48.719
because I have so many of the things

00:50:48.720 --> 00:50:49.609
that I need to focus on.

00:50:49.610 --> 00:50:51.410
But as it goes on, I'm

00:50:51.830 --> 00:50:53.869
finding that it

00:50:53.870 --> 00:50:55.459
seems very important to find out what

00:50:55.460 --> 00:50:57.175
happened to her afterwards, how, if

00:50:57.770 --> 00:50:59.089
there's any way of finding out what

00:50:59.090 --> 00:51:01.520
happened to her and also how she used

00:51:01.910 --> 00:51:03.679
this period that I'm writing about,

00:51:03.680 --> 00:51:06.409
because a lot of these oral histories

00:51:06.770 --> 00:51:09.139
are, you know they're, they

00:51:09.140 --> 00:51:10.140
are told sort

00:51:11.300 --> 00:51:12.949
of from a point of view of a happy

00:51:12.950 --> 00:51:14.599
ending, even though many of the people

00:51:14.600 --> 00:51:16.340
are traumatized for life and maybe they

00:51:16.350 --> 00:51:18.065
wouldn't-- that goes into the whole

00:51:18.320 --> 00:51:19.759
trauma thing, if you can have a happy

00:51:19.760 --> 00:51:21.289
ending after what was experienced.

00:51:21.680 --> 00:51:24.290
But, but nevertheless,

00:51:24.980 --> 00:51:26.659
the memoirs and oral histories talk

00:51:26.660 --> 00:51:28.369
about that a lot. How did you survive?

00:51:28.370 --> 00:51:29.779
What did, what helped you do this?

00:51:29.810 --> 00:51:31.489
How did, why were you resilient?

00:51:31.490 --> 00:51:32.870
These words and

00:51:34.120 --> 00:51:35.779
you know, this is what we don't have

00:51:35.780 --> 00:51:37.103
from the, from the diaries.

00:51:37.400 --> 00:51:38.855
We don't know if she ever...

00:51:40.170 --> 00:51:42.030
what, did she consider then, that

00:51:42.300 --> 00:51:44.579
chapter of her life just one chapter

00:51:44.580 --> 00:51:46.080
and she put it away and she moved on.

00:51:46.110 --> 00:51:48.179
Was she able to move or, move on in

00:51:48.180 --> 00:51:49.180
some way?

00:51:50.400 --> 00:51:52.019
I don't know. And to me, that is the

00:51:52.140 --> 00:51:54.449
biggest difference between these diaries

00:51:54.450 --> 00:51:56.369
specifically and

00:51:56.490 --> 00:51:57.490
the letters also.

00:51:57.690 --> 00:51:59.503
I didn't go into the letters, but the

00:51:59.730 --> 00:52:01.396
letters contained the biographical

00:52:01.590 --> 00:52:03.270
information that I provided.

00:52:03.490 --> 00:52:05.579
She writes them to her deceased

00:52:05.850 --> 00:52:07.469
loved ones, and

00:52:08.040 --> 00:52:09.959
they are narrative in form

00:52:10.290 --> 00:52:11.290
and

00:52:12.450 --> 00:52:14.609
they are different.

00:52:14.850 --> 00:52:16.418
They're more similar to the oral

00:52:16.890 --> 00:52:19.019
histories in that sense, except for that

00:52:19.290 --> 00:52:21.144
she did not write them to an audi--

00:52:21.340 --> 00:52:23.340
or to what audience did she write them.

00:52:24.570 --> 00:52:26.009
So I don't know. I could talk on and on

00:52:26.010 --> 00:52:27.382
about this entire section of

00:52:28.140 --> 00:52:30.269
my project about sort of what was the,

00:52:30.450 --> 00:52:31.590
why did she write?

00:52:31.740 --> 00:52:33.059
For whom was she writing?

00:52:33.060 --> 00:52:34.060
I mean, she wanted the diaries to be

00:52:34.560 --> 00:52:35.549
kept.

00:52:35.550 --> 00:52:37.323
She finds out about Anna Frank

00:52:37.970 --> 00:52:40.559
in the fifties and is very moved

00:52:40.560 --> 00:52:42.030
by this. So I think about that

00:52:42.090 --> 00:52:43.289
sometimes. But of course, she'd already

00:52:43.290 --> 00:52:44.669
been keeping the diaries for a long

00:52:44.670 --> 00:52:45.569
time.

00:52:45.570 --> 00:52:48.030
If she's extremely moved by

00:52:49.650 --> 00:52:50.819
the knowledge of that. And she in

00:52:50.820 --> 00:52:52.850
general was a writer, she wrote a screen

00:52:52.860 --> 00:52:54.209
book and tried to have it, I'm sorry, a

00:52:54.960 --> 00:52:56.969
screenplay and tried to

00:52:56.970 --> 00:52:58.860
have it published. And so,

00:52:59.280 --> 00:53:00.900
yeah, these are all questions that I'm

00:53:01.050 --> 00:53:02.050
dealing with.

00:53:04.170 --> 00:53:05.170
Thank you.

00:53:05.550 --> 00:53:07.169
If anyone has, thank you so much for

00:53:07.200 --> 00:53:08.699
answering, and if anyone has any other

00:53:09.000 --> 00:53:10.127
questions, let me know.

00:53:10.290 --> 00:53:11.519
If not, we're just going to

00:53:12.990 --> 00:53:14.849
actually move on to our next

00:53:14.850 --> 00:53:16.980
presentation by Professor

00:53:17.190 --> 00:53:18.190
Wendy Lower.

00:53:19.050 --> 00:53:20.780
I'll shortly present Professor

00:53:21.090 --> 00:53:22.090
Lower.

00:53:22.440 --> 00:53:24.269
Wendy Lower is the John K.

00:53:24.270 --> 00:53:26.132
Roth Professor of History and Director

00:53:26.310 --> 00:53:27.749
of the Center for Human Rights at

00:53:27.750 --> 00:53:29.309
Claremont McKenna

00:53:30.510 --> 00:53:31.510
College.

00:53:32.280 --> 00:53:34.109
Lower chairs the Academics Committee of

00:53:34.110 --> 00:53:35.429
the United States Holocaust Memorial

00:53:35.430 --> 00:53:37.709
Museum and serves as acting director

00:53:37.710 --> 00:53:39.200
of the Jack, Joseph and Morton Mandel

00:53:39.570 --> 00:53:41.459
Center for Advanced Holocaust Studies of

00:53:41.460 --> 00:53:42.789
the United States Holocaust Memorial

00:53:42.790 --> 00:53:44.999
Museum between, she served between 2016

00:53:45.210 --> 00:53:47.099
and 2018, and

00:53:48.060 --> 00:53:49.824
she was a German Research Foundation

00:53:50.340 --> 00:53:52.096
Fellow at the Ludwing Maximilians

00:53:52.260 --> 00:53:54.329
Universität in Munich between

00:53:54.330 --> 00:53:56.361
2007-2012, where she introduced courses

00:53:57.060 --> 00:53:58.710
on the Holocaust with the testimonies of

00:53:59.130 --> 00:54:01.139
perpetrators and bystanders, and

00:54:01.140 --> 00:54:02.369
led an initiative to establish a

00:54:02.370 --> 00:54:03.599
federally funded German center for

00:54:03.610 --> 00:54:05.339
Holocaust studies at the Institute for

00:54:05.340 --> 00:54:06.340
Contemporary History.

00:54:06.900 --> 00:54:08.819
And Professor Lower is the author of

00:54:08.820 --> 00:54:10.679
multiple books, which you can see

00:54:11.220 --> 00:54:12.869
listed on her profile on her website.

00:54:13.140 --> 00:54:15.360
Among them, the most recent one is

00:54:15.630 --> 00:54:17.599
The Ravine: A Family, A Photograph, A

00:54:17.630 --> 00:54:19.709
Holocaust Massacre Revealed, which she

00:54:19.710 --> 00:54:21.719
also presented about at Yale, I

00:54:21.720 --> 00:54:23.070
think about a month ago.

00:54:23.700 --> 00:54:25.619
And I'm sure many of you have seen also

00:54:25.620 --> 00:54:27.750
the reviews in the press.

00:54:28.110 --> 00:54:29.550
And I'll just let

00:54:30.210 --> 00:54:32.520
Professor Lower start presenting.

00:54:34.320 --> 00:54:36.231
Thank you so much, Gil, can you hear me

00:54:36.360 --> 00:54:37.380
and see the screen?

00:54:37.740 --> 00:54:38.740
Yes.

00:54:38.850 --> 00:54:39.739
Great.

00:54:39.740 --> 00:54:41.159
Well, I want to thank everybody on the

00:54:41.160 --> 00:54:42.777
team at the Fortunoff archive and

00:54:43.260 --> 00:54:44.588
our hosts, Stephen Naron and the

00:54:44.610 --> 00:54:46.559
MacMillan Center. It's been a real honor

00:54:47.070 --> 00:54:48.929
to be the William Rosenberg scholar this

00:54:48.930 --> 00:54:49.930
semester.

00:54:50.490 --> 00:54:52.739
And I just, you know, despite

00:54:52.740 --> 00:54:54.719
the limitations with Covid, we really

00:54:54.720 --> 00:54:56.519
enjoyed our time in New Haven and

00:54:56.730 --> 00:54:58.396
our visits to the library, and our

00:54:58.860 --> 00:55:00.239
mining of the archives there.

00:55:00.240 --> 00:55:02.160
And just incredibly

00:55:02.370 --> 00:55:04.183
grateful for that. And I hope that we

00:55:04.350 --> 00:55:06.419
can return at some point and do

00:55:06.420 --> 00:55:07.530
some more of this in person.

00:55:07.980 --> 00:55:09.599
So let me just get right down to my

00:55:09.600 --> 00:55:10.600
presentation.

00:55:11.280 --> 00:55:12.750
I'm basically broken down into

00:55:13.380 --> 00:55:14.539
kind of seven sections.

00:55:14.550 --> 00:55:16.069
Don't be-- I'm going to keep to

00:55:16.590 --> 00:55:17.590
my time.

00:55:18.060 --> 00:55:19.619
But the way I've organized it is

00:55:19.620 --> 00:55:20.880
basically to start with,

00:55:21.660 --> 00:55:23.620
Eric Hauser is the testimony of focusing

00:55:23.880 --> 00:55:25.608
on, his bio, his biography.

00:55:26.160 --> 00:55:27.160
Then why I selected

00:55:28.590 --> 00:55:30.929
him and my kind of selection process

00:55:30.930 --> 00:55:33.269
and my research process, how I handled

00:55:33.270 --> 00:55:35.940
this source and his testimony,

00:55:36.450 --> 00:55:37.969
and then delve into the content

00:55:38.610 --> 00:55:40.650
of his presentation, why he

00:55:40.950 --> 00:55:42.273
testified, and he states so

00:55:43.110 --> 00:55:45.599
explicitly, and I listen to that and

00:55:45.600 --> 00:55:47.909
studied, you know, what his rationale

00:55:47.910 --> 00:55:49.527
was and what stories he wanted to

00:55:49.560 --> 00:55:50.560
emphasize.

00:55:51.360 --> 00:55:53.130
And then further

00:55:53.800 --> 00:55:55.600
in, in subsequent sections of,

00:55:56.730 --> 00:55:58.289
to look at some of the ideas that

00:55:58.290 --> 00:55:59.610
Lawrence Langer has presented

00:56:00.390 --> 00:56:02.154
as like "frozen moments of anguish,"

00:56:03.030 --> 00:56:05.159
as he puts it, memory

00:56:05.160 --> 00:56:06.979
of, of the senses.

00:56:06.990 --> 00:56:08.429
These were some of the elements of his

00:56:08.430 --> 00:56:10.229
testimony to me that popped out, that

00:56:10.230 --> 00:56:11.640
stood out, that I wanted to

00:56:12.300 --> 00:56:13.300
delve into.

00:56:13.500 --> 00:56:14.921
And then lastly, the material

00:56:15.540 --> 00:56:17.609
culture part of this or the performative

00:56:17.610 --> 00:56:19.949
part of this, how he presents things

00:56:19.950 --> 00:56:21.689
during his testimony, the objects he

00:56:21.690 --> 00:56:23.939
decides to bring into

00:56:23.940 --> 00:56:25.949
his story and the documents

00:56:25.950 --> 00:56:26.950
that he shares.

00:56:27.480 --> 00:56:29.069
And then at some concluding questions

00:56:29.070 --> 00:56:30.491
about the challenge of, as an

00:56:31.110 --> 00:56:33.269
historian, of annotating

00:56:33.270 --> 00:56:34.889
and contextualizing and

00:56:36.150 --> 00:56:38.520
what we mean by contextualization and

00:56:39.270 --> 00:56:40.936
the limits of that, as well as the

00:56:41.190 --> 00:56:42.562
potential problems with over

00:56:43.500 --> 00:56:45.659
contextualizing and kind

00:56:45.660 --> 00:56:48.059
of decentering the

00:56:48.060 --> 00:56:49.980
survivor's testimony

00:56:50.490 --> 00:56:52.303
as a result. So I'm kind of grappling

00:56:52.380 --> 00:56:53.458
with that balancing as

00:56:54.420 --> 00:56:55.420
well as ethical

00:56:57.210 --> 00:56:59.099
endeavor as an historian.

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:01.109
OK, let me just tell you a little bit

00:57:01.110 --> 00:57:02.110
about Eric Hauser.

00:57:02.250 --> 00:57:04.049
Here he is. My screenshot of him from

00:57:04.050 --> 00:57:05.050
the testimony

00:57:06.120 --> 00:57:07.410
that he, he gave.

00:57:07.980 --> 00:57:10.380
He was born in 1908 in

00:57:11.100 --> 00:57:12.510
Gródek, Gródek, Jagiellonski,

00:57:13.260 --> 00:57:14.480
Poland, which is now Horodok,

00:57:15.450 --> 00:57:17.263
Ukraine. He had two siblings, younger

00:57:17.520 --> 00:57:18.598
siblings, a sister and

00:57:19.590 --> 00:57:20.590
a brother.

00:57:20.970 --> 00:57:22.440
Gródek's gentile population at

00:57:22.980 --> 00:57:25.049
the time of his youth, barred

00:57:25.050 --> 00:57:26.819
Jews from entering the trade guilds and

00:57:26.820 --> 00:57:28.380
as a result, the Jewish community there

00:57:28.410 --> 00:57:29.410
was rather poor.

00:57:29.790 --> 00:57:31.559
But despite this, Isaac's family was

00:57:31.560 --> 00:57:33.539
well off. His father, Moses, was a

00:57:33.540 --> 00:57:35.249
contractor of his own business, and he

00:57:35.250 --> 00:57:36.569
built a lot of houses and issued

00:57:36.570 --> 00:57:38.249
mortgages to other Jewish families.

00:57:38.820 --> 00:57:39.996
And Isaac's grandfather.

00:57:40.980 --> 00:57:43.469
I'm referring to Isaac now,

00:57:44.250 --> 00:57:45.599
you know, interchanging out with Eric.

00:57:45.600 --> 00:57:47.519
He took the kind of American name Eric

00:57:48.510 --> 00:57:50.309
when he arrived in America in

00:57:50.850 --> 00:57:52.124
1949. So I referred to him

00:57:52.620 --> 00:57:54.209
interchangeably as Isaac and Eric.

00:57:54.210 --> 00:57:56.039
So I hope that's not that confusing.

00:57:56.520 --> 00:57:57.794
So Isaac's grandfather was

00:57:58.570 --> 00:57:59.999
the head of the Jewish community and he

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:01.710
was able to use his influence to place

00:58:02.430 --> 00:58:04.049
Isaac in the Catholic high school where

00:58:04.050 --> 00:58:05.730
he graduated valedictorian in

00:58:06.360 --> 00:58:07.360
1926.

00:58:07.950 --> 00:58:09.250
And the community itself from Gródek

00:58:10.110 --> 00:58:11.188
prioritized education.

00:58:11.280 --> 00:58:13.049
Most could read Polish and German, and

00:58:13.050 --> 00:58:15.059
everyone read religious texts.

00:58:15.360 --> 00:58:17.222
And like other Jewish youth, Isaac had

00:58:17.310 --> 00:58:19.439
leftist and socialist leanings under

00:58:19.440 --> 00:58:20.760
Polish rule.

00:58:21.180 --> 00:58:23.069
As a Jew, Isaac was not accepted to a

00:58:23.070 --> 00:58:24.359
Polish university and moved to

00:58:24.360 --> 00:58:26.189
Czechoslovakia to attend the technical

00:58:26.190 --> 00:58:28.409
university in Brno, and

00:58:28.410 --> 00:58:30.449
in 1931 he got a master's degree

00:58:30.450 --> 00:58:31.799
in electrical and mechanical

00:58:31.800 --> 00:58:33.269
engineering. This is going to be really

00:58:33.270 --> 00:58:35.219
important as far as his ability to

00:58:35.220 --> 00:58:36.659
survive, that education.

00:58:37.260 --> 00:58:38.789
After completing his studies at the

00:58:38.790 --> 00:58:40.559
university, he returned to Poland.

00:58:40.950 --> 00:58:42.629
His degree was not recognized by the

00:58:42.630 --> 00:58:44.069
authorities and he had to take another

00:58:44.070 --> 00:58:46.019
exam. This is something he delves int in

00:58:46.020 --> 00:58:47.020
his presentation.

00:58:48.480 --> 00:58:50.342
He worked as an engineer and assistant

00:58:50.610 --> 00:58:52.889
manager for a Jewish owned oil field

00:58:52.890 --> 00:58:54.059
in western Galicia.

00:58:54.990 --> 00:58:56.020
And in 1939,

00:58:57.060 --> 00:58:58.829
his family moved into a large house in

00:58:58.830 --> 00:59:00.480
Lwów. They moved into the city

00:59:01.350 --> 00:59:03.050
of Lwów, L'viv, Lvov,

00:59:03.960 --> 00:59:04.960
Poland

00:59:06.150 --> 00:59:07.479
in September '39.

00:59:07.680 --> 00:59:09.000
With the start of the war

00:59:09.990 --> 00:59:10.859
in accordance with the

00:59:10.860 --> 00:59:13.229
Molotov-Ribbentrop pact, Germany invaded

00:59:13.230 --> 00:59:14.129
western Poland.

00:59:14.130 --> 00:59:16.349
The Soviet army annexed eastern Poland.

00:59:16.950 --> 00:59:17.979
Lwów was occupied and

00:59:18.960 --> 00:59:21.449
incorporated into the Ukrainian Soviet

00:59:21.450 --> 00:59:23.009
Socialist Republic, now part of the

00:59:23.010 --> 00:59:24.529
Russian dominated Soviet Union.

00:59:24.570 --> 00:59:26.150
No more interwar, no longer interwar

00:59:26.430 --> 00:59:27.430
Poland, with the break, with the

00:59:27.510 --> 00:59:28.510
destruction of Poland.

00:59:29.400 --> 00:59:32.040
So Isaac's, a manager,

00:59:32.070 --> 00:59:34.089
at this moment emptied most of the

00:59:34.090 --> 00:59:35.920
company's bank account and fled

00:59:36.250 --> 00:59:37.570
for America,

00:59:38.380 --> 00:59:40.199
leaving Isaac in charge of two hundred

00:59:40.220 --> 00:59:41.979
twenty employees and no payroll.

00:59:42.760 --> 00:59:44.559
His reputation among the employees

00:59:44.560 --> 00:59:46.239
prevented them from turning on him.

00:59:47.080 --> 00:59:48.579
Shortly thereafter, as the Germans

00:59:48.580 --> 00:59:50.289
approached, Isaac was given orders to

00:59:50.290 --> 00:59:52.103
dismantle the oil wells and equipment

00:59:52.360 --> 00:59:53.820
to make them nonfunctional.

00:59:54.700 --> 00:59:56.000
And he goes into a lot of detail in

00:59:56.050 --> 00:59:58.479
testimony about the plants,

00:59:58.480 --> 01:00:00.310
the oil fields in

01:00:00.520 --> 01:00:02.679
Boryslav. And he's really impressed

01:00:02.680 --> 01:00:04.120
with these, these,

01:00:04.990 --> 01:00:07.659
the industrial materials

01:00:07.660 --> 01:00:09.909
there and the kind of modern

01:00:10.030 --> 01:00:11.409
features of those plants

01:00:12.280 --> 01:00:13.600
that he still, he's still kind of

01:00:13.930 --> 01:00:15.610
reminisces about that and his eyes

01:00:15.940 --> 01:00:17.139
light up when he talks about it.

01:00:17.140 --> 01:00:19.119
So, but when they're dismantling these

01:00:19.120 --> 01:00:21.579
oil wells and they're hiding carburetors

01:00:21.580 --> 01:00:23.349
and trying to make them nonfunctional,

01:00:23.350 --> 01:00:25.149
this kind of sabotage to him I think is

01:00:25.150 --> 01:00:26.409
kind of is kind of painful, but

01:00:26.410 --> 01:00:27.410
necessary.

01:00:27.910 --> 01:00:29.388
During the night, he took four other

01:00:29.440 --> 01:00:31.869
Jews and they tried to escape in a car

01:00:32.650 --> 01:00:34.239
that he had hidden nearby, he was very

01:00:34.240 --> 01:00:35.240
clever.

01:00:35.680 --> 01:00:37.689
And he, they, they, but he was driving

01:00:37.690 --> 01:00:39.159
on some dark, rough roads and he ended

01:00:39.160 --> 01:00:40.740
up crashing the car into a river.

01:00:42.070 --> 01:00:43.070
They survived.

01:00:43.390 --> 01:00:45.252
And is, this group, that with the four

01:00:45.370 --> 01:00:47.349
other Jews, they managed to walk to the

01:00:47.350 --> 01:00:48.459
railroad station.

01:00:48.880 --> 01:00:49.880
They made it to,

01:00:51.910 --> 01:00:53.681
caught a train to Lwów.

01:00:54.160 --> 01:00:55.299
And the description there of the

01:00:55.300 --> 01:00:57.189
railroad station is really memorable as

01:00:57.190 --> 01:00:58.540
well of the German Stukas

01:00:59.380 --> 01:01:01.242
strafing and coming down and shooting,

01:01:01.270 --> 01:01:02.859
deliberately shooting into the windows

01:01:02.860 --> 01:01:04.929
of the railway cars

01:01:04.930 --> 01:01:06.008
and killing civilians.

01:01:06.610 --> 01:01:08.570
And that was the train they had to board

01:01:08.590 --> 01:01:09.590
and take to Lwów.

01:01:11.230 --> 01:01:13.389
There, when he got to Lwów, he met and

01:01:13.390 --> 01:01:14.920
married Louise Finkler

01:01:15.460 --> 01:01:17.229
so he would be with his wife throughout

01:01:17.230 --> 01:01:18.609
the war

01:01:19.240 --> 01:01:20.400
and survived with her.

01:01:21.100 --> 01:01:22.989
He couldn't find work in Lwów, so he and

01:01:22.990 --> 01:01:24.656
his wife Louise headed forty seven

01:01:25.210 --> 01:01:26.254
miles south to Boryslav,

01:01:27.340 --> 01:01:29.079
where he had extended family, and he

01:01:29.080 --> 01:01:30.609
became the head of a mechanical and

01:01:30.610 --> 01:01:31.813
electrical department at the Boryslav

01:01:32.320 --> 01:01:34.329
Power Plant and worked

01:01:34.330 --> 01:01:35.769
there as the head of the workshop.

01:01:36.040 --> 01:01:37.239
And Louise worked there as well.

01:01:38.680 --> 01:01:40.653
They were among 13,000 Jews of Boryslav

01:01:41.320 --> 01:01:43.179
and subjected to severe restrictions and

01:01:43.180 --> 01:01:45.099
psychological abuse by the Soviets.

01:01:45.790 --> 01:01:47.797
In June '41, when Germany invaded the

01:01:47.953 --> 01:01:49.851
Soviet Union and occupied Boryslav,

01:01:50.740 --> 01:01:52.659
a Ukrainian militia organized a violent

01:01:52.660 --> 01:01:54.750
pogrom against the Jewish population.

01:01:56.020 --> 01:01:57.669
Here's the location here to give you a

01:01:57.670 --> 01:01:59.100
little bit of a geographic orientation.

01:01:59.110 --> 01:02:00.789
This is from Thomas Sandkühler's book

01:02:00.790 --> 01:02:01.790
"Endlösung" in Galizien.

01:02:03.490 --> 01:02:05.430
Here we have the city of Lemberg, L'viv,

01:02:05.800 --> 01:02:07.739
Lwów, Gródek

01:02:08.350 --> 01:02:10.479
here. This is where he was born and grew

01:02:10.480 --> 01:02:11.480
up, Isaac, and

01:02:12.550 --> 01:02:14.559
he's making his way down here to

01:02:14.560 --> 01:02:16.599
the oil fields or petroleum fields of

01:02:16.600 --> 01:02:18.849
Boryslav, not far from

01:02:19.780 --> 01:02:20.780
Drohobych.

01:02:24.276 --> 01:02:26.049
A Ukrainian militia organized a violent

01:02:26.050 --> 01:02:28.180
pogrom against the Jewish population,

01:02:28.930 --> 01:02:30.729
an administration, German administration

01:02:30.730 --> 01:02:32.592
soon authorized the establishment of a

01:02:32.620 --> 01:02:34.482
Jewish ghetto in Boryslav near the end

01:02:34.570 --> 01:02:36.789
of November, when they conducted

01:02:36.790 --> 01:02:38.559
their first organized aktion in the

01:02:38.560 --> 01:02:40.389
city, capturing and killing 7 to

01:02:40.570 --> 01:02:41.570
800 Jews.

01:02:41.800 --> 01:02:44.229
30 members of Isaac's extended family

01:02:44.230 --> 01:02:45.540
were killed as a result.

01:02:45.940 --> 01:02:47.704
These actions against the population

01:02:47.770 --> 01:02:49.840
continue to take place every few months.

01:02:50.140 --> 01:02:51.880
And even though they had papers,

01:02:52.390 --> 01:02:54.459
Isaac had papers, that they were, that

01:02:54.460 --> 01:02:55.789
were supposed to protect them,

01:02:56.530 --> 01:02:58.294
they still hid during the actions to

01:02:58.300 --> 01:03:00.790
avoid getting picked off on the streets.

01:03:01.150 --> 01:03:03.212
In 1942, Ukrainian and mounted

01:03:03.280 --> 01:03:05.709
German police surrounded his column

01:03:05.710 --> 01:03:07.327
marching to work, and Isaac fled,

01:03:07.690 --> 01:03:09.552
jumping over a fence of an oil storage

01:03:09.670 --> 01:03:11.170
facility while being shot at.

01:03:11.500 --> 01:03:13.389
During another early Aktion, he and his

01:03:13.390 --> 01:03:14.859
wife went to the house of the member of

01:03:14.860 --> 01:03:16.809
a Jewish militia who let them hide in

01:03:16.810 --> 01:03:17.739
his apartment.

01:03:17.740 --> 01:03:19.200
Other times, they hid for days in

01:03:19.240 --> 01:03:20.650
partially flooded basements.

01:03:21.250 --> 01:03:23.229
Isaac also helped build an escape tunnel

01:03:23.230 --> 01:03:24.729
for Jews to hide, which they were able

01:03:24.730 --> 01:03:26.250
to construct without detection.

01:03:26.740 --> 01:03:28.504
In November '42, a forced labor camp

01:03:28.900 --> 01:03:30.180
was established for the 1,470

01:03:32.380 --> 01:03:34.179
Jews who were working for the oil

01:03:34.180 --> 01:03:35.518
company in Boryslav.

01:03:36.070 --> 01:03:38.139
And during that month, Isaac was issued

01:03:38.140 --> 01:03:39.267
an R badge, identifying

01:03:40.570 --> 01:03:42.000
him as a Rüstung arbeiter.

01:03:42.010 --> 01:03:43.659
This was a very life saving document.

01:03:43.660 --> 01:03:45.670
During the interview, he placed, he

01:03:45.790 --> 01:03:47.407
still has the badge and he places

01:03:47.920 --> 01:03:48.920
it on his chest,

01:03:50.530 --> 01:03:51.969
in this kind of, in this kind of

01:03:51.970 --> 01:03:53.832
gesture, it was clear that this was an

01:03:53.980 --> 01:03:55.401
incredibly important document

01:03:56.170 --> 01:03:57.640
for him, that he kept

01:03:58.270 --> 01:03:59.979
this pass, allowed him to leave the camp

01:03:59.980 --> 01:04:01.689
to conduct technical work for the oil

01:04:01.690 --> 01:04:02.690
wells.

01:04:02.920 --> 01:04:05.170
He had to pay bribes to officials,

01:04:06.490 --> 01:04:08.499
issuing another ID card

01:04:08.500 --> 01:04:10.149
that specified he was a specialized

01:04:10.150 --> 01:04:11.049
engineer.

01:04:11.050 --> 01:04:12.579
His profession obviously was important

01:04:12.580 --> 01:04:13.580
for the war effort.

01:04:14.500 --> 01:04:15.909
Authorities continue to kill and

01:04:15.910 --> 01:04:17.949
transport Jews to concentration camps,

01:04:17.950 --> 01:04:19.389
and they completed the liquidation of

01:04:19.390 --> 01:04:20.390
the Boryslav ghetto

01:04:21.760 --> 01:04:22.844
in June '43.

01:04:24.190 --> 01:04:25.809
So in this last phase of

01:04:26.800 --> 01:04:28.209
the war in April '44,

01:04:28.900 --> 01:04:30.729
as the Germans were doing these final,

01:04:30.730 --> 01:04:32.690
completing these final liquidations, and

01:04:32.920 --> 01:04:34.749
the Soviet army began advancing toward

01:04:34.750 --> 01:04:36.729
Boryslav, Isaac believed that the

01:04:36.730 --> 01:04:38.469
Germans would kill the remaining Jews.

01:04:38.470 --> 01:04:40.087
And so he forged to pass, another

01:04:40.600 --> 01:04:42.129
document for his wife, and they were

01:04:42.130 --> 01:04:43.479
able to escape the camp.

01:04:43.660 --> 01:04:45.639
And for six weeks they hid in the attic

01:04:45.640 --> 01:04:46.929
of a Ukrainian peasant

01:04:47.710 --> 01:04:49.510
alongside 16 other people.

01:04:49.810 --> 01:04:51.489
Boryslav was liberated by the Soviet

01:04:51.490 --> 01:04:52.809
army on August 7th.

01:04:53.080 --> 01:04:54.849
After liberation, Isaac was able to

01:04:54.850 --> 01:04:56.596
acquire Polish papers for Louise and

01:04:56.710 --> 01:04:58.269
himself, and they were permitted to

01:04:58.270 --> 01:04:59.739
cross the border into Poland.

01:05:00.100 --> 01:05:01.959
They went back to Lwów, where Isaac's

01:05:01.960 --> 01:05:03.600
family had been forced from the Lwów

01:05:03.940 --> 01:05:06.130
ghetto into the Janowska Street camp,

01:05:06.490 --> 01:05:08.289
and his family was probably killed there

01:05:08.290 --> 01:05:10.419
in 1943, when the ghetto

01:05:10.420 --> 01:05:11.769
and the camp were destroyed, and the

01:05:11.770 --> 01:05:13.690
city was declared free of Jews.

01:05:15.700 --> 01:05:17.229
The couple then traveled to Munich,

01:05:17.230 --> 01:05:18.945
where Isaac acquired a job with the

01:05:19.030 --> 01:05:20.030
World ORT Union as

01:05:21.040 --> 01:05:22.853
the director of the vocational school

01:05:23.380 --> 01:05:25.090
there with two thousand students.

01:05:26.950 --> 01:05:28.689
He provided training courses to

01:05:28.690 --> 01:05:30.249
survivors to help them rebuild their

01:05:30.250 --> 01:05:31.960
lives in new countries.

01:05:32.170 --> 01:05:33.969
And in 1947, Isaac

01:05:34.210 --> 01:05:35.529
and Louise gave depositions for the

01:05:35.530 --> 01:05:36.951
court proceedings against the

01:05:37.570 --> 01:05:39.253
police and Gestapo in Boryslav

01:05:39.760 --> 01:05:40.760
and Drohobych.

01:05:41.020 --> 01:05:42.969
Isaac and Louise lived in Munich until

01:05:42.970 --> 01:05:44.469
emigrating to the United States in

01:05:44.470 --> 01:05:45.470
October 1949, where

01:05:46.510 --> 01:05:48.039
they, when they change their names to

01:05:48.040 --> 01:05:49.755
Eric and Louise, and settled in New

01:05:50.080 --> 01:05:51.080
York.

01:05:52.880 --> 01:05:54.529
Here's an image of the oil fields in

01:05:54.530 --> 01:05:55.820
Boryslav where they worked.

01:05:56.110 --> 01:05:57.079
Sorry, this is a bit blurry.

01:05:57.080 --> 01:05:58.893
This was the orphanage he mentions in

01:05:58.940 --> 01:06:00.650
his testimony.

01:06:02.810 --> 01:06:03.810
So

01:06:05.150 --> 01:06:07.699
let me tell you a little bit about my

01:06:08.180 --> 01:06:09.180
selection process.

01:06:09.380 --> 01:06:11.599
And here is the document that he,

01:06:11.810 --> 01:06:14.030
his testimony that he gave, I mentioned,

01:06:14.750 --> 01:06:16.999
in Munich to the office,

01:06:17.000 --> 01:06:18.469
at the office of Liberated Jews in

01:06:19.040 --> 01:06:21.019
1947, which was held in the

01:06:21.740 --> 01:06:23.029
Sterling Memorial, part of the

01:06:23.030 --> 01:06:24.649
collection of the Fortunoff Archive

01:06:24.650 --> 01:06:26.479
there, that I was able to to study as

01:06:26.480 --> 01:06:27.480
well.

01:06:28.550 --> 01:06:30.510
Well, I started out looking at survivors

01:06:30.860 --> 01:06:32.659
of wartime Ukraine, today's borders,

01:06:32.660 --> 01:06:33.979
because I'd worked on Ukraine and I

01:06:33.980 --> 01:06:35.059
thought about it would be a good

01:06:35.060 --> 01:06:36.060
foundation.

01:06:36.260 --> 01:06:37.583
And then I, and I looked at

01:06:38.300 --> 01:06:39.619
those, the ones that actually were in

01:06:39.620 --> 01:06:41.620
English. This testimony, Hauser

01:06:41.870 --> 01:06:44.239
gave in English, and his testimony

01:06:44.240 --> 01:06:45.919
really was outstanding

01:06:46.250 --> 01:06:48.559
for me because of the vivid

01:06:49.250 --> 01:06:51.739
memories that he presented,

01:06:52.790 --> 01:06:54.289
the image I described to you of the

01:06:54.290 --> 01:06:56.449
German planes firing

01:06:56.450 --> 01:06:57.920
into the trains, the fact that

01:06:58.520 --> 01:07:00.500
he, he was able to provide that kind of

01:07:00.650 --> 01:07:02.659
empirically rich detail, specific

01:07:02.660 --> 01:07:04.939
names and places of people

01:07:04.940 --> 01:07:06.349
he interacted with, the Poles,

01:07:06.350 --> 01:07:08.239
Ukrainians, Russians, Germans

01:07:08.480 --> 01:07:09.709
and fellow Jews.

01:07:10.460 --> 01:07:12.273
Identifying the worst perpetrators in

01:07:12.440 --> 01:07:14.008
the region. I, and many of those

01:07:14.480 --> 01:07:15.769
names were familiar to me, and I knew

01:07:15.770 --> 01:07:17.719
some of them were very obscure,

01:07:18.080 --> 01:07:19.459
not that well known in the literature.

01:07:19.460 --> 01:07:21.109
And yet he was calling them out

01:07:21.710 --> 01:07:22.710
for, like one

01:07:23.840 --> 01:07:25.702
of the chiefs, chiefs I wrote about in

01:07:25.760 --> 01:07:27.739
Hitler's Furies actually, is the

01:07:27.950 --> 01:07:29.809
husband and wife and, in Drohobych, the

01:07:29.810 --> 01:07:30.810
Blocks.

01:07:31.640 --> 01:07:32.869
Also, we have is supporting

01:07:32.870 --> 01:07:34.729
documentation, this testimony that's on

01:07:34.730 --> 01:07:36.144
the screen there from 1947,

01:07:36.740 --> 01:07:38.449
which would open up another,

01:07:39.740 --> 01:07:41.553
possibilities for research to kind of

01:07:41.750 --> 01:07:43.669
corroborate those names and to look into

01:07:43.670 --> 01:07:46.160
those war crimes cases.

01:07:46.550 --> 01:07:48.649
His, the fact that he covered kind of

01:07:48.650 --> 01:07:50.749
each chapter of his life, his youth

01:07:51.080 --> 01:07:52.130
in Poland.

01:07:52.970 --> 01:07:55.369
He described the inter ethnic tensions

01:07:55.370 --> 01:07:57.134
of Poles and Ukrainians and Jews and

01:07:57.440 --> 01:07:59.269
how that was aggravated and exploited by

01:07:59.270 --> 01:08:01.449
the Soviet and Nazi occupiers.

01:08:02.270 --> 01:08:04.459
He was candid about some of the most

01:08:04.460 --> 01:08:06.440
cruel and shameful behavior,

01:08:06.590 --> 01:08:08.989
including crimes committed by Jews,

01:08:09.800 --> 01:08:10.939
sexual violence.

01:08:11.770 --> 01:08:12.770
You know, he just,

01:08:13.970 --> 01:08:15.830
there were no taboos in the way that he

01:08:16.490 --> 01:08:18.739
presented his, his account,

01:08:19.310 --> 01:08:20.829
and the clarity of his purpose.

01:08:21.050 --> 01:08:22.819
He said, I'm giving my testimony and

01:08:23.060 --> 01:08:24.677
he would stop and kind of cue the

01:08:24.920 --> 01:08:27.079
audience, you know, pay

01:08:27.080 --> 01:08:28.080
attention to this now.

01:08:29.090 --> 01:08:31.129
But also the fact that he was not

01:08:32.660 --> 01:08:33.849
in his straightforward

01:08:34.819 --> 01:08:37.159
accounts, you know, there were a lot

01:08:37.160 --> 01:08:39.289
of, it was very emotional as well.

01:08:39.290 --> 01:08:41.719
And he would stop, the kinds of gestures

01:08:41.720 --> 01:08:42.994
on the screen, the pauses,

01:08:44.149 --> 01:08:45.149
the coughs, the

01:08:46.189 --> 01:08:47.209
downward glances.

01:08:47.210 --> 01:08:48.858
Sometimes he holds up the paper and kind

01:08:48.859 --> 01:08:50.119
of hides behind the papers.

01:08:50.120 --> 01:08:52.100
He's talking because he's getting,

01:08:53.750 --> 01:08:54.889
his eyes are welling up.

01:08:54.890 --> 01:08:56.869
And when he, what he says, moments when

01:08:56.870 --> 01:08:58.399
he becomes overcome.

01:08:59.540 --> 01:09:01.010
And the themes that are in his

01:09:01.069 --> 01:09:03.169
storytelling of gender, the sexual

01:09:03.170 --> 01:09:05.209
violence, the attention to women's

01:09:05.210 --> 01:09:07.249
history, the attention to class,

01:09:07.250 --> 01:09:08.935
talking about what he calls the Klondike

01:09:09.229 --> 01:09:11.000
laborers in the oil fields that he

01:09:11.210 --> 01:09:13.170
managed, the drill masters, the welders,

01:09:13.279 --> 01:09:15.043
the blacksmiths, the rough types, he

01:09:15.229 --> 01:09:17.091
said, who are eager to fight, whom the

01:09:17.180 --> 01:09:19.489
Russians broke and turned it into ants

01:09:19.490 --> 01:09:21.079
and lambs and informers.

01:09:22.160 --> 01:09:23.679
His descriptions of race in the

01:09:24.050 --> 01:09:26.029
hierarchies, the Nazi ladder that he

01:09:26.120 --> 01:09:27.919
talked of, related privileges and

01:09:27.920 --> 01:09:29.869
deprivations that were determined by

01:09:30.140 --> 01:09:31.140
race.

01:09:32.180 --> 01:09:33.454
So my process of, of going

01:09:34.310 --> 01:09:36.349
through his,

01:09:36.350 --> 01:09:38.510
his testimony was,

01:09:39.359 --> 01:09:40.437
you know, multipronged

01:09:41.870 --> 01:09:42.870
in a way.

01:09:43.250 --> 01:09:44.569
First, I listened to it.

01:09:45.109 --> 01:09:47.329
I was stopping and taking

01:09:47.600 --> 01:09:49.640
notes, put, inserting footnotes

01:09:50.210 --> 01:09:51.799
and various annotations, and then I

01:09:51.800 --> 01:09:53.659
would put my own remarks in about his

01:09:53.660 --> 01:09:55.369
manners and his gestures and facial

01:09:55.370 --> 01:09:56.329
expressions.

01:09:56.330 --> 01:09:58.189
I basically combined the Christopher

01:09:58.190 --> 01:10:00.169
Browning approach of triangulation, of

01:10:00.190 --> 01:10:02.689
testimony with other sources and various

01:10:03.020 --> 01:10:04.588
tests of, say, probability, and,

01:10:04.970 --> 01:10:06.530
veracity and logic.

01:10:06.860 --> 01:10:08.689
I checked his

01:10:09.410 --> 01:10:11.223
stories and some of his the names and

01:10:11.420 --> 01:10:13.699
places in it and accounts against

01:10:14.870 --> 01:10:16.069
secondary literature.

01:10:16.670 --> 01:10:18.099
Dieter Pohl's work, Thomas Sandkühler,

01:10:18.860 --> 01:10:20.149
Tim Snyder's work,

01:10:21.260 --> 01:10:22.260
Kai Struve,

01:10:23.630 --> 01:10:24.920
John-Paul Himka, of course.

01:10:25.610 --> 01:10:26.786
Then I checked the trial

01:10:27.680 --> 01:10:29.444
records for the perpetrators that he

01:10:29.720 --> 01:10:31.909
identified and

01:10:32.660 --> 01:10:35.089
many of them were indicted

01:10:35.960 --> 01:10:37.479
in a case in Bremen and also an

01:10:37.850 --> 01:10:39.467
important case that's occurred in

01:10:39.920 --> 01:10:41.488
Austria, I'm waiting to get that

01:10:41.900 --> 01:10:42.900
indictment actually from the Wiener

01:10:43.100 --> 01:10:44.100
Library.

01:10:44.750 --> 01:10:47.029
I went into the ITS records and found

01:10:47.030 --> 01:10:48.529
his name on various lists

01:10:49.190 --> 01:10:50.479
from the World Jewish Congress.

01:10:52.460 --> 01:10:53.460
I also tried to find

01:10:54.470 --> 01:10:55.339
his descendants.

01:10:55.340 --> 01:10:57.739
I tried to, I found the Hauser family

01:10:57.740 --> 01:10:59.479
in New York, but it was Buffalo, New

01:10:59.480 --> 01:11:00.550
York, not Rego Park.

01:11:00.740 --> 01:11:02.209
I found the wrong Hauser family.

01:11:02.210 --> 01:11:04.699
And other family of the descendants

01:11:04.700 --> 01:11:05.876
of survivors I talked to

01:11:06.800 --> 01:11:08.419
actually yesterday in Buffalo, New York,

01:11:08.420 --> 01:11:09.420
but not the right one.

01:11:10.160 --> 01:11:11.930
So I'm trying to pursue that

01:11:12.200 --> 01:11:13.200
as well.

01:11:13.430 --> 01:11:15.349
So I combine this empirical approach

01:11:15.620 --> 01:11:17.539
with what kind of Noah

01:11:17.689 --> 01:11:19.310
Shenker and Han-- Hank Greenspan

01:11:19.580 --> 01:11:21.229
stressed in their work on listening and

01:11:21.230 --> 01:11:22.910
reframing Holocaust testimony

01:11:23.270 --> 01:11:24.379
with special attention on the

01:11:24.380 --> 01:11:25.409
performative aspects.

01:11:25.640 --> 01:11:27.049
Because, of course, this is a video

01:11:27.050 --> 01:11:28.009
account.

01:11:28.010 --> 01:11:30.099
And I was inspired as well by what

01:11:30.110 --> 01:11:31.849
the founders of the Fortunoff Archive

01:11:31.850 --> 01:11:33.919
envisioned, that is

01:11:33.920 --> 01:11:36.079
a collection of voices that effectively,

01:11:36.710 --> 01:11:39.140
effectively and affectively

01:11:39.950 --> 01:11:42.199
created a provisional community

01:11:42.200 --> 01:11:44.419
bound by memory and the recognition

01:11:44.420 --> 01:11:45.420
of trauma.

01:11:45.470 --> 01:11:46.819
So I wanted to be conscious of the

01:11:46.820 --> 01:11:48.680
manner in which the testimonies

01:11:49.100 --> 01:11:51.130
were collected, the stories,

01:11:51.470 --> 01:11:53.539
how they are told of human suffering,

01:11:53.810 --> 01:11:55.159
and then the history that is then

01:11:55.940 --> 01:11:57.459
created as a result or conveyed

01:11:57.950 --> 01:11:59.749
and, and received.

01:12:01.010 --> 01:12:02.989
In Noah Shenker's book, Reframing

01:12:02.990 --> 01:12:04.558
Holocaust Testimony, he makes an

01:12:04.640 --> 01:12:06.229
interesting, distinct-- distinction

01:12:06.230 --> 01:12:08.869
between or among chronologically

01:12:08.870 --> 01:12:10.220
charted testimonies.

01:12:10.610 --> 01:12:12.739
On the one hand, the redemptive,

01:12:12.740 --> 01:12:14.602
cathartic version, a kind of dialectic

01:12:14.720 --> 01:12:16.760
with the interviewer, and then

01:12:17.090 --> 01:12:19.159
the third, what he refers to as

01:12:19.160 --> 01:12:21.679
these testimonies that contain,

01:12:22.520 --> 01:12:25.039
in Langer's words, frozen moments

01:12:25.190 --> 01:12:26.190
of anguish.

01:12:26.240 --> 01:12:28.399
And this is what Hauser called in his

01:12:28.400 --> 01:12:30.589
own self-effacing way, moments

01:12:30.590 --> 01:12:32.329
when, as he stated at the end of his

01:12:32.330 --> 01:12:34.609
interview, quote, "I cannot collect

01:12:34.610 --> 01:12:36.169
myself sometimes so well.

01:12:36.470 --> 01:12:38.509
One is overcome." So I was

01:12:38.510 --> 01:12:40.579
interested in this on

01:12:40.580 --> 01:12:42.739
a more conceptual side, the storytelling

01:12:42.740 --> 01:12:44.569
that, the ruptures, the unsettled

01:12:44.570 --> 01:12:46.609
moments, not the usual digressions

01:12:46.610 --> 01:12:48.949
that we talk about in history

01:12:48.950 --> 01:12:51.169
writing, but as Shenker states, those

01:12:51.170 --> 01:12:53.329
both shattering and unifying

01:12:53.330 --> 01:12:54.330
impulses.

01:12:56.140 --> 01:12:57.140
So why did

01:12:58.270 --> 01:13:00.152
our, our subject, Isaac

01:13:00.280 --> 01:13:01.419
Hauser, why did he

01:13:02.620 --> 01:13:03.620
testify?

01:13:03.910 --> 01:13:05.679
First of all, the pursuit of justice.

01:13:05.680 --> 01:13:06.819
Here, I've had this slide up for a

01:13:06.820 --> 01:13:07.749
while.

01:13:07.750 --> 01:13:09.789
The list of names was rather

01:13:09.790 --> 01:13:11.439
astounding. You can, if you can read

01:13:11.440 --> 01:13:12.969
this German document, it's also an

01:13:12.970 --> 01:13:15.010
English translation

01:13:15.250 --> 01:13:17.169
of, of, of the killers and

01:13:17.170 --> 01:13:18.939
distinguishing between the Gestapo and

01:13:18.940 --> 01:13:20.620
the Schupo. Had, you know, had

01:13:21.250 --> 01:13:23.529
it really, had retained

01:13:23.530 --> 01:13:25.269
that information and deliberately during

01:13:25.270 --> 01:13:27.390
the war, documented that

01:13:27.400 --> 01:13:28.840
and then immediately went or,

01:13:29.440 --> 01:13:31.839
went to the Liberated Jews

01:13:31.960 --> 01:13:33.626
Commission in Munich to to provide

01:13:34.420 --> 01:13:35.420
that information.

01:13:36.130 --> 01:13:38.199
Secondly, he stated, "the reason I

01:13:38.200 --> 01:13:40.149
do this interview only I would like to

01:13:40.150 --> 01:13:42.279
show that the Russians broke

01:13:42.280 --> 01:13:44.319
our spirit in

01:13:44.320 --> 01:13:45.810
a way there.

01:13:45.820 --> 01:13:47.739
Well, the Germans killed us physically."

01:13:47.740 --> 01:13:48.999
So there are a couple of occasions where

01:13:49.000 --> 01:13:50.949
he compares the Germans and the Russians

01:13:51.760 --> 01:13:54.039
and of course, in this period

01:13:54.040 --> 01:13:55.690
in the literature especially Timothy

01:13:55.691 --> 01:13:57.850
Snyder's work, among others, of this

01:13:58.120 --> 01:14:00.189
notion and the politicization of the

01:14:00.190 --> 01:14:02.530
double occupation, or the entanglement

01:14:02.830 --> 01:14:05.320
of the Soviet and Nazi systems.

01:14:05.650 --> 01:14:07.509
And it's, in his testimony, it became

01:14:07.510 --> 01:14:09.370
clear to me that this entanglement,

01:14:09.940 --> 01:14:12.069
that, that kind of characterization is

01:14:12.070 --> 01:14:13.749
maybe not entirely correct and certainly

01:14:13.750 --> 01:14:15.661
not this notion of a binary of a double

01:14:15.880 --> 01:14:18.489
occupation that can be compared

01:14:18.490 --> 01:14:20.800
as worse or better,

01:14:20.830 --> 01:14:22.790
both qualitatively or quantitatively.

01:14:23.200 --> 01:14:25.629
But instead, Eric, Isaac,

01:14:26.320 --> 01:14:28.539
he describes what

01:14:28.540 --> 01:14:30.219
he calls the kind of Russian cure

01:14:31.120 --> 01:14:32.949
and describing it very quickly, a moment

01:14:33.520 --> 01:14:35.690
as part of the

01:14:35.740 --> 01:14:37.330
the radicalization.

01:14:38.380 --> 01:14:39.999
And I guess Snyder would go back to

01:14:40.570 --> 01:14:42.100
1933 to show how this kind of

01:14:43.000 --> 01:14:44.260
occurs over time.

01:14:44.920 --> 01:14:47.020
But the way that

01:14:47.470 --> 01:14:48.470
the Russians treated

01:14:49.630 --> 01:14:51.759
the, you know, in this first phase was

01:14:51.760 --> 01:14:53.080
so demoralizing,

01:14:54.310 --> 01:14:55.329
as he describes it.

01:14:58.810 --> 01:15:00.400
He says, I'm quoting now,

01:15:01.810 --> 01:15:03.380
"We didn't, my reason for appearing

01:15:03.730 --> 01:15:05.641
today is I wanted to make sure, I don't

01:15:05.740 --> 01:15:07.651
know if I did, to stress the point that

01:15:07.810 --> 01:15:09.909
we should have resisted much

01:15:09.910 --> 01:15:12.069
more strongly than we did." We,

01:15:12.070 --> 01:15:13.070
as in the Jews.

01:15:13.270 --> 01:15:15.279
"And we didn't because the Russians have

01:15:15.280 --> 01:15:16.479
conditioned it to us.

01:15:16.690 --> 01:15:18.039
The Russians, with their preceding

01:15:18.040 --> 01:15:20.079
occupation, have changed us into a bunch

01:15:20.080 --> 01:15:21.430
of low animals.

01:15:21.820 --> 01:15:23.859
I don't know, probably lower than rats,

01:15:24.070 --> 01:15:25.959
because I read that when rats are pushed

01:15:25.960 --> 01:15:27.939
towards the wall, they will rear up

01:15:27.940 --> 01:15:29.739
their legs and they will show their

01:15:29.740 --> 01:15:31.329
teeth and they will try to bite.

01:15:31.780 --> 01:15:33.642
The Russians have methods and the best

01:15:34.000 --> 01:15:35.889
proof is right in the Russian history of

01:15:35.890 --> 01:15:37.466
the last 40 years.

01:15:38.290 --> 01:15:39.479
The Germans were murderous.

01:15:39.490 --> 01:15:40.569
This is all well known.

01:15:40.580 --> 01:15:41.770
There's no secret to it.

01:15:42.040 --> 01:15:43.810
The Russian rule of calamity

01:15:45.130 --> 01:15:46.420
is not as well known.

01:15:46.600 --> 01:15:48.399
They didn't, they didn't liberate us.

01:15:48.580 --> 01:15:50.379
We survived because of some skill, of

01:15:50.380 --> 01:15:51.609
some good luck, of some happy

01:15:51.610 --> 01:15:52.610
coincidence.

01:15:52.870 --> 01:15:54.781
I hope the, I think the Russians helped

01:15:55.000 --> 01:15:56.764
kill us. My father was a very strong

01:15:57.280 --> 01:15:59.140
man. He was a building contractor.

01:15:59.470 --> 01:16:01.329
But they wrote, they broke his spirit.

01:16:01.330 --> 01:16:02.330
They came in.

01:16:02.530 --> 01:16:03.459
He wasn't permitted.

01:16:03.460 --> 01:16:04.359
They came into our house.

01:16:04.360 --> 01:16:05.360
He took our house.

01:16:05.470 --> 01:16:07.089
Every night, they came to try to arrest

01:16:07.090 --> 01:16:08.559
him. Every night, he slept on the roof

01:16:08.560 --> 01:16:10.239
because he, he wouldn't go up on the

01:16:10.240 --> 01:16:11.240
roof to look."

01:16:12.340 --> 01:16:14.379
Just describing this incredibly

01:16:14.860 --> 01:16:15.880
demoralizing

01:16:16.960 --> 01:16:17.960
period preceding the

01:16:19.030 --> 01:16:21.310
German, German occupation that

01:16:21.340 --> 01:16:24.069
ultimately created this situation.

01:16:24.820 --> 01:16:26.299
Frozen moments of anguish,

01:16:26.950 --> 01:16:27.950
quoting Langer here.

01:16:28.240 --> 01:16:29.477
And in Hauser's

01:16:30.370 --> 01:16:31.987
reflections about the fate of his

01:16:32.020 --> 01:16:33.020
family,

01:16:34.420 --> 01:16:36.489
here he describes how

01:16:36.490 --> 01:16:37.666
his father at the moment

01:16:38.560 --> 01:16:40.270
of the onset of the war in

01:16:40.570 --> 01:16:42.383
'39, with the bombs falling, that his

01:16:42.520 --> 01:16:44.619
father just became so focused

01:16:44.620 --> 01:16:46.300
on his work, that was his

01:16:47.440 --> 01:16:48.369
identity.

01:16:48.370 --> 01:16:49.929
That was his his dignity.

01:16:50.740 --> 01:16:52.419
He says, "my father was building a huge

01:16:52.420 --> 01:16:53.289
house for Poland.

01:16:53.290 --> 01:16:55.029
It was a huge house, a four story house

01:16:55.030 --> 01:16:56.030
in the city of Lemberg.

01:16:56.050 --> 01:16:57.609
He finished building it as the first

01:16:57.610 --> 01:16:59.586
bombs were falling in Lemberg in 1939.

01:17:00.220 --> 01:17:02.082
What is interesting for my father that

01:17:02.260 --> 01:17:03.260
he paid his debts.

01:17:03.640 --> 01:17:05.909
He was convinced his worth is as good.."

01:17:06.550 --> 01:17:08.049
And then this is when he starts to get

01:17:08.050 --> 01:17:09.177
choked up and he stops,

01:17:10.060 --> 01:17:12.310
and he gets, he looks down, he starts

01:17:12.430 --> 01:17:14.529
to scratch his chin and

01:17:14.530 --> 01:17:16.329
then he kind of smiles a little bit.

01:17:16.720 --> 01:17:18.489
"That his worth is as good...as

01:17:20.580 --> 01:17:21.509
gold.

01:17:21.510 --> 01:17:23.323
While the bombs were falling, he paid

01:17:23.550 --> 01:17:24.677
his debts." And then he

01:17:25.590 --> 01:17:27.390
describes how the Russians completely

01:17:27.810 --> 01:17:29.520
broke the spirit of his father.

01:17:30.210 --> 01:17:31.827
But there's a kind of critique of

01:17:32.340 --> 01:17:33.779
his father's reaction

01:17:34.530 --> 01:17:36.629
to the war and the first bombs falling.

01:17:37.410 --> 01:17:39.298
And a kind of irony there as far as his

01:17:39.299 --> 01:17:41.039
contin-- wanting to focus on work and

01:17:41.040 --> 01:17:42.412
not realizing the gravity of

01:17:43.050 --> 01:17:44.050
the situation.

01:17:44.400 --> 01:17:45.989
And then secondly, when

01:17:46.920 --> 01:17:48.960
Eric is fleeing with

01:17:49.320 --> 01:17:51.089
with his wife to Boryslav, when they

01:17:51.090 --> 01:17:52.310
realize they need to get out of Lwów,

01:17:53.520 --> 01:17:55.186
he runs back during the pogrom and

01:17:55.590 --> 01:17:57.109
his family is sitting together,

01:17:57.270 --> 01:17:59.078
including his sister and his brothers,

01:17:59.460 --> 01:18:00.569
younger sister and brother.

01:18:00.780 --> 01:18:02.642
And he says, "I went in, my family was

01:18:02.760 --> 01:18:04.421
there. I told them, 'disperse!

01:18:04.590 --> 01:18:06.149
There is something vicious going on.'

01:18:06.360 --> 01:18:08.429
And they said, 'no, we sit together.

01:18:08.700 --> 01:18:10.401
We feel better when we sit together.'

01:18:10.800 --> 01:18:12.025
And so they sat together.

01:18:12.060 --> 01:18:13.260
They all died together.

01:18:13.480 --> 01:18:15.359
I ran away and I hid out."

01:18:16.170 --> 01:18:18.239
And so is this kind

01:18:18.240 --> 01:18:20.489
of frustration that he seems to

01:18:20.490 --> 01:18:22.109
communicate about not being able to

01:18:22.110 --> 01:18:23.923
rescue his, you know, save his family

01:18:23.970 --> 01:18:25.739
and convince them to, to, to leave.

01:18:26.130 --> 01:18:27.659
And he doesn't see them again after

01:18:27.660 --> 01:18:28.660
that.

01:18:29.040 --> 01:18:30.216
And he assumes that they

01:18:31.140 --> 01:18:32.266
died in Janowska.

01:18:32.940 --> 01:18:34.530
And I'm going to show you

01:18:35.190 --> 01:18:36.869
how that kind of follows through into

01:18:36.870 --> 01:18:38.340
the rest of his biography, not

01:18:39.090 --> 01:18:40.890
knowing kind of their ultimate fate,

01:18:41.070 --> 01:18:42.720
really. I mean, what happened what

01:18:42.900 --> 01:18:45.240
happened to them after he left

01:18:45.630 --> 01:18:46.812
in those intervening years.

01:18:48.860 --> 01:18:50.510
I don't know if I have that much time.

01:18:52.700 --> 01:18:53.720
How much time do I have left?

01:18:55.640 --> 01:18:57.529
Um, we're a little bit over time, so if

01:18:57.530 --> 01:18:58.219
you can just, uh--

01:18:58.220 --> 01:18:59.479
OK, then I'm going to, then I'm going to

01:18:59.480 --> 01:19:00.480
skip through this.

01:19:00.800 --> 01:19:02.119
I just want to point out here, we can't

01:19:02.120 --> 01:19:03.120
read all this.

01:19:03.260 --> 01:19:05.299
The other element of the testimony

01:19:05.540 --> 01:19:06.679
having to do with the memory of the

01:19:06.680 --> 01:19:08.659
senses and being attuned to

01:19:10.130 --> 01:19:12.289
expressions of a set,

01:19:12.290 --> 01:19:13.368
of sound, of sight, of

01:19:14.390 --> 01:19:16.579
smell, in fact, that he describes

01:19:16.580 --> 01:19:18.499
here, in his,

01:19:18.890 --> 01:19:20.710
one of his intentions was to

01:19:20.960 --> 01:19:22.519
show the total brutalization that

01:19:22.520 --> 01:19:24.859
occurred. So he has vivid descriptions

01:19:24.860 --> 01:19:25.999
of of the violence.

01:19:26.780 --> 01:19:29.270
And here is a German official

01:19:29.690 --> 01:19:31.288
who is kills,

01:19:31.880 --> 01:19:33.319
kills his Jewish boy,

01:19:34.370 --> 01:19:35.929
12 to 13 year old boy

01:19:36.470 --> 01:19:37.699
who's already wounded.

01:19:37.700 --> 01:19:39.660
And the SS man says, oh, at some there's

01:19:39.920 --> 01:19:42.109
a discussion, should we leave this child

01:19:42.110 --> 01:19:43.110
in peace?

01:19:43.940 --> 01:19:44.940
The kid is afraid.

01:19:45.080 --> 01:19:46.789
And then the more brutal German says

01:19:46.790 --> 01:19:48.619
Solle lernen, cried the first one, he

01:19:48.620 --> 01:19:50.209
should learn. Now that is something he

01:19:50.210 --> 01:19:51.210
should learn.

01:19:52.120 --> 01:19:54.040
Solle lernen. And then Isaac Hauser

01:19:54.300 --> 01:19:55.999
says, it sounds in my ears and

01:19:56.400 --> 01:19:58.340
they kind of finish off that child.

01:19:59.450 --> 01:20:01.160
And similar kind of

01:20:01.820 --> 01:20:03.229
way of storytelling where he says, "I

01:20:03.230 --> 01:20:04.369
have to say. I saw this.

01:20:04.370 --> 01:20:06.109
I saw this." And that's the other

01:20:06.110 --> 01:20:07.979
account on the right that has to do with

01:20:07.980 --> 01:20:10.069
the sexual violence, the rape of

01:20:10.070 --> 01:20:12.289
a Jewish woman in his office by a

01:20:12.290 --> 01:20:14.210
Jewish coworker.

01:20:15.380 --> 01:20:16.380
These are snapshots.

01:20:17.390 --> 01:20:19.007
Here are still screenshots of the

01:20:19.280 --> 01:20:21.199
documents that he presents during his

01:20:21.200 --> 01:20:23.209
interview that I wanted to pay

01:20:23.210 --> 01:20:24.210
attention to.

01:20:24.380 --> 01:20:26.320
And then lastly, this is a prisoner bowl

01:20:26.930 --> 01:20:29.359
that he brought to the Holocaust Museum.

01:20:29.360 --> 01:20:31.609
So I checked where Eric Hauser might

01:20:31.610 --> 01:20:33.472
have donated things or given testimony

01:20:33.680 --> 01:20:34.760
at other institutions.

01:20:34.890 --> 01:20:37.039
And this was given in 1990 after,

01:20:37.310 --> 01:20:39.409
after his 1985 testimony.

01:20:39.410 --> 01:20:41.329
Why he didn't have the bowl at this,

01:20:41.510 --> 01:20:42.931
you know, at this moment, but

01:20:43.520 --> 01:20:45.409
his papers, is a question

01:20:45.740 --> 01:20:46.969
I thought was interesting. But he ended

01:20:46.970 --> 01:20:47.899
up donating this bowl.

01:20:47.900 --> 01:20:49.970
It's a bowl he found when he went to the

01:20:49.980 --> 01:20:52.009
Janowska camp in search of his family.

01:20:52.700 --> 01:20:54.219
There's no, it didn't belong to

01:20:54.770 --> 01:20:56.269
his family, it's just kind of symbolic.

01:20:56.270 --> 01:20:58.720
It's what he has left of his family.

01:20:59.750 --> 01:21:01.269
So my concluding questions have

01:21:01.850 --> 01:21:03.770
to do with the Critical Edition and

01:21:04.010 --> 01:21:06.079
how do I bring this all together as far

01:21:06.080 --> 01:21:07.939
as both the performative,

01:21:08.300 --> 01:21:09.623
the kind of sensory aspects

01:21:10.310 --> 01:21:11.310
of his storytelling

01:21:12.470 --> 01:21:14.779
and the deep memory, and then

01:21:14.780 --> 01:21:16.279
the contextualization of that as a

01:21:16.280 --> 01:21:18.093
historian, in the more empirical side

01:21:18.530 --> 01:21:20.509
of that? And how far do I go with that

01:21:21.230 --> 01:21:23.180
without kind of diminishing his

01:21:23.990 --> 01:21:25.656
story? Or do I fill in his gaps of

01:21:26.060 --> 01:21:27.060
memory?

01:21:27.230 --> 01:21:28.609
And how far do I go with that in my

01:21:28.610 --> 01:21:30.140
introduction, in my annotation?

01:21:30.410 --> 01:21:31.430
And I open that.

01:21:31.760 --> 01:21:33.319
And with that and I invite your

01:21:33.320 --> 01:21:34.939
questions. I welcome them and looking

01:21:34.940 --> 01:21:36.040
forward to your reactions.

01:21:36.050 --> 01:21:37.050
Thank you very much.

01:21:39.370 --> 01:21:40.830
Thank you Wendy, thank you so much for

01:21:41.360 --> 01:21:42.339
the talk.

01:21:42.340 --> 01:21:43.850
I'm going to present

01:21:44.690 --> 01:21:46.069
the next talk and then we'll take some

01:21:46.070 --> 01:21:48.289
questions at the end and please

01:21:48.290 --> 01:21:50.119
feel free to write them in the Q&amp;A

01:21:50.960 --> 01:21:51.949
box.

01:21:51.950 --> 01:21:53.779
So our next speaker is

01:21:54.050 --> 01:21:55.050
Jonathan Petropoulos,

01:21:56.420 --> 01:21:58.092
who is the John V.

01:21:58.317 --> 01:21:59.899
Croul Professor of European History at

01:21:59.900 --> 01:22:01.399
Claremont McKenna College in Southern

01:22:01.400 --> 01:22:02.400
California.

01:22:02.930 --> 01:22:05.290
Between 1998

01:22:05.300 --> 01:22:06.415
and 2000, Dr. Petropolis, Professor

01:22:07.560 --> 01:22:09.409
Petropolis served as research director

01:22:09.410 --> 01:22:10.999
for Art and Culture Property on the

01:22:11.000 --> 01:22:12.000
Presidential Commission on Holocaust

01:22:12.380 --> 01:22:13.990
Assets in the United States, where he

01:22:14.000 --> 01:22:15.431
helped draft the report Restitution and

01:22:16.040 --> 01:22:17.839
Plunder: the U.S. and Holocaust Victims'

01:22:17.840 --> 01:22:18.840
Assets.

01:22:19.280 --> 01:22:21.289
He also provided expert testimony

01:22:21.290 --> 01:22:22.526
to the Select Committee on cultural

01:22:22.710 --> 01:22:25.069
media-- Culture, Media and Sports

01:22:25.070 --> 01:22:26.659
in the UK House of Commons and tp the

01:22:26.660 --> 01:22:28.429
Banking and Finance Committee of the

01:22:28.430 --> 01:22:30.109
U.S. House of Representatives regarding

01:22:30.110 --> 01:22:31.489
these issues. He's the author of

01:22:31.490 --> 01:22:33.139
multiple books, which you can read about

01:22:33.140 --> 01:22:34.580
on his profile page on our website.

01:22:34.760 --> 01:22:36.760
Among them, most recently, Göring's

01:22:37.040 --> 01:22:38.853
Man in Paris: The Story of a Nazi Art

01:22:38.960 --> 01:22:40.520
Plunderer and his World,

01:22:40.970 --> 01:22:42.559
which was published by Yale University

01:22:42.560 --> 01:22:43.560
Press.

01:22:43.610 --> 01:22:45.200
And I'll let you speak.

01:22:46.640 --> 01:22:48.710
Thank you, Gil, for that very gracious

01:22:48.890 --> 01:22:50.720
introduction. And thanks to Stephen

01:22:50.960 --> 01:22:52.609
Naron and Timothy Snyder,

01:22:53.060 --> 01:22:54.775
Christy Bailey-Tomecek, and all the

01:22:55.220 --> 01:22:57.259
folks at the Fortunoff Video

01:22:57.770 --> 01:22:59.869
Testimony Archive, it's been, it

01:22:59.880 --> 01:23:01.790
was a wonderful semester on sabbatical.

01:23:01.940 --> 01:23:02.940
Such an honor.

01:23:03.560 --> 01:23:04.944
So I'm going to talk about Sydney

01:23:05.330 --> 01:23:06.319
Bruskin.

01:23:06.320 --> 01:23:07.349
And I would mention I

01:23:08.390 --> 01:23:09.909
selected him because of my next

01:23:10.790 --> 01:23:11.790
project.

01:23:12.080 --> 01:23:13.669
I'm writing a book along with Wendy

01:23:13.670 --> 01:23:15.416
Lower about 1945

01:23:16.130 --> 01:23:17.257
and war's end, and what

01:23:18.290 --> 01:23:20.810
happens to some of the Nazi leaders and,

01:23:21.040 --> 01:23:22.755
and Sydney Bruskin's story fit into

01:23:23.270 --> 01:23:24.379
that that project.

01:23:24.440 --> 01:23:26.629
I had considered doing a testimony

01:23:26.640 --> 01:23:28.430
involving Nazi looted art,

01:23:28.760 --> 01:23:29.760
it's been my specialty.

01:23:29.840 --> 01:23:31.604
But I found that Bruskin's testimony

01:23:32.210 --> 01:23:34.170
was very rich and took me in a direction

01:23:34.310 --> 01:23:35.310
I wanted to go.

01:23:35.540 --> 01:23:37.353
So, Bruskin is an interesting figure.

01:23:37.760 --> 01:23:39.800
He's a son of Jewish immigrants.

01:23:40.010 --> 01:23:42.049
He's a New Haven lad,

01:23:42.500 --> 01:23:44.539
a Yale graduate, a

01:23:44.540 --> 01:23:46.669
member of the counterintelligence

01:23:46.670 --> 01:23:48.630
corps that arrested French collaborators

01:23:49.430 --> 01:23:51.229
and Nazi war criminals.

01:23:51.500 --> 01:23:53.629
He was a liberator of a concentration

01:23:53.630 --> 01:23:56.239
camp and a person whose wartime

01:23:56.240 --> 01:23:58.279
experiences shaped his faith

01:23:58.280 --> 01:23:59.509
and his identity.

01:24:00.260 --> 01:24:01.880
Bruskin was interviewed in

01:24:02.630 --> 01:24:04.198
1994, which was rather early for

01:24:04.850 --> 01:24:06.290
interviewing veterans.

01:24:06.650 --> 01:24:08.479
Saving Private Ryan, the film, came

01:24:08.480 --> 01:24:09.754
along in 1998 and that led

01:24:10.520 --> 01:24:11.647
to a kind of collective

01:24:12.830 --> 01:24:14.659
project to interview the greatest

01:24:14.660 --> 01:24:15.660
generation.

01:24:15.980 --> 01:24:18.079
Congress passed a bill

01:24:18.440 --> 01:24:20.359
starting an interview, a federal project

01:24:20.360 --> 01:24:21.977
to interview veterans, and in the

01:24:22.430 --> 01:24:23.840
first 10 years they interviewed over

01:24:24.590 --> 01:24:26.158
65,000 but Bruskin's is earlier.

01:24:27.140 --> 01:24:28.904
And, but it does fit into that trope

01:24:29.300 --> 01:24:31.429
of, of, of veterans

01:24:31.430 --> 01:24:32.430
interviews.

01:24:32.660 --> 01:24:34.459
And I should also mention that I found

01:24:34.460 --> 01:24:36.619
in the Yale University archives

01:24:37.430 --> 01:24:39.390
an interview that Bruskin did nine years

01:24:39.680 --> 01:24:41.779
later in 2003 as part

01:24:41.780 --> 01:24:44.029
of a New Haven oral history project.

01:24:44.180 --> 01:24:45.769
And he was interviewed by a local high

01:24:45.770 --> 01:24:48.180
school student and reflected on his

01:24:48.200 --> 01:24:49.369
years growing up in New Haven.

01:24:49.370 --> 01:24:50.989
And that was useful for the first part

01:24:50.990 --> 01:24:52.270
of the annotation.

01:24:52.970 --> 01:24:55.159
So as I mentioned, Bruskin was the

01:24:55.160 --> 01:24:56.479
son of immigrants.

01:24:56.930 --> 01:24:59.006
His father and mother arrived in 1907

01:24:59.600 --> 01:25:01.699
and 1909, respectively, from

01:25:01.700 --> 01:25:03.799
Russia. And his father was

01:25:03.800 --> 01:25:05.960
a barber. His mother was a dressmaker.

01:25:06.170 --> 01:25:07.170
And, and so Bruskin

01:25:08.210 --> 01:25:10.789
is part of a broader immigration

01:25:10.790 --> 01:25:11.790
process or pattern.

01:25:11.990 --> 01:25:14.869
You know, between 1881 and 1914,

01:25:15.020 --> 01:25:16.196
over 1.5 million Russian

01:25:17.330 --> 01:25:19.489
Jews fled persecution in czarist

01:25:19.490 --> 01:25:20.490
Russia.

01:25:20.570 --> 01:25:21.709
As you probably know that there were

01:25:21.710 --> 01:25:23.410
pogroms in the early 1880s,

01:25:23.930 --> 01:25:25.819
and then between 1903 and 1906.

01:25:26.690 --> 01:25:28.405
And scholars say generally that the

01:25:28.760 --> 01:25:30.720
early pogroms didn't precipitate so much

01:25:30.920 --> 01:25:33.115
immigration, but the 1903 to 1906

01:25:33.530 --> 01:25:34.429
ones did.

01:25:34.430 --> 01:25:36.310
And there's one scholar, Yannay

01:25:36.560 --> 01:25:39.169
Spitzer, who wrote, "Jewish migration

01:25:39.170 --> 01:25:40.909
from the Russian Empire to the United

01:25:40.910 --> 01:25:43.699
States in the years 1881 to 1914

01:25:43.850 --> 01:25:45.418
was one of the most intense mass

01:25:45.800 --> 01:25:47.989
population movements in history.

01:25:48.260 --> 01:25:50.073
Over a single generation, more than a

01:25:50.300 --> 01:25:52.300
quarter of Jewish Russian, of

01:25:52.310 --> 01:25:53.927
the Jewish Russian population was

01:25:54.080 --> 01:25:55.838
resettled oversea, seas.

01:25:56.090 --> 01:25:57.640
The Russian empire was home to some 5.3

01:25:58.610 --> 01:26:00.440
million Jews in 1897,

01:26:00.680 --> 01:26:02.329
approximately half of the world's

01:26:02.330 --> 01:26:04.489
Jewry." So I think to see

01:26:04.490 --> 01:26:06.439
Bruskin as part of that process was for

01:26:06.440 --> 01:26:08.119
me, rather, rather interesting.

01:26:08.690 --> 01:26:10.664
Also the, let's see if

01:26:10.770 --> 01:26:11.770
we get this.

01:26:13.770 --> 01:26:14.799
The, the assimilation

01:26:16.050 --> 01:26:18.119
in New Haven and what it said about

01:26:18.120 --> 01:26:19.835
the, the local history of New Haven

01:26:20.250 --> 01:26:21.359
was to me very interesting.

01:26:21.360 --> 01:26:23.173
As many of you know, in New Haven, it

01:26:23.250 --> 01:26:25.529
was called Copper City because

01:26:25.530 --> 01:26:26.804
of the important trades in

01:26:27.600 --> 01:26:29.540
heavy machinery and manufacturing.

01:26:29.730 --> 01:26:31.479
And so you see these immigrants come in

01:26:31.480 --> 01:26:33.479
and it's part of the industrial

01:26:33.480 --> 01:26:34.379
revolution.

01:26:34.380 --> 01:26:36.291
And so that's the story of assimilation

01:26:36.570 --> 01:26:38.100
to me, which was quite interesting.

01:26:38.520 --> 01:26:40.382
Bruskin went to Hillhouse High School,

01:26:40.560 --> 01:26:42.329
still there, but it's changed its

01:26:42.330 --> 01:26:43.770
demographic markedly.

01:26:44.520 --> 01:26:46.039
And he, he was raised in a very

01:26:46.680 --> 01:26:47.758
secular Jewish family.

01:26:48.970 --> 01:26:50.249
And I'll talk about that a little bit

01:26:50.250 --> 01:26:52.170
more towards the end of my presentation.

01:26:52.950 --> 01:26:54.126
Very smart. Very clever.

01:26:54.450 --> 01:26:55.773
And he was admitted to Yale

01:26:56.460 --> 01:26:58.380
and he matriculated from

01:26:59.100 --> 01:27:00.528
1932 to 1936.

01:27:01.050 --> 01:27:02.820
And of course this was a period with

01:27:03.000 --> 01:27:04.000
numerus clausus.

01:27:04.770 --> 01:27:05.848
In 1935, Yale accepted

01:27:06.960 --> 01:27:08.999
76 applicants from a pool

01:27:09.000 --> 01:27:10.000
of 501.

01:27:10.590 --> 01:27:12.359
About 200 of those applicants were

01:27:12.360 --> 01:27:13.934
Jewish. Five got in, right,

01:27:15.120 --> 01:27:16.737
and there was a quote from a dean

01:27:17.220 --> 01:27:18.650
at the time, Dean Milton Winternitz,

01:27:19.230 --> 01:27:21.149
and he said, "Never admit more than five

01:27:21.150 --> 01:27:22.473
Jews. Take only two Italian

01:27:23.340 --> 01:27:24.712
Catholics and take no blacks

01:27:25.350 --> 01:27:27.319
at all." And this

01:27:27.360 --> 01:27:28.950
continued through the 1960s.

01:27:29.520 --> 01:27:30.520
This is quite well known.

01:27:30.960 --> 01:27:32.999
There was a limit, a quota of

01:27:33.000 --> 01:27:34.109
about 10 percent.

01:27:34.770 --> 01:27:36.810
Dan Oren, 1979

01:27:37.020 --> 01:27:38.909
Yale graduate, wrote a book that's

01:27:39.390 --> 01:27:41.640
been well recognized, Joining the Club.

01:27:41.940 --> 01:27:42.989
And so

01:27:43.950 --> 01:27:46.199
Sydney Bruskin embodied

01:27:46.200 --> 01:27:48.239
this, right. He, he actually lived at

01:27:48.240 --> 01:27:49.240
home.

01:27:50.790 --> 01:27:52.113
Jews and Christians at Yale

01:27:53.130 --> 01:27:54.420
were segregated until

01:27:55.200 --> 01:27:57.369
1948 and he didn't have enough money to

01:27:57.420 --> 01:27:58.379
live on campus.

01:27:58.380 --> 01:28:00.329
So he lived at home and he was socially

01:28:00.330 --> 01:28:02.100
isolated in this way.

01:28:02.340 --> 01:28:04.104
And it was very interesting with his

01:28:04.350 --> 01:28:05.249
experiences there.

01:28:05.250 --> 01:28:06.989
I love the story of the first time he

01:28:06.990 --> 01:28:08.189
had a ham sandwich.

01:28:08.370 --> 01:28:10.079
"I was teased into it because I wasn't

01:28:10.080 --> 01:28:11.789
supposed to, but I could see some of the

01:28:11.790 --> 01:28:13.739
guys were, some of the Jewish boys were

01:28:13.740 --> 01:28:14.818
eating ham sandwiches.

01:28:14.880 --> 01:28:16.568
So I said, 'all right, here it goes.'

01:28:17.040 --> 01:28:18.755
I took it. And I said, oh, boy, I'm

01:28:18.930 --> 01:28:20.596
probably going to drop dead, but I

01:28:20.640 --> 01:28:23.129
survived." But more important,

01:28:23.140 --> 01:28:25.349
with less levity, he noted,

01:28:25.350 --> 01:28:27.212
"we Jewish boys accepted the fact that

01:28:27.390 --> 01:28:28.739
there was a sort of, we were sort of

01:28:28.740 --> 01:28:30.989
outcasts, not exactly outcasts,

01:28:31.110 --> 01:28:33.209
but we were at the at the

01:28:33.210 --> 01:28:34.974
edge. We accepted that and we didn't

01:28:35.340 --> 01:28:36.479
do anything about it.

01:28:36.510 --> 01:28:37.770
We didn't protest.".

01:28:38.580 --> 01:28:40.649
So Bruskin at Yale,

01:28:40.650 --> 01:28:42.267
he mentored in, majored in French

01:28:42.960 --> 01:28:44.283
languages, French and other

01:28:45.180 --> 01:28:46.503
romance languages, Italian.

01:28:47.460 --> 01:28:48.479
He had some German.

01:28:48.480 --> 01:28:49.949
He learned some Yiddish later on.

01:28:50.190 --> 01:28:51.726
And I found this to be very interesting,

01:28:51.810 --> 01:28:52.949
interesting. It reminded me of what

01:28:52.950 --> 01:28:54.779
George Steiner said.

01:28:55.230 --> 01:28:56.609
George Steiner was born in

01:28:57.240 --> 01:28:59.339
1929 to Viennese parents who

01:28:59.340 --> 01:29:01.260
had left Austria and gone to Paris,

01:29:01.470 --> 01:29:03.899
and his father, who had raised

01:29:03.900 --> 01:29:05.729
the family, the children, multilingual,

01:29:05.730 --> 01:29:07.249
said that he believed Jews were

01:29:07.770 --> 01:29:10.109
endangered guests wherever they went,

01:29:10.260 --> 01:29:11.879
and equipped his children with

01:29:11.880 --> 01:29:13.739
languages. In the Steiner household, it

01:29:13.740 --> 01:29:15.119
was German, French and English.

01:29:15.330 --> 01:29:17.489
But it wasn't clear with Bruskin

01:29:17.490 --> 01:29:18.629
if this was one of the things he was

01:29:18.630 --> 01:29:19.829
thinking about, that this would be

01:29:19.830 --> 01:29:22.319
helpful. But he loved these languages

01:29:22.320 --> 01:29:24.479
and it proved important in

01:29:24.480 --> 01:29:25.649
his subsequent, in subsequent

01:29:25.650 --> 01:29:26.650
developments.

01:29:27.150 --> 01:29:29.459
When he graduated in 1936, he didn't

01:29:29.460 --> 01:29:30.930
have good job prospects in the

01:29:31.350 --> 01:29:33.065
Depression, but through complicated

01:29:33.660 --> 01:29:35.879
circumstances he stumbled into a

01:29:35.880 --> 01:29:36.880
bike business.

01:29:37.470 --> 01:29:38.879
Interesting. He could not even ride a

01:29:38.880 --> 01:29:40.649
bike. And I wondered about that, why he

01:29:40.650 --> 01:29:41.579
couldn't.

01:29:41.580 --> 01:29:43.540
But he was able to open a shop on Chapel

01:29:43.890 --> 01:29:44.890
Street and I

01:29:45.930 --> 01:29:48.109
went to look for the for the shop, his

01:29:48.120 --> 01:29:50.189
bicycle shop, and I couldn't find

01:29:50.190 --> 01:29:51.419
it. A little bit more research, I

01:29:51.420 --> 01:29:52.589
realized what had happened.

01:29:52.590 --> 01:29:54.652
It was torn down in 1961

01:29:55.080 --> 01:29:56.910
and it was replaced by the,

01:29:57.470 --> 01:29:58.739
the school, the Yale School of

01:29:58.740 --> 01:30:01.199
Architecture, that controversial

01:30:01.200 --> 01:30:03.390
building, the brutalist architecture.

01:30:04.110 --> 01:30:05.849
Architectural historian Elizabeth Mills

01:30:05.850 --> 01:30:07.610
of Brown had said "This

01:30:07.860 --> 01:30:10.109
building has probably caused more, fewer

01:30:10.110 --> 01:30:11.969
than any other American architectural

01:30:11.970 --> 01:30:14.279
work of the mid 20th century, a storm

01:30:14.280 --> 01:30:16.289
center from the start, praised as the

01:30:16.290 --> 01:30:18.103
prophet of a new architecture, damned

01:30:18.510 --> 01:30:20.939
as willful and egocentric, dogged

01:30:20.940 --> 01:30:23.399
by misadventure, victim of arson,

01:30:23.400 --> 01:30:25.439
student vandalism, remodeling

01:30:25.440 --> 01:30:26.440
and endless complaint.

01:30:26.790 --> 01:30:28.603
The A and A building has a bitter and

01:30:28.830 --> 01:30:29.970
embattled career.

01:30:30.060 --> 01:30:31.910
So that's what happened to Sydney

01:30:32.400 --> 01:30:33.400
Bruskin's bike shop.

01:30:34.080 --> 01:30:36.090
He had married and,

01:30:36.600 --> 01:30:38.939
and so he was not immediately

01:30:38.940 --> 01:30:39.809
drafted.

01:30:39.810 --> 01:30:41.819
And I talk a little bit about the, about

01:30:41.820 --> 01:30:43.589
conscription, the first peacetime

01:30:43.590 --> 01:30:45.479
conscription in American history in

01:30:45.840 --> 01:30:46.799
1940.

01:30:46.800 --> 01:30:48.319
He had a daughter and that also

01:30:49.020 --> 01:30:51.209
delayed his induction into the military.

01:30:51.480 --> 01:30:52.480
But by 1943 he

01:30:53.640 --> 01:30:56.189
was, he was drafted,

01:30:56.640 --> 01:30:58.355
went through basic training at Camp

01:30:58.830 --> 01:31:00.741
Shelby, Fort Shelby down in Mississippi

01:31:01.020 --> 01:31:02.441
and then at Fort Devens up in

01:31:02.670 --> 01:31:03.670
Massachusetts.

01:31:03.810 --> 01:31:05.672
And then he was shipped to England not

01:31:05.910 --> 01:31:07.499
knowing exactly what he was going to do.

01:31:08.070 --> 01:31:09.809
And here is Sydney Bruskin as a, as a

01:31:09.810 --> 01:31:12.149
private shortly after finishing.

01:31:12.680 --> 01:31:14.100
basic training and such.

01:31:14.480 --> 01:31:16.399
And in the U.K.,

01:31:16.880 --> 01:31:19.039
the counterintelligence corps was

01:31:19.040 --> 01:31:20.412
looking for individuals with

01:31:21.080 --> 01:31:22.080
language skills.

01:31:22.550 --> 01:31:23.922
There was a dire shortage of

01:31:24.620 --> 01:31:26.384
individuals who could speak European

01:31:26.630 --> 01:31:28.699
languages. And so Bruskin had his

01:31:28.700 --> 01:31:30.350
opportunity with his language skills.

01:31:30.620 --> 01:31:32.990
And so he became a member of the

01:31:33.110 --> 01:31:34.923
counterintelligence corps of the 80th

01:31:35.120 --> 01:31:36.120
division. And he

01:31:37.160 --> 01:31:39.529
started-- well, he arrived

01:31:39.530 --> 01:31:41.689
at, in France on

01:31:41.690 --> 01:31:43.723
D-Day plus 30 at Omaha Beach, right, so

01:31:44.540 --> 01:31:46.760
one month after D-Day in

01:31:46.940 --> 01:31:49.669
early July 19, 1944.

01:31:49.910 --> 01:31:52.129
And he was in his cohort,

01:31:52.130 --> 01:31:55.189
were given lists of Nazi

01:31:55.370 --> 01:31:57.330
officials and their job was to go search

01:31:57.590 --> 01:31:59.899
for them. And the lists were published

01:31:59.900 --> 01:32:01.669
after the war. And when I was writing

01:32:01.670 --> 01:32:03.460
Göring's Man in Paris about Bruno Lohse,

01:32:04.100 --> 01:32:05.089
this was part of the list.

01:32:05.090 --> 01:32:06.139
Just to give you a sense, they had a

01:32:06.140 --> 01:32:08.209
thick volume of high ranking

01:32:08.210 --> 01:32:09.280
Nazis, including the Ortsgruppenleiter,

01:32:10.250 --> 01:32:12.210
the local officials, and, and they would

01:32:12.380 --> 01:32:14.720
go pursue, pursue the Nazis.

01:32:14.900 --> 01:32:16.699
They started off actually with French

01:32:16.700 --> 01:32:17.700
collaborators.

01:32:18.350 --> 01:32:19.579
And that was kind of a training for

01:32:19.580 --> 01:32:22.070
Bruskin as he would learn to interrogate

01:32:22.460 --> 01:32:24.273
informants and to, to work with local

01:32:24.680 --> 01:32:27.109
resistance and to ply his trade.

01:32:27.290 --> 01:32:29.149
But he really became much more

01:32:29.150 --> 01:32:31.012
aggressive when they went into Germany

01:32:31.100 --> 01:32:32.899
and Austria later on.

01:32:32.900 --> 01:32:34.819
And, and probably the high point for

01:32:34.820 --> 01:32:36.409
Bruskin, certainly was a high point in

01:32:36.410 --> 01:32:38.209
terms of hunting for Nazis, was in

01:32:38.210 --> 01:32:39.210
Altaussee,

01:32:40.370 --> 01:32:41.410
a small town in the Salzkammergut.

01:32:42.950 --> 01:32:44.839
And I had spent quite a bit of time in

01:32:44.840 --> 01:32:45.979
Altaussee myself.

01:32:46.140 --> 01:32:47.512
It's right where the US zone

01:32:48.260 --> 01:32:50.059
is here. It's not too far from Salzburg,

01:32:50.060 --> 01:32:51.289
but it's in the middle of the Austrian

01:32:51.290 --> 01:32:52.700
Alps there. And Altaussee

01:32:53.300 --> 01:32:54.979
had been a repository for

01:32:55.490 --> 01:32:57.859
artworks, including Hitler's

01:32:57.860 --> 01:32:59.680
collection, over 4,500

01:32:59.870 --> 01:33:01.638
pictures in the Führermuseum

01:33:02.300 --> 01:33:04.039
were there, and many other looted works.

01:33:04.550 --> 01:33:06.589
There had been drama there at war's

01:33:06.590 --> 01:33:08.720
end as the local Gauleiter

01:33:08.810 --> 01:33:10.084
Eigruber had ordered bombs

01:33:10.910 --> 01:33:12.199
to be placed in the entrance to the

01:33:12.200 --> 01:33:14.329
mine, and it took an act of resistance,

01:33:14.330 --> 01:33:15.653
the local miners to, to, to

01:33:16.520 --> 01:33:18.439
pull them out and defuse the bombs.

01:33:18.440 --> 01:33:20.510
And here they are with some American

01:33:20.750 --> 01:33:22.579
soldiers who have come in, the first to

01:33:22.580 --> 01:33:23.580
arrive.

01:33:23.660 --> 01:33:25.159
And of course, there was a long process

01:33:25.160 --> 01:33:26.540
of evacuating the mines.

01:33:26.750 --> 01:33:28.429
In the Monuments Men film, they do it in

01:33:28.430 --> 01:33:30.259
one afternoon and in fact, it took them

01:33:30.260 --> 01:33:31.549
four months to do so.

01:33:32.030 --> 01:33:33.290
The roads were impassable.

01:33:33.920 --> 01:33:36.109
So in Altaussee though, besides looted

01:33:36.110 --> 01:33:38.149
artworks, one found an array of high

01:33:38.150 --> 01:33:40.430
ranking Nazis, from Ernst Kaltenbrunner,

01:33:40.580 --> 01:33:42.079
to Adolf Eichmann, to Otto

01:33:42.620 --> 01:33:43.620
Skorzeny,

01:33:44.640 --> 01:33:46.489
August Eigruber the Gauleiter was there.

01:33:46.490 --> 01:33:48.679
Hugo Jury, Franz Stangl

01:33:48.680 --> 01:33:49.680
was there, the Sobibor/Treblinka

01:33:50.780 --> 01:33:52.910
camp commander, Anton Burger, the head

01:33:52.960 --> 01:33:54.087
of-- you get the point.

01:33:54.260 --> 01:33:56.449
It was just filthy with Nazis.

01:33:56.450 --> 01:33:58.312
And so that's where, where Bruskin and

01:33:58.550 --> 01:34:00.740
his partner Robert Matteson arrived.

01:34:00.920 --> 01:34:02.869
And I should also mention, I had gone

01:34:02.870 --> 01:34:04.722
and found old Nazis in Altaussee too,

01:34:05.060 --> 01:34:06.481
in the 1990s and early 2000s.

01:34:07.160 --> 01:34:08.473
I had interviewed Wilhem Höttl

01:34:09.500 --> 01:34:10.429
and some others there.

01:34:10.430 --> 01:34:12.619
So it's still a place that

01:34:12.620 --> 01:34:14.449
has a brown past, if you will.

01:34:15.320 --> 01:34:17.419
So Bruskin, he played a

01:34:17.420 --> 01:34:18.449
key role in arresting

01:34:19.430 --> 01:34:21.619
Eigruber the Gauleiter and specifically

01:34:21.620 --> 01:34:22.729
Eigruber's wife.

01:34:23.150 --> 01:34:24.469
That was Bruskin alone.

01:34:25.070 --> 01:34:27.199
But, and this is, this is,

01:34:27.200 --> 01:34:29.390
this is Bruskin in his interview

01:34:29.840 --> 01:34:31.249
to the left, I know it is very blurry,

01:34:31.250 --> 01:34:32.818
it's a screenshot, but gives you

01:34:33.290 --> 01:34:35.600
a sense how they were just finding,

01:34:35.750 --> 01:34:37.318
uh, local facilities and turning

01:34:37.850 --> 01:34:39.510
them into prisons and arresting these,

01:34:39.590 --> 01:34:40.590
these people.

01:34:40.820 --> 01:34:41.990
The highlight of his

01:34:43.460 --> 01:34:45.379
activity and hunting for Nazis

01:34:45.530 --> 01:34:47.449
was arresting Ernst Kaltenbrunner,

01:34:48.020 --> 01:34:49.819
the head of the Reich Security Main

01:34:49.820 --> 01:34:51.045
Office, and Kaltenbrunner

01:34:51.890 --> 01:34:52.890
had a villa there.

01:34:53.420 --> 01:34:55.489
I mentioned earlier it was filled with

01:34:55.490 --> 01:34:57.529
treasure. Over sixty kilos

01:34:57.530 --> 01:34:58.910
of gold was found there.

01:34:58.940 --> 01:35:00.170
Most of it disappeared.

01:35:00.410 --> 01:35:02.362
But he took flight as the

01:35:02.696 --> 01:35:03.919
Americans arrived and he went into the

01:35:03.920 --> 01:35:05.890
deep wilderness there, up into

01:35:06.020 --> 01:35:08.479
the Alps. And, and reports

01:35:08.600 --> 01:35:10.789
came in that, that Kaltenbrunner

01:35:10.910 --> 01:35:12.674
was probably up there in the, in the

01:35:12.920 --> 01:35:13.789
hinterlands.

01:35:13.790 --> 01:35:15.280
And so Robert Matteson,

01:35:16.490 --> 01:35:18.229
Bruskin's partner took, partner took the

01:35:18.230 --> 01:35:19.651
lead. He was very gung ho, if

01:35:20.270 --> 01:35:22.399
you will, and he put on Austrian

01:35:22.400 --> 01:35:23.580
hunting gear, attire.

01:35:24.140 --> 01:35:26.479
He found some local Austrians who were,

01:35:26.720 --> 01:35:28.339
had been in the Wehrmacht, but were now

01:35:28.340 --> 01:35:30.109
prepared to help. And along with some

01:35:30.110 --> 01:35:32.419
American GIs, they hiked all night

01:35:32.420 --> 01:35:34.520
for seven hours over snow and ice

01:35:34.700 --> 01:35:36.219
and found Kaltenbrunner and his

01:35:36.350 --> 01:35:38.179
adjutant, Major Scheidler,

01:35:38.660 --> 01:35:40.099
in this, this hut there.

01:35:40.280 --> 01:35:42.409
And it was a very tense standoff,

01:35:42.410 --> 01:35:44.370
but they managed to arrest Kaltenbrunner

01:35:44.600 --> 01:35:46.220
without, without firing shots.

01:35:46.370 --> 01:35:48.149
He claimed to be a physician and to be

01:35:48.170 --> 01:35:49.170
someone else.

01:35:49.250 --> 01:35:50.896
Bruskin was back with Kaltenbrunner's

01:35:51.200 --> 01:35:53.720
mistress, an aristocrat

01:35:54.260 --> 01:35:55.926
who had just given birth to twins.

01:35:56.390 --> 01:35:58.520
Kaltenbrunner's twins, and,

01:35:58.790 --> 01:36:01.069
and Scheidler's wife

01:36:01.070 --> 01:36:01.969
was also there.

01:36:01.970 --> 01:36:03.439
So when they marched these two people

01:36:03.440 --> 01:36:05.679
back into Altaussee and Bruskin had

01:36:05.690 --> 01:36:07.249
the women there, he was keeping them

01:36:07.550 --> 01:36:08.569
under, under guard.

01:36:08.720 --> 01:36:10.339
They ran to their, to their

01:36:11.030 --> 01:36:12.745
romantic partners and they embraced

01:36:13.020 --> 01:36:14.189
them, so that was the form of

01:36:14.190 --> 01:36:16.199
identification with the, with the women

01:36:16.200 --> 01:36:17.630
showing who they actually were.

01:36:17.970 --> 01:36:20.279
And it was interesting how, how

01:36:20.280 --> 01:36:21.959
this was such a high point in Bruskin's

01:36:21.960 --> 01:36:23.626
career, for Matteson too, he wrote

01:36:24.000 --> 01:36:25.949
a book about arresting Kaltenbrunner and

01:36:25.950 --> 01:36:28.080
would lecture, and, and

01:36:28.530 --> 01:36:30.098
they, they, they kept souvenirs,

01:36:31.050 --> 01:36:32.249
if you will, trophies from their

01:36:32.250 --> 01:36:33.250
experience.

01:36:33.480 --> 01:36:35.048
Matteson kept the identity badge

01:36:35.550 --> 01:36:37.461
of Kaltenbrunner and Arthur Scheidler's

01:36:38.130 --> 01:36:40.090
went to Bruskin, which he still had, and

01:36:40.170 --> 01:36:41.939
he was holding up as a symbol.

01:36:42.090 --> 01:36:44.459
He also brought back Scheidler rifle,

01:36:44.640 --> 01:36:46.110
which he sold in the 1990s for

01:36:46.680 --> 01:36:47.639
four hundred dollars.

01:36:47.640 --> 01:36:49.170
And that was kind of interesting in its

01:36:49.470 --> 01:36:50.470
way.

01:36:50.490 --> 01:36:52.529
Also, besides arresting Kaltenbrunner

01:36:52.530 --> 01:36:54.029
and Eigruber and some of these others,

01:36:54.600 --> 01:36:55.927
Bruskin went to Ebensee.

01:36:56.670 --> 01:36:58.581
Right. And this is a photo that he took

01:36:58.680 --> 01:36:59.680
upon arrival.

01:36:59.820 --> 01:37:01.859
And Ebensee is a

01:37:01.860 --> 01:37:02.860
subcamp of Mauthausen.

01:37:03.300 --> 01:37:05.279
It, it really

01:37:05.970 --> 01:37:08.189
came into existence about

01:37:08.190 --> 01:37:09.270
halfway through the war,

01:37:10.230 --> 01:37:11.230
in 1943.

01:37:11.500 --> 01:37:13.949
It held about 28,000 male

01:37:13.950 --> 01:37:16.799
inmates and about 11,000

01:37:16.800 --> 01:37:18.270
of them had died in the camp.

01:37:18.540 --> 01:37:19.667
And Bruskin's testimony

01:37:20.550 --> 01:37:21.550
is interesting here, right.

01:37:21.694 --> 01:37:23.950
He says, "On our way, Bob Matteson

01:37:24.480 --> 01:37:26.023
picked me up in Kempten, Bavaria--"

01:37:26.340 --> 01:37:28.229
Actually Baden Württemberg, but, "and we

01:37:28.230 --> 01:37:30.403
were going on down to, to Altaussee.

01:37:30.690 --> 01:37:32.339
On the way, we had heard that there was

01:37:32.340 --> 01:37:34.104
a concentration camp, Ebensee, which

01:37:34.470 --> 01:37:36.087
was not far from Altaussee, so we

01:37:36.540 --> 01:37:38.080
drove there. Well, we got there,

01:37:38.550 --> 01:37:40.530
there were-- Bob wrote a book and I have

01:37:40.620 --> 01:37:42.188
at home, a sort of pamphlet-- he

01:37:42.660 --> 01:37:44.729
claims that we broke the lock to get

01:37:44.730 --> 01:37:46.347
in there. I say definitely, I say

01:37:46.740 --> 01:37:48.329
that we were among the first troops

01:37:48.330 --> 01:37:50.094
there. I remember going through this

01:37:50.100 --> 01:37:51.766
gate and there were a few soldiers

01:37:51.780 --> 01:37:53.819
there, very few." So

01:37:53.820 --> 01:37:55.499
whether Bob Matteson broke the gate to

01:37:55.500 --> 01:37:57.569
get into Ebensee or not, we're not quite

01:37:57.570 --> 01:37:58.570
sure.

01:37:58.710 --> 01:38:01.109
But, you know, this was the, the

01:38:01.110 --> 01:38:02.745
scene that Bruskin and Matteson

01:38:03.000 --> 01:38:04.000
encountered there as

01:38:05.010 --> 01:38:06.970
liberators. And there's some fascinating

01:38:07.140 --> 01:38:09.779
accounts of the liberation

01:38:09.780 --> 01:38:11.740
there as the Americans, well, subsequent

01:38:12.120 --> 01:38:14.399
to Bruskin and Mattsson

01:38:14.400 --> 01:38:16.115
arriving, the American army arrived

01:38:16.560 --> 01:38:18.180
in Panzers and they drove in.

01:38:18.360 --> 01:38:20.340
And the discussion was how

01:38:20.490 --> 01:38:22.979
the Americans were looking

01:38:22.980 --> 01:38:24.599
at the inmates as if they came from a

01:38:24.600 --> 01:38:26.429
different planet, that they were hardly

01:38:26.430 --> 01:38:28.019
recognizable. And same with inmates

01:38:28.020 --> 01:38:29.020
looking at the Americans.

01:38:29.220 --> 01:38:30.779
Some of the inmates tried to climb up on

01:38:30.780 --> 01:38:32.399
the Panzers, on the tanks, and the

01:38:32.400 --> 01:38:34.319
Americans gently but firmly

01:38:34.560 --> 01:38:36.149
pushed them down and said, no, we have

01:38:36.150 --> 01:38:37.150
to keep away.

01:38:37.680 --> 01:38:39.509
They brought up one or two prisoners and

01:38:39.510 --> 01:38:41.129
gave them Lucky Strikes and said, tell

01:38:41.130 --> 01:38:42.130
us what's going on here.

01:38:42.630 --> 01:38:44.609
A couple who could speak English and

01:38:44.610 --> 01:38:45.899
they were getting a sense of what

01:38:45.900 --> 01:38:46.900
happened.

01:38:46.950 --> 01:38:48.209
There was an interesting episode when

01:38:48.210 --> 01:38:49.925
they went, found the kitchen there,

01:38:50.220 --> 01:38:52.082
that the cook there wanted to give the

01:38:52.170 --> 01:38:54.359
American liberators some

01:38:54.360 --> 01:38:56.189
of, some food and served them the soup.

01:38:56.370 --> 01:38:57.839
And the Americans didn't, didn't want to

01:38:57.840 --> 01:38:59.219
do so. And they were told that it would

01:38:59.220 --> 01:39:01.229
be rude not to accept this

01:39:01.470 --> 01:39:02.479
act of generosity.

01:39:02.700 --> 01:39:04.559
So they were given bowls and they took a

01:39:04.560 --> 01:39:06.629
couple of soup spoons of the,

01:39:06.630 --> 01:39:08.249
of the broth. And of course, they said

01:39:08.250 --> 01:39:09.250
it tasted wretched.

01:39:09.390 --> 01:39:10.349
It was terrible.

01:39:10.350 --> 01:39:12.510
But this very interesting moment of, of

01:39:12.870 --> 01:39:14.489
encounter between the American

01:39:14.490 --> 01:39:16.050
liberators and the camp inmates,

01:39:16.890 --> 01:39:18.899
quite a few of them died from the

01:39:18.900 --> 01:39:20.819
refeeding syndrome that I think you all

01:39:20.820 --> 01:39:21.959
know about there.

01:39:21.960 --> 01:39:23.773
So there's an interesting moment with

01:39:24.060 --> 01:39:26.430
Bruskin as he, as he

01:39:27.180 --> 01:39:28.944
experiences this, this concentration

01:39:29.640 --> 01:39:30.539
camp.

01:39:30.540 --> 01:39:32.157
There was a crematorium there and

01:39:32.640 --> 01:39:34.829
above the crematorium was this sign.

01:39:34.830 --> 01:39:37.229
It was a little poem about the bodies

01:39:37.230 --> 01:39:38.969
wanting to be burned and returning to

01:39:38.970 --> 01:39:40.904
nature. And Bruskin

01:39:41.640 --> 01:39:43.404
ordered an inmate he said was a Kapo

01:39:43.770 --> 01:39:45.583
who was tall and well-fed to tear it,

01:39:45.840 --> 01:39:46.919
to bring down the sign.

01:39:46.920 --> 01:39:47.920
And, and he did.

01:39:48.090 --> 01:39:49.462
And Bruskin carried the sign

01:39:50.220 --> 01:39:52.680
all the way back to America.

01:39:52.830 --> 01:39:54.006
And he put this notation

01:39:54.840 --> 01:39:55.840
on his copy.

01:39:55.920 --> 01:39:57.719
He donated the originals to the U.S.

01:39:57.720 --> 01:39:59.369
Holocaust Memorial Museum.

01:39:59.790 --> 01:40:01.829
But this was another war trophy.

01:40:01.830 --> 01:40:03.899
Right? The crematorium sign that, that

01:40:03.900 --> 01:40:05.129
he, that he brought that back.

01:40:05.130 --> 01:40:06.449
And I thought that was interesting what

01:40:06.450 --> 01:40:08.079
he was carrying all over Europe for the

01:40:08.080 --> 01:40:09.929
next, next six months.

01:40:11.340 --> 01:40:13.590
Eventually, Bruskin went to Nuremberg

01:40:13.740 --> 01:40:15.329
and he was part of the team of

01:40:15.330 --> 01:40:16.680
interpreters that worked there.

01:40:17.310 --> 01:40:19.289
His German was not good enough for him

01:40:19.290 --> 01:40:21.089
to be an interpreter for Kaltenbrunner,

01:40:21.090 --> 01:40:23.050
for a major war criminal, but, criminal.

01:40:23.160 --> 01:40:24.630
But he was part of the broader

01:40:24.870 --> 01:40:26.520
interrogation process and then

01:40:26.970 --> 01:40:29.020
came home in late 1940,

01:40:29.790 --> 01:40:30.790
45,

01:40:31.890 --> 01:40:33.420
early on in the Nuremberg trials.

01:40:34.080 --> 01:40:35.080
This was another

01:40:36.780 --> 01:40:38.549
photograph that he made in a Jewish

01:40:38.550 --> 01:40:40.131
cemetery. And he captures himself

01:40:40.620 --> 01:40:42.659
there. And one thing that I found

01:40:42.660 --> 01:40:44.099
was really striking about this

01:40:44.100 --> 01:40:45.930
testimony, well,

01:40:46.890 --> 01:40:47.890
this quotation here.

01:40:48.930 --> 01:40:49.930
Right.

01:40:50.400 --> 01:40:52.360
"But it changed my whole outlook," going

01:40:52.500 --> 01:40:53.999
to Europe and hunting Nazis and

01:40:54.000 --> 01:40:56.159
liberating Ebensee, "on everything.

01:40:56.280 --> 01:40:57.779
I had never had much of a Jewish

01:40:57.780 --> 01:40:59.309
upbringing. We never belonged to a

01:40:59.310 --> 01:41:01.439
synagogue. I didn't have a bar mitzvah

01:41:01.530 --> 01:41:02.429
and so forth.

01:41:02.430 --> 01:41:03.720
But that changed completely.

01:41:03.840 --> 01:41:05.653
No, no. I went back to my roots after

01:41:05.760 --> 01:41:07.859
seeing particularly the, the

01:41:07.860 --> 01:41:10.220
camp and the women in Ludwigshafen

01:41:10.310 --> 01:41:11.650
and so forth." He had seen

01:41:13.070 --> 01:41:15.109
victims in Ludwigshafen on Lake

01:41:15.110 --> 01:41:16.110
Konstanz as well.

01:41:16.460 --> 01:41:18.199
"When I came back, I decided that I was

01:41:18.200 --> 01:41:19.989
going to become a Jew boy again.

01:41:20.240 --> 01:41:21.500
I didn't know how to start.

01:41:21.680 --> 01:41:23.539
And then I got a letter from the Lodge

01:41:23.540 --> 01:41:25.520
B'nai B'rith inviting me to a breakfast.

01:41:25.700 --> 01:41:27.379
And any group that approached me at that

01:41:27.380 --> 01:41:28.759
point, they were going to get me.

01:41:28.940 --> 01:41:30.851
I signed up right away because I figure

01:41:30.920 --> 01:41:33.079
this way I could identify myself as

01:41:33.080 --> 01:41:35.080
Jewish." And with, Sydney

01:41:35.270 --> 01:41:36.859
Bruskin and looking for

01:41:37.550 --> 01:41:39.169
what happened to him after the war.

01:41:39.380 --> 01:41:41.269
He shows up in the New Haven and in the

01:41:41.270 --> 01:41:43.000
Hamden Jewish communities, and he's,

01:41:43.370 --> 01:41:44.938
he's participated in dinners and

01:41:45.470 --> 01:41:47.659
other kind of community events

01:41:47.660 --> 01:41:49.819
and such. And, and Wendy

01:41:49.820 --> 01:41:51.430
and I, we went and found Sydney

01:41:51.710 --> 01:41:53.960
Bruskin's graves, gravestone,

01:41:54.350 --> 01:41:55.882
his last resting place in the Mishkan

01:41:56.300 --> 01:41:57.949
Israel cemetery there.

01:41:58.520 --> 01:42:00.199
So it was interesting to see

01:42:00.530 --> 01:42:02.569
someone who had focused so much

01:42:02.570 --> 01:42:04.369
early on in assimilation and being an

01:42:04.370 --> 01:42:05.370
American and

01:42:06.440 --> 01:42:08.302
going to Yale, and then the experience

01:42:08.660 --> 01:42:11.119
of the war, which shaped him, so,

01:42:11.120 --> 01:42:12.560
so, so profoundly.

01:42:12.770 --> 01:42:14.029
So. So, yes.

01:42:14.030 --> 01:42:16.069
So mine is still

01:42:16.070 --> 01:42:17.469
very much a work in progress.

01:42:18.020 --> 01:42:19.699
I also hope to find

01:42:20.270 --> 01:42:22.369
relatives of Sydney Bruskin and I

01:42:22.370 --> 01:42:24.319
was calling Bruskins around the country.

01:42:24.710 --> 01:42:26.479
We can see from this gravestone behind

01:42:26.480 --> 01:42:28.490
that he has children and grandchildren,

01:42:28.730 --> 01:42:30.249
but I have yet to to find them.

01:42:30.410 --> 01:42:32.539
So I'm going to continue to work on my

01:42:32.540 --> 01:42:33.961
testimony in the coming weeks

01:42:34.640 --> 01:42:35.640
and months.

01:42:35.800 --> 01:42:36.829
That will be done this summer.

01:42:37.550 --> 01:42:39.109
But hopefully I can find some family

01:42:39.110 --> 01:42:41.119
members and get that perspective as

01:42:41.120 --> 01:42:43.029
well. So thank you for your attention.

01:42:43.040 --> 01:42:44.040
I appreciate it.

01:42:47.060 --> 01:42:48.060
Thank you so much.

01:42:48.260 --> 01:42:49.849
Fascinating talking, fascinating

01:42:50.360 --> 01:42:51.829
to talk to you. I'm going to start

01:42:51.830 --> 01:42:53.419
actually reading out the first question

01:42:53.420 --> 01:42:55.430
and please, everyone,

01:42:55.490 --> 01:42:57.470
please write to us with your

01:42:57.680 --> 01:42:58.760
questions for

01:42:59.730 --> 01:43:01.256
Professor Lower, Professor Petropoulos.

01:43:01.850 --> 01:43:03.536
So, Lawrence Langer asks,

01:43:04.100 --> 01:43:05.179
he writes here, "for the sake of

01:43:05.180 --> 01:43:07.370
accuracy, memory of the senses

01:43:07.400 --> 01:43:09.199
is Charlotte Delbo's language, which I

01:43:09.200 --> 01:43:11.399
borrowed. My question to Wendy is,

01:43:11.600 --> 01:43:13.279
is anything lost from the impact of the

01:43:13.280 --> 01:43:15.259
personal ordeal of the survivor by such

01:43:15.260 --> 01:43:16.600
extensive contextualization?

01:43:16.820 --> 01:43:18.560
This is a question from a non historian,

01:43:18.830 --> 01:43:19.830
not a criticism."

01:43:22.490 --> 01:43:23.989
Well, I think, you know, I'd be curious

01:43:23.990 --> 01:43:26.119
to hear from others on this as well, on

01:43:26.120 --> 01:43:27.469
how to think about,

01:43:28.310 --> 01:43:30.409
you know, is there

01:43:30.410 --> 01:43:31.999
a balance to be

01:43:32.540 --> 01:43:34.310
achieved or aware of?

01:43:35.150 --> 01:43:36.669
It just, it just struck me as I

01:43:37.430 --> 01:43:39.350
was embarking on the critical edition

01:43:39.560 --> 01:43:41.059
and looking at the typical kinds of

01:43:41.060 --> 01:43:42.949
introductions. And I introduced a diary

01:43:42.950 --> 01:43:44.839
that I published years ago as well.

01:43:46.190 --> 01:43:47.562
The, as an historian, I have

01:43:48.290 --> 01:43:49.290
this compulsion.

01:43:49.640 --> 01:43:51.160
I have a

01:43:51.710 --> 01:43:53.659
routine of, as well, of

01:43:54.410 --> 01:43:56.749
trying to check the facts

01:43:56.750 --> 01:43:57.926
and fill out the history

01:43:58.760 --> 01:44:00.720
and, and tell the story of the Holocaust

01:44:01.040 --> 01:44:02.569
and Boryslav and tell the story of the

01:44:02.570 --> 01:44:03.679
Holocaust and Galicia.

01:44:04.460 --> 01:44:06.273
But that kind of, adding that kind of

01:44:06.380 --> 01:44:07.550
apparatus, that kind of

01:44:08.480 --> 01:44:09.769
either, however you want to put it,

01:44:09.770 --> 01:44:12.080
grounding or introduction,

01:44:12.440 --> 01:44:14.210
you know, at what point does that

01:44:14.480 --> 01:44:16.699
then diminish the power

01:44:16.700 --> 01:44:19.370
of the story of, of the survivor,

01:44:20.270 --> 01:44:22.220
as you put it, the impact of that?

01:44:22.550 --> 01:44:24.510
Because that's the subjectivity of that.

01:44:24.800 --> 01:44:26.479
And the, and those

01:44:27.080 --> 01:44:29.180
frozen moments of anguish, for instance,

01:44:29.990 --> 01:44:31.509
in and of themselves are so, so

01:44:31.640 --> 01:44:33.453
revealing. So I, that's, I don't have

01:44:33.830 --> 01:44:35.599
really a direct answer to your question,

01:44:35.600 --> 01:44:36.923
because as I concluded, you

01:44:37.670 --> 01:44:38.895
know, how do we deal with

01:44:40.010 --> 01:44:41.578
annotation and kind of traumatic

01:44:42.500 --> 01:44:44.166
autobiographies that contain these

01:44:44.390 --> 01:44:45.949
ruptures and unsettled moments?

01:44:45.950 --> 01:44:47.449
Do we fill in those gaps?

01:44:48.920 --> 01:44:50.640
Do we add layers of...do

01:44:51.530 --> 01:44:53.479
we add to the layers of personal memory,

01:44:53.480 --> 01:44:55.369
another layer of official history

01:44:56.510 --> 01:44:58.639
and how much is, is

01:44:59.990 --> 01:45:02.569
useful and

01:45:02.570 --> 01:45:04.760
illuminating and supportive of,

01:45:05.720 --> 01:45:07.640
of that testimony, of that survivors,

01:45:08.450 --> 01:45:10.199
not diminishing that survivors voice?

01:45:10.220 --> 01:45:12.033
So, I mean, I'd like to hear from Gil

01:45:12.080 --> 01:45:14.119
and some of the other participants on

01:45:14.120 --> 01:45:15.709
this issue if they've thought about it

01:45:15.710 --> 01:45:16.710
in any way.

01:45:18.970 --> 01:45:20.409
Yes, I'm going to actually tie this to

01:45:20.410 --> 01:45:22.539
maybe something--if people want to ask

01:45:22.540 --> 01:45:23.859
any additional questions here,

01:45:24.670 --> 01:45:26.679
but I wondered if you want

01:45:26.680 --> 01:45:28.248
to tie this a little bit about a

01:45:28.270 --> 01:45:30.219
question I received for Jonathan,

01:45:30.610 --> 01:45:31.779
also someone just wrote me.

01:45:31.780 --> 01:45:33.829
But if, if you could

01:45:34.120 --> 01:45:36.459
maybe discuss this and kind of discuss

01:45:36.670 --> 01:45:38.409
also your previous work on looted art

01:45:38.770 --> 01:45:40.450
in relation to this, and just see this

01:45:40.740 --> 01:45:42.819
question about how does this relate to

01:45:43.240 --> 01:45:44.439
Fortunoff testimonies?

01:45:44.440 --> 01:45:46.509
And you can see if

01:45:46.510 --> 01:45:48.189
any of the Fortunoff testimonies add to

01:45:48.190 --> 01:45:49.419
that in some way.

01:45:49.900 --> 01:45:50.900
It's one of the questions.

01:45:52.750 --> 01:45:53.750
So this is for me?

01:45:54.680 --> 01:45:56.409
Yeah, and I'll connect also to Wendys

01:45:56.410 --> 01:45:57.410
question in a minute.

01:45:58.150 --> 01:45:59.816
So, no, I'm glad that the question

01:46:00.160 --> 01:46:02.379
was posed because it goes back to

01:46:02.380 --> 01:46:03.850
the process by which I arrived

01:46:04.450 --> 01:46:05.892
at Sydney Bruskin and, you know

01:46:08.170 --> 01:46:10.569
I looked through the Fortunoff

01:46:10.570 --> 01:46:12.609
Archives for looted art and

01:46:12.610 --> 01:46:13.930
any references of art.

01:46:14.110 --> 01:46:16.240
And it was striking how

01:46:16.420 --> 01:46:18.670
art came up frequently

01:46:19.300 --> 01:46:20.949
as part of people's lives.

01:46:20.950 --> 01:46:22.959
And, and some of the individuals had

01:46:22.960 --> 01:46:24.729
been artists or had, they had family

01:46:24.730 --> 01:46:26.396
members who were artists, they had

01:46:26.770 --> 01:46:27.789
art collections.

01:46:27.970 --> 01:46:29.499
And, and

01:46:30.130 --> 01:46:31.306
what I, what I took away

01:46:32.200 --> 01:46:33.866
from reading those testimonies was

01:46:34.210 --> 01:46:36.609
something I had sensed before

01:46:36.720 --> 01:46:37.945
and, you know, read about

01:46:38.860 --> 01:46:40.479
before, but it was really palpable in

01:46:40.480 --> 01:46:41.803
the testimonies, is how art

01:46:42.520 --> 01:46:44.859
is different

01:46:44.860 --> 01:46:46.719
from most kinds of assets and that it's

01:46:46.720 --> 01:46:47.720
so personal, it's

01:46:48.850 --> 01:46:50.469
such a part of people's lives.

01:46:50.680 --> 01:46:52.869
And, and so, you know, people

01:46:52.870 --> 01:46:54.830
live with artworks in proximity that are

01:46:55.000 --> 01:46:56.470
passed down from generation to

01:46:56.500 --> 01:46:57.489
generation.

01:46:57.490 --> 01:46:58.989
And so I did see

01:46:59.590 --> 01:47:01.749
that come to life as people talked about

01:47:01.900 --> 01:47:03.939
family portraits that were missing and

01:47:03.940 --> 01:47:05.450
collections that had disappeared.

01:47:05.650 --> 01:47:08.229
And so to see that animated

01:47:08.230 --> 01:47:10.119
and such was striking,

01:47:10.900 --> 01:47:13.030
you know, with, with Sydney Bruskin,

01:47:13.990 --> 01:47:15.803
you know, the objects that he brought

01:47:16.030 --> 01:47:17.609
back and the way he treated them.

01:47:17.620 --> 01:47:18.879
And I think Wendy talked about these

01:47:18.880 --> 01:47:20.829
too, what, what Hauser kept, you know,

01:47:20.830 --> 01:47:22.689
they had a kind of a special power.

01:47:22.690 --> 01:47:23.690
They were talismans.

01:47:23.920 --> 01:47:25.350
And the fact that he had held onto Major

01:47:25.927 --> 01:47:27.691
Scheidler's identification badge for

01:47:28.330 --> 01:47:29.751
50 years was you know, it was

01:47:30.100 --> 01:47:31.359
interesting and, and I think

01:47:31.360 --> 01:47:33.249
representative, but, but it was almost--

01:47:34.090 --> 01:47:36.159
Bruskin sense a kind of evilness

01:47:36.160 --> 01:47:37.160
that was there.

01:47:37.530 --> 01:47:39.399
And this was almost something magical

01:47:39.400 --> 01:47:41.409
that he had that, wasn't going to

01:47:41.410 --> 01:47:43.659
protect him, but it had a certain aura

01:47:43.660 --> 01:47:45.099
to it and a quality and he was

01:47:45.100 --> 01:47:46.100
fascinated with it.

01:47:46.270 --> 01:47:48.489
So I think this notion about objects

01:47:48.490 --> 01:47:50.499
and auras is really, really

01:47:50.500 --> 01:47:52.309
interesting to me and connects both then

01:47:52.360 --> 01:47:54.180
Nazi memorabilia objects and

01:47:54.520 --> 01:47:56.439
the looted artworks that people lived

01:47:56.440 --> 01:47:57.440
with.

01:47:58.990 --> 01:48:00.819
And back to what

01:48:01.060 --> 01:48:03.219
Wendy said, and I'll maybe open it also

01:48:04.120 --> 01:48:05.499
if you have other questions, but also to

01:48:05.500 --> 01:48:06.909
Julie and Chad even, because I think

01:48:06.910 --> 01:48:08.589
it's a great question, I'm thinking

01:48:08.590 --> 01:48:10.640
about what does it mean to, is there at

01:48:10.730 --> 01:48:12.239
all, is there a risk there of over

01:48:12.250 --> 01:48:13.569
contextualizing, if there's too

01:48:13.570 --> 01:48:14.799
extensive contextualizing, what does

01:48:14.800 --> 01:48:17.079
that mean for the personal experience

01:48:17.320 --> 01:48:18.320
of the survivor?

01:48:18.880 --> 01:48:20.559
Right. Because we use the the, the, the

01:48:20.560 --> 01:48:22.177
contextualization Zisl to put the

01:48:22.450 --> 01:48:24.116
survivor in a different story than

01:48:24.580 --> 01:48:26.349
the one that he or she may be wanting to

01:48:26.350 --> 01:48:27.279
tell.

01:48:27.280 --> 01:48:28.499
So I'm wondering also if there's

01:48:28.510 --> 01:48:29.980
something that Chad and Julie,

01:48:30.760 --> 01:48:32.680
or Jonathan, you also want to address

01:48:33.490 --> 01:48:35.679
how that, that tension.

01:48:37.420 --> 01:48:39.169
Something that you might think about or

01:48:39.170 --> 01:48:40.909
deal with in the way you're approaching

01:48:40.910 --> 01:48:41.910
testimonies.

01:48:43.950 --> 01:48:45.659
I don't know how to answer this yet, but

01:48:45.660 --> 01:48:46.660
I think there's,

01:48:48.150 --> 01:48:50.069
there's one interesting book I read

01:48:50.070 --> 01:48:52.170
recently and reviewed

01:48:52.620 --> 01:48:54.779
that might offer some

01:48:54.780 --> 01:48:56.420
tips that Eva Noack-Mosse's

01:48:58.740 --> 01:49:00.779
recently published memoir

01:49:00.780 --> 01:49:01.880
of The Last Days of Theresienstadt

01:49:04.410 --> 01:49:06.569
in that it's, it's a memoir

01:49:06.570 --> 01:49:08.639
that she wrote, you might think of it as

01:49:08.640 --> 01:49:10.453
a diary that she wrote as, as she was

01:49:10.770 --> 01:49:11.770
in Theresienstadt.

01:49:12.240 --> 01:49:13.829
And then she went back and tried to

01:49:13.830 --> 01:49:15.030
mediate her own

01:49:15.840 --> 01:49:17.969
diary. And the press attempted

01:49:17.970 --> 01:49:20.059
to place all of her and

01:49:20.070 --> 01:49:22.319
her postwar editions, all of her

01:49:22.830 --> 01:49:24.545
additions to the book that were not

01:49:24.630 --> 01:49:25.529
contemporaneous.

01:49:25.530 --> 01:49:27.089
They tried to put all of those in

01:49:27.090 --> 01:49:28.090
italics.

01:49:29.400 --> 01:49:30.630
But if you go through it

01:49:31.620 --> 01:49:33.329
with a fine tooth comb, you realize

01:49:33.330 --> 01:49:35.540
there's a lot more mediation there than

01:49:35.580 --> 01:49:37.319
they were able to get in the italics.

01:49:37.320 --> 01:49:39.329
And it's kind of just...It's a

01:49:39.330 --> 01:49:40.439
fascinating book.

01:49:40.470 --> 01:49:41.489
It's almost like

01:49:42.720 --> 01:49:44.130
it presupposes

01:49:44.820 --> 01:49:47.189
these edited testimonies,

01:49:47.190 --> 01:49:49.589
right? Because it's, it's the survivor

01:49:49.590 --> 01:49:50.699
doing it themselves.

01:49:50.700 --> 01:49:51.959
It's the, it's the individual who

01:49:51.960 --> 01:49:54.359
experienced this, trying to mediate

01:49:54.360 --> 01:49:55.585
their own early testimony

01:49:56.790 --> 01:49:58.313
of those events and...

01:50:00.680 --> 01:50:02.210
It's difficult to pick apart,

01:50:03.080 --> 01:50:04.699
really difficult to pick apart what they

01:50:04.700 --> 01:50:06.039
knew at the time and what they didn't,

01:50:06.060 --> 01:50:07.060
but fascinating

01:50:08.570 --> 01:50:09.589
in the attempt at least.

01:50:12.040 --> 01:50:13.399
And you raise an interesting point about

01:50:13.400 --> 01:50:15.479
survivors themselves and, you know,

01:50:16.030 --> 01:50:17.030
the earlier

01:50:18.560 --> 01:50:20.719
approach in reading these and listening

01:50:20.720 --> 01:50:22.141
to these testimonies was more

01:50:22.520 --> 01:50:23.389
historical.

01:50:23.390 --> 01:50:25.008
Really, I think the Fortunoff, you know,

01:50:26.750 --> 01:50:28.171
made huge strides in shifting

01:50:28.970 --> 01:50:30.783
attention to the stories of suffering

01:50:31.100 --> 01:50:32.719
and of themselves and to study the

01:50:32.720 --> 01:50:34.729
trauma and to study, to listen to those

01:50:34.730 --> 01:50:36.249
voices in of themselves and the

01:50:36.710 --> 01:50:37.720
subjectivity of them.

01:50:38.480 --> 01:50:39.829
But then it's yes, you have the other

01:50:39.830 --> 01:50:41.720
phenomenon of the survivor who

01:50:41.900 --> 01:50:44.329
adds his or her own contextualization

01:50:44.330 --> 01:50:45.829
and pulls out history books while

01:50:45.830 --> 01:50:46.939
they're narrating their own

01:50:46.940 --> 01:50:48.919
autobiography and don't get it right all

01:50:48.920 --> 01:50:50.390
the time or kind of,

01:50:51.110 --> 01:50:53.029
you know, they don't tell us their own

01:50:53.030 --> 01:50:54.649
personal stories. They tell us kind of

01:50:54.650 --> 01:50:56.479
what is kind of common, common memory

01:50:56.480 --> 01:50:57.480
versus their own

01:50:58.790 --> 01:51:00.949
deeper memories. So it

01:51:00.950 --> 01:51:01.950
can happen both by

01:51:03.020 --> 01:51:04.729
scholars as well as by the survivors

01:51:04.730 --> 01:51:05.730
themselves.

01:51:07.570 --> 01:51:08.570
Yeah,

01:51:10.000 --> 01:51:11.519
I mean, when, when looking at a

01:51:11.770 --> 01:51:13.534
testimony, I'm reminded of something

01:51:13.960 --> 01:51:15.549
that one of my professors actually in

01:51:15.550 --> 01:51:16.628
England said to me and

01:51:17.590 --> 01:51:19.501
he said historians, you know, we engage

01:51:19.600 --> 01:51:21.999
in lumping and splitting and

01:51:22.000 --> 01:51:24.060
into with testimonies, I, you know,

01:51:24.070 --> 01:51:25.359
clearly we want something that's

01:51:25.360 --> 01:51:27.399
representative and we

01:51:27.400 --> 01:51:29.709
can, you know, gain insight

01:51:29.710 --> 01:51:31.610
into, into, you know,

01:51:32.530 --> 01:51:34.930
events that affected many more people.

01:51:35.110 --> 01:51:37.359
But we also want to do the splitting

01:51:37.360 --> 01:51:38.679
and preserve what is unique in the

01:51:38.680 --> 01:51:40.269
individual, right, and, and to have a

01:51:40.270 --> 01:51:42.189
voice and have an experience that is

01:51:42.190 --> 01:51:43.300
exceptional. So it's always that

01:51:43.480 --> 01:51:45.189
balancing act and,

01:51:45.580 --> 01:51:46.929
between representativeness and

01:51:46.930 --> 01:51:49.449
uniqueness in terms of the testimony.

01:51:50.230 --> 01:51:51.489
But just to add that in terms of

01:51:51.490 --> 01:51:52.820
everything else that was said that, that

01:51:52.900 --> 01:51:53.920
I very much agree with.

01:51:54.690 --> 01:51:55.819
You know, sometimes the over

01:51:55.820 --> 01:51:57.859
contextualization is done, if it's not a

01:51:57.860 --> 01:51:59.710
survivor testimony, this is,

01:52:00.080 --> 01:52:01.579
you're going to think this is kind of a

01:52:01.580 --> 01:52:02.509
strange comparison.

01:52:02.510 --> 01:52:03.510
But think

01:52:04.580 --> 01:52:06.770
about the fact that the

01:52:09.340 --> 01:52:11.289
writings of perpetrators

01:52:11.620 --> 01:52:14.169
are often heavily

01:52:14.170 --> 01:52:15.836
contextualized by scholars because

01:52:16.870 --> 01:52:18.536
we, you know, we want to, in a way

01:52:18.550 --> 01:52:20.509
diminish the impact and the

01:52:20.650 --> 01:52:22.210
resonance of that voice.

01:52:22.660 --> 01:52:24.729
So a kind of, there's a decision

01:52:24.730 --> 01:52:26.543
that's made and that kind of academic

01:52:27.250 --> 01:52:28.523
research decision empirical--

01:52:32.170 --> 01:52:33.170
She dropped.

01:52:33.830 --> 01:52:34.830
Oh, we lost her.

01:52:36.700 --> 01:52:37.700
Oh.

01:52:38.710 --> 01:52:40.630
Yeah, OK, well, she'll come in.

01:52:41.350 --> 01:52:42.579
Yeah, I was I was just thinking about

01:52:43.960 --> 01:52:45.320
the name, the name just

01:52:46.200 --> 01:52:47.580
escaped my mind, which I shouldn't.

01:52:47.860 --> 01:52:50.020
But the book by another

01:52:50.240 --> 01:52:52.200
professor here at Yale about...the image

01:52:52.510 --> 01:52:53.833
of the genocide survivor is

01:52:54.640 --> 01:52:56.109
the, Stephen is probably going to help

01:52:56.110 --> 01:52:57.159
me recover the name,

01:52:58.270 --> 01:52:59.799
the historical image of the survivor of

01:52:59.800 --> 01:53:01.720
genocide as kind of

01:53:02.870 --> 01:53:04.732
a general, where something represented

01:53:05.090 --> 01:53:06.969
the general victim in the postwar period

01:53:06.970 --> 01:53:07.999
looking for that, the

01:53:09.010 --> 01:53:09.999
name of the book that's going to come
back

01:53:10.000 --> 01:53:11.000
in a minute.

01:53:11.350 --> 01:53:13.419
But just generally how, right, that

01:53:13.420 --> 01:53:15.520
experience has, has become

01:53:15.770 --> 01:53:17.529
kind of a cultural experience, rather

01:53:17.530 --> 01:53:18.720
their unique and personal experience.

01:53:18.730 --> 01:53:20.592
I think this is something that touched

01:53:21.040 --> 01:53:22.600
what Wendy just said.

01:53:24.140 --> 01:53:25.855
I would, I would also note that the

01:53:26.330 --> 01:53:28.370
idea behind the Critical Edition

01:53:28.640 --> 01:53:31.039
series is not exclusively this,

01:53:32.180 --> 01:53:34.460
aimed at sort of deep

01:53:34.610 --> 01:53:35.840
contextualization.

01:53:35.850 --> 01:53:37.819
I mean, that's a part of the work, the

01:53:37.820 --> 01:53:38.820
annotation,

01:53:40.310 --> 01:53:42.800
but it's also about

01:53:43.490 --> 01:53:45.529
seeing a testimony through the eyes

01:53:45.530 --> 01:53:47.490
of the individual scholar who is working

01:53:47.600 --> 01:53:48.770
with it. Right. Sort of.

01:53:49.790 --> 01:53:51.559
And that's, that's an understanding

01:53:51.560 --> 01:53:53.689
that, that's your, this is your

01:53:53.690 --> 01:53:56.180
view of the value significance

01:53:56.630 --> 01:53:58.909
of this testimony for historiographical

01:53:58.910 --> 01:54:00.282
questions. It's not the only

01:54:01.130 --> 01:54:03.109
meaning this, this testimony has.

01:54:03.120 --> 01:54:05.119
It's through the eyes of you as

01:54:05.780 --> 01:54:07.310
the, as the scholar.

01:54:08.750 --> 01:54:10.939
And I would also note that, yeah,

01:54:11.720 --> 01:54:12.979
I think this is a really, this is

01:54:12.980 --> 01:54:14.509
something that we haven't discussed

01:54:14.510 --> 01:54:16.369
enough. It's, it's still sort of new

01:54:16.820 --> 01:54:17.820
for us.

01:54:18.350 --> 01:54:19.350
But

01:54:20.600 --> 01:54:22.970
in the end, the, the original document,

01:54:23.200 --> 01:54:25.159
the sort of frozen moments of anguish,

01:54:25.160 --> 01:54:26.484
et cetera, are in the audiovisual

01:54:27.020 --> 01:54:28.020
recording.

01:54:28.820 --> 01:54:31.040
And they're not, they're not,

01:54:31.400 --> 01:54:32.400
I think,

01:54:33.890 --> 01:54:36.109
made less powerful by this, this

01:54:36.110 --> 01:54:37.729
textual contextualization.

01:54:37.730 --> 01:54:38.730
Right.

01:54:38.810 --> 01:54:39.810
It's another layer.

01:54:39.890 --> 01:54:41.703
But I don't think it needs to be seen

01:54:41.960 --> 01:54:42.960
as a sort of

01:54:44.180 --> 01:54:46.609
a dangerous layer

01:54:46.610 --> 01:54:48.276
that could remove the power of the

01:54:48.620 --> 01:54:50.180
subjectivity of the testimony.

01:54:51.080 --> 01:54:52.080
That's my hope.

01:54:52.370 --> 01:54:54.289
Yeah. No, it's just something to be

01:54:54.290 --> 01:54:55.711
aware of, you know, something

01:54:56.510 --> 01:54:58.129
to kind of just, just think about,

01:54:58.880 --> 01:55:00.770
because there is this impulse

01:55:00.800 --> 01:55:01.800
academically to add

01:55:02.880 --> 01:55:04.497
the layers, add the knowledge, do

01:55:04.970 --> 01:55:06.199
the empirical work, especially on the

01:55:06.200 --> 01:55:08.160
part of an historian, and to really kind

01:55:08.390 --> 01:55:10.629
of, you know, present

01:55:10.630 --> 01:55:11.967
that in a way and kind of show that off

01:55:12.290 --> 01:55:13.900
as advances in knowledge.

01:55:13.910 --> 01:55:15.499
Right. But the layering, the layering,

01:55:15.500 --> 01:55:17.960
the layering, again,

01:55:18.500 --> 01:55:19.859
that potential exists there.

01:55:19.880 --> 01:55:20.880
That's all.

01:55:23.100 --> 01:55:25.119
And I'll also note that another

01:55:25.120 --> 01:55:26.469
goal of the contextualization, of

01:55:26.470 --> 01:55:27.999
course, is that the recognition of just

01:55:28.000 --> 01:55:30.340
how complicated these documents can be.

01:55:30.820 --> 01:55:32.880
Right. And so, you know,

01:55:33.550 --> 01:55:35.049
trying to learn about the history of the

01:55:35.050 --> 01:55:36.699
Holocaust exclusively through survivor

01:55:36.700 --> 01:55:38.439
testimony is very difficult,

01:55:39.280 --> 01:55:40.320
if not impossible project.

01:55:41.230 --> 01:55:43.659
So this contextualization is really,

01:55:44.140 --> 01:55:46.270
ideally there to, to,

01:55:46.300 --> 01:55:48.130
to help provide

01:55:49.480 --> 01:55:51.219
the kind of understanding that might be

01:55:51.220 --> 01:55:53.229
lost if, if, if there

01:55:53.260 --> 01:55:55.350
was not a scholar with you to help you

01:55:55.360 --> 01:55:57.430
guide you through this complex document,

01:55:58.720 --> 01:55:59.720
at least.

01:56:00.760 --> 01:56:02.010
That's one of the goals.

01:56:03.180 --> 01:56:04.680
I agree, I agree with you, Stephen.

01:56:04.750 --> 01:56:06.149
I think one of the things you get, that

01:56:06.150 --> 01:56:07.499
you get from reading the Critical

01:56:07.500 --> 01:56:09.689
Editions is, precisely looking at how

01:56:09.690 --> 01:56:11.760
a scholar uses testimonies and what is

01:56:12.230 --> 01:56:14.339
the whole richer world that she

01:56:14.340 --> 01:56:16.259
or he brings and the kind of view of the

01:56:16.260 --> 01:56:17.260
testimony.

01:56:17.610 --> 01:56:19.580
And it tries to bring it just from a

01:56:19.830 --> 01:56:21.006
story about the personal

01:56:21.960 --> 01:56:23.550
experience of someone into a story

01:56:24.000 --> 01:56:25.169
that is tied. So if you highlight a

01:56:25.170 --> 01:56:26.859
certain place, if you know the place or

01:56:26.950 --> 01:56:28.839
certain place was a place in which there

01:56:28.840 --> 01:56:30.779
was, kind of anti-Jewish violence in the

01:56:30.780 --> 01:56:32.544
years preceded that, or preceded the

01:56:32.640 --> 01:56:33.780
war, you could have get a whole different

01:56:33.960 --> 01:56:36.149
understanding of the relation

01:56:36.150 --> 01:56:37.435
between the,

01:56:38.900 --> 01:56:41.259
the testimony and

01:56:41.270 --> 01:56:42.399
the context of, which I think,

01:56:42.410 --> 01:56:43.699
definitely one of the things here is

01:56:43.700 --> 01:56:45.319
not, it's not so much a question of do

01:56:45.320 --> 01:56:46.850
we want, is there too much or too little

01:56:47.450 --> 01:56:49.850
context? It's about showing how

01:56:50.240 --> 01:56:52.260
often a context can, can offer one way

01:56:52.270 --> 01:56:53.359
to enrich our understanding of this

01:56:53.360 --> 01:56:54.360
testimony.

01:56:57.670 --> 01:56:59.729
Does anyone else have,

01:56:59.980 --> 01:57:02.409
you have any more questions from

01:57:03.520 --> 01:57:05.229
the audience for the participants?

01:57:06.440 --> 01:57:07.600
It was very

01:57:08.500 --> 01:57:09.500
fascinating.

01:57:10.670 --> 01:57:11.740
Your thoughts, very rich.

01:57:11.770 --> 01:57:13.839
I can understand that people have a lot

01:57:13.840 --> 01:57:15.639
to think about from four different

01:57:16.810 --> 01:57:18.117
contexts and perspectives

01:57:20.830 --> 01:57:21.830
here.

01:57:23.960 --> 01:57:25.940
Well, I want to say one thing I

01:57:26.150 --> 01:57:27.619
think is really interesting about the

01:57:27.620 --> 01:57:29.060
possibilities of the Critical Editions.

01:57:30.950 --> 01:57:32.567
In the testimonies that I'm often

01:57:33.140 --> 01:57:34.819
using in my research, I'm trying to put

01:57:34.820 --> 01:57:37.039
back together a cellular

01:57:37.040 --> 01:57:38.450
resistance organization.

01:57:38.540 --> 01:57:40.909
So by design, not everyone

01:57:40.910 --> 01:57:41.910
knew about everyone.

01:57:44.470 --> 01:57:46.899
And I learn almost as much

01:57:47.380 --> 01:57:48.999
from what they don't know as what they

01:57:49.000 --> 01:57:50.000
do know.

01:57:51.550 --> 01:57:52.628
And were there to be a

01:57:53.560 --> 01:57:55.324
great candidate for doing a Critical

01:57:55.750 --> 01:57:58.209
Edition of a resistance

01:57:58.210 --> 01:57:59.979
like that, you would learn so much if

01:57:59.980 --> 01:58:01.539
you were the reader going through,

01:58:01.540 --> 01:58:03.157
looking at, well, here's what the

01:58:03.520 --> 01:58:05.649
witness knows and here's

01:58:05.650 --> 01:58:07.316
what had to be filled in alongside

01:58:07.990 --> 01:58:09.754
that. And I'm just, because of where

01:58:10.000 --> 01:58:11.469
I'm coming from with my work, I'm really

01:58:11.470 --> 01:58:13.509
fascinated by the chance to look

01:58:13.510 --> 01:58:15.369
at those two things next to each other.

01:58:21.370 --> 01:58:22.513
No, I have, I have to second that Chad.

01:58:22.514 --> 01:58:23.889
I also think from the, what things that

01:58:23.890 --> 01:58:25.089
I work on, there's always a gap of

01:58:25.090 --> 01:58:26.349
knowledge by the person you're writing

01:58:26.350 --> 01:58:27.520
about, not only so much.

01:58:27.790 --> 01:58:28.790
Part of it is because during the

01:58:29.020 --> 01:58:30.669
Holocaust, I mean, knowledge was so

01:58:30.670 --> 01:58:32.439
sporadic and putting things together

01:58:33.160 --> 01:58:34.499
is a hard task.

01:58:35.140 --> 01:58:36.549
Some people's knowledge might be

01:58:36.550 --> 01:58:38.439
confined to the period in which he

01:58:38.440 --> 01:58:39.369
lives. He doesn't know what's going to

01:58:39.370 --> 01:58:41.140
happen next. And we have that foresight

01:58:41.410 --> 01:58:43.149
or that knowledge or just because of the

01:58:43.150 --> 01:58:44.522
geographical location and so

01:58:45.220 --> 01:58:47.260
forth. But I totally agree with that.

01:58:52.610 --> 01:58:54.117
And one thing you'll see too in terms of

01:58:54.830 --> 01:58:56.060
knowledge is

01:58:56.840 --> 01:58:58.921
the inconsistencies that are,

01:58:59.510 --> 01:59:00.510
that can be there.

01:59:00.900 --> 01:59:02.640
Sydney Bruskin was asked by the

01:59:02.690 --> 01:59:04.850
Fortunoff interviewers several times

01:59:05.360 --> 01:59:06.360
what he knew about

01:59:07.580 --> 01:59:09.162
camps prior to entering into Ebensee.

01:59:10.100 --> 01:59:12.140
And he completely contradicted himself

01:59:12.410 --> 01:59:14.410
in the process at times, that

01:59:14.600 --> 01:59:16.689
he had no idea about camps and he just

01:59:16.700 --> 01:59:18.513
sort of stumbled into them, this one.

01:59:18.590 --> 01:59:19.962
And I would, I had never seen or imagine

01:59:20.510 --> 01:59:21.510
anything like this.

01:59:21.600 --> 01:59:23.029
And in other times in the testimony he

01:59:23.030 --> 01:59:24.439
says, "yeah, we had, we heard

01:59:25.370 --> 01:59:27.049
from our other GIs that there were these

01:59:27.150 --> 01:59:29.049
camps and, just to realize

01:59:29.230 --> 01:59:30.455
that it's messy sometimes

01:59:31.640 --> 01:59:32.660
and that there is conflict.

01:59:33.890 --> 01:59:35.720
And we have to factor in that, as we

01:59:36.080 --> 01:59:37.080
understand.

01:59:45.160 --> 01:59:46.959
So I think on this note,

01:59:48.130 --> 01:59:49.880
I'm going to, to end this

01:59:50.260 --> 01:59:51.879
event and I want to thank again all the

01:59:51.880 --> 01:59:53.559
speakers for their wonderful thoughts

01:59:53.560 --> 01:59:55.449
and presentations and,

01:59:56.330 --> 01:59:58.419
and I look forward to reading some

01:59:58.420 --> 01:59:59.529
of the Critical Editions that are going

01:59:59.530 --> 02:00:00.772
to come out. I think we all look forward

02:00:00.773 --> 02:00:01.990
to reading the Critical Editions that

02:00:02.070 --> 02:00:03.981
are going to come out from this work in

02:00:04.180 --> 02:00:05.180
the coming months.

02:00:05.890 --> 02:00:07.605
And thank you, everyone, for coming

02:00:08.050 --> 02:00:09.141
and attending and listening to this

02:00:09.280 --> 02:00:10.280
event.

02:00:10.750 --> 02:00:11.889
Stephen, would you like to add anything

02:00:11.890 --> 02:00:12.890
or?

02:00:13.150 --> 02:00:14.680
No, thanks. Thanks to everyone.

02:00:14.770 --> 02:00:15.770
This is great.

02:00:16.090 --> 02:00:18.089
And thanks to our listeners for, for

02:00:18.150 --> 02:00:20.229
sticking with us to the very end.

02:00:22.960 --> 02:00:24.359
Thank you, Gil. Thank you, David.

02:00:24.580 --> 02:00:25.519
Thanks.

02:00:25.520 --> 02:00:26.520
Thank you,

02:00:26.890 --> 02:00:27.890
Chad.

02:00:28.450 --> 02:00:28.950
Yeah.

